+{"01-01-04": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer What is cancer?\nCancer happens when certain cells in the body grow out of control."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-01-04.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer What is cancer?"}, "01-01-05": {"chunks": ["What is cancer?\nNormally, our bodies have signals that tell cells when to stop growing, but cancer cells ignore these signals. They keep multiplying and can form lumps called tumors in different parts of the body or they replace the healthy cells in bone marrow and blood.", "These cells can directly spread into, or invade, surrounding tissues, spread to lymph nodes, or spread to other organs in the body, like the lung."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-01-05.jpg", "heading": "What is cancer?"}, "01-01-06": {"chunks": ["What is head and neck\ncancer?"], "photo": null, "heading": "What is head and neck"}, "01-01-07": {"chunks": ["What is head and neck cancer?\nMost head and neck cancer start in the lining of the mouth or throat, which is called the “mucosa.” Cancers that start in the saliva glands are also considered head and neck cancers."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-01-07.jpg", "heading": "What is head and neck cancer?"}, "01-01-08": {"chunks": ["Locations of Head and Neck Cancer\nThey are classiﬁed based on their location. Oral cavity (mouth) Pharynx (throat) Larynx (voice box) Paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity Salivary glands (not shown in the image)"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-01-08.jpg", "heading": "Locations of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-01-09": {"chunks": ["Other types of cancers located in the head and neck\nSome cancers that start in the head and neck and are not labeled as head and neck cancers. These include:\n• Sarcomas\n• Thyroid cancer\n• Melanoma\n• Non-melanoma skin cancers", "However, the primary focus will be on aerodigestive head and neck cancers, which arise from the lining of the upper digestive tract."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-01-09.jpg", "heading": "Other types of cancers located in the head and neck"}, "01-01-10": {"chunks": ["Types of Head and Neck\nCancer"], "photo": null, "heading": "Types of Head and Neck"}, "01-01-11": {"chunks": ["What is the most common type of head and neck cancer?\nThe most common type of head and neck cancer is mucosal squamous cell carcinoma. This cancer begins in squamous cells, which are tiny ﬂat cells that cover and protect your mouth, throat, and voicebox.", "It is important to know that squamous cells also cover many other parts of you body, like your skin and lungs. If cancer starts from these cells, this is not considered head and neck cancer."], "photo": null, "heading": "What is the most common type of head and neck cancer?"}, "01-01-12": {"chunks": ["What are the other types of head and neck cancer?\nSalivary gland carcinomas are the other major type of head and neck cancer. These can start in any of the 3 major pairs salivary glands, which make saliva and release it into the mouth.", "Salivary gland cancers can also start from minor salivary glands, which line your mouth, throat, and voicebox."], "photo": null, "heading": "What are the other types of head and neck cancer?"}, "01-01-13": {"chunks": ["There are 3 major salivary glands that release saliva into the mouth.\nParotid glands 1 Submandibular glands Sublingual glands"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-01-13.jpg", "heading": "There are 3 major salivary glands that release saliva into the mouth."}, "01-01-14": {"chunks": ["References:\nTo learn more about head and neck cancer and head and neck cancer types, please visit the following webpages, which are hosted by the National Cancer Institute and the American Cancer Society. https://www.cancer.gov/types/head-and-neck/head-neck-fact-sheet", "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/understanding-cancer/what-is-cancer.html"], "photo": null, "heading": "References:"}, "01-02-02": {"chunks": ["Head and Neck Cancer Risk Factors\nCommon Causes of Head and Neck Cancer Less Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer Title of Section"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-02.jpg", "heading": "Head and Neck Cancer Risk Factors"}, "01-02-03": {"chunks": ["Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer\nTobacco 1 Alcohol 2 Human Papillomavirus (HPV) 3"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-03.jpg", "heading": "Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-02-04": {"chunks": ["Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Chemical Carcinogens\nTobacco 1 There are many different forms of tobacco. Cigarettes, cigars, pipes, and smokeless tobacco are just a few Tobacco is the single most important risk factor for cancer. It is linked to 70% of head and neck cancer diagnosis."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-04.jpg", "heading": "Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Chemical Carcinogens"}, "01-02-05": {"chunks": ["Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Chemical Carcinogens\nAlcohol 2 There is no safe dose of alcohol. However, drinking alcohol heavily (more than 14 drinks per week) is linked to an increased risk of head and neck cancer, especially when patients smoke and drink heavily."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-05.jpg", "heading": "Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Chemical Carcinogens"}, "01-02-06": {"chunks": ["Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Human Papillomavirus (HPV)\nHPV is a common sexually transmitted disease. Approximately 80-90% of adults in the United States will have an HPV infection at some point in their lifetime."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-06.jpg", "heading": "Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Human Papillomavirus (HPV)"}, "01-02-07": {"chunks": ["Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Human Papillomavirus (HPV)\nThere are different types of HPV. Some types, such as HPV 6 and HPV 11, cause benign diseases like warts.", "Other types, like HPV 16, cause cancer. There are 14 different types of HPV which cause cancer. HPV 16 is the most common cause of HPV-related head and neck cancer in the United States."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-07.jpg", "heading": "Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer- Human Papillomavirus (HPV)"}, "01-02-08": {"chunks": ["Less Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer\nChronic Trauma 1 Chronic Inﬂammatory Disorders 2 Genetic Predisposition 3"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-08.jpg", "heading": "Less Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-02-09": {"chunks": ["Less Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer\nChronic Trauma 1 Repeated irritation or trauma from a sharp or broken tooth, dentures, or constant biting of lip/cheek can cause chronic inﬂammation, which can lead to cancer."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-09.jpg", "heading": "Less Common Causes of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-02-10": {"chunks": ["Less Common/Rare Causes of Head and Neck Cancer\nChronic Inﬂammatory Disorders 2 Oral lichen planus is a common oral inﬂammatory disorder which can lead to cancer. Oral lichen planus is a condition where the body’s own immune system attacks lining of the mouth, causing white patches, redness, or sores.", "Careful monitoring is recommended in those affected."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-10.jpg", "heading": "Less Common/Rare Causes of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-02-11": {"chunks": ["Less Common/Rare Causes of Head and Neck Cancer\nGenetic Predisposition 3 There are several genetic disorders which are linked to an increased risk for head and neck cancer. Some of the more common disorders include:\n• Li-Fraumeni Syndrome\n• Fanconi anemia\n• Dyskeratosis congenita"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-02-11.jpg", "heading": "Less Common/Rare Causes of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-02-12": {"chunks": ["Causes of Head and Neck Cancer\nIf you do not have any of the common risk factors, genetic work up may be helpful. You may be referred for genetics counseling, if a genetic predisposition is found. Speak to your doctor if you have any conditions discussed in this module.", "If a patient does not have any of the discussed conditions, their cancer may come from an unknown source."], "photo": null, "heading": "Causes of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-03-02": {"chunks": ["Title of Section\nBiopsies Cancer Staging and Prognosis Title of Section"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-02.jpg", "heading": "Title of Section"}, "01-03-03": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Biopsy\nA biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosing cancer. It involves removing a small sample of tissue, which is then examined under a microscope to determine whether cancer cells are present."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-03.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Biopsy"}, "01-03-04": {"chunks": ["Types of Biopsy Techniques\nDirect tumor biopsy Tissue is obtained directly from the primary tumor, often using cup forceps or punch biopsy. Needle biopsy of lymph nodes", "If a deeper tumor or a suspicious lymph node in the neck is detected, a ﬁne needle aspiration or core needle biopsy can be performed. These are often performed with an ultrasound to ensure accuracy."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-04.jpg", "heading": "Types of Biopsy Techniques"}, "01-03-05": {"chunks": ["Types of Biopsy Techniques\nBiopsies are important not only for conﬁrming the presence of cancer but also for identifying the type of cancer and testing for human papillomavirus (HPV), which can affect the treatment plan."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-05.jpg", "heading": "Types of Biopsy Techniques"}, "01-03-06": {"chunks": ["What happens after\ndiagnosis?"], "photo": null, "heading": "What happens after"}, "01-03-07": {"chunks": ["Cancer Staging\nOnce a diagnosis is made, your doctors will need to determine:\n• The size and location of the tumor\n• The extent of local tissue invasion\n• Whether lymph nodes are involved\n• Whether the cancer has spread (metastasized) to other sites This process is called staging."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-07.jpg", "heading": "Cancer Staging"}, "01-03-08": {"chunks": ["Cancer Staging\nThe following are some commonly used imaging tests. Each test has unique advantages over other tests.\n• Ultrasound\n• CT scan\n• MRI\n• PET-CT Your oncology team will choose the best test or tests for you."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-08.jpg", "heading": "Cancer Staging"}, "01-03-09": {"chunks": ["What does the cancer stage mean?\nStaging follows the TNM classiﬁcation system:\n• T (Tumor): How big is the tumor? Where is it?\n• N (Nodes): Has it spread to lymph nodes?\n• M (Metastasis): Has it spread to other parts of the body?"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-09.jpg", "heading": "What does the cancer stage mean?"}, "01-03-10": {"chunks": ["What does the cancer stage mean?\nBased on the TNM ﬁndings, the cancer is then assigned an overall stage:\n• Stage 1-2: Early stage - Usually smaller tumors, may not have spread.\n• Stage 3-4: Advanced stage - Usually larger, there may be nodal involvement or distant metastases."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-10.jpg", "heading": "What does the cancer stage mean?"}, "01-03-11": {"chunks": ["Staging is Important because it Guides Treatment Strategy\nEarly stages (1-2) may only require one type of treatment (e.g., surgery or radiation alone). Advanced stages (3-4) usually require more than one type of cancer treatment (e.g., surgery followed by radiation or radiation with chemotherapy).", "Refer to module 4 for more information about surgery and module 9 for radiation."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-11.jpg", "heading": "Staging is Important because it Guides Treatment Strategy"}, "01-03-12": {"chunks": ["Staging is Important because it Helps Predict Prognosis\nEarly-stage cancers tend to have higher remission rates. Advanced-stage cancers may have a less favorable outlook, but prognosis varies based on several factors. Ask your oncology team about your prognosis."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-03-12.jpg", "heading": "Staging is Important because it Helps Predict Prognosis"}, "01-04-04": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer What is cancer?\nCancer happens when certain cells in the body grow out of control."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-04-04.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer What is cancer?"}, "01-04-05": {"chunks": ["What is cancer?\nNormally, our bodies have signals that tell cells when to stop growing, but cancer cells ignore these signals. They keep multiplying and can form lumps called tumors in different parts of the body or they replace the healthy cells in bone marrow and blood.", "These cells can directly spread into, or invade, surrounding tissues, spread to lymph nodes, or spread to other organs in the body, like the lung."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-04-05.jpg", "heading": "What is cancer?"}, "01-04-06": {"chunks": ["What is head and neck\ncancer?"], "photo": null, "heading": "What is head and neck"}, "01-04-07": {"chunks": ["What is head and neck cancer?\nMost head and neck cancer start in the lining of the mouth or throat, which is called the “mucosa.” Cancers that start in the saliva glands are also considered head and neck cancers."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-04-07.jpg", "heading": "What is head and neck cancer?"}, "01-04-08": {"chunks": ["Locations of Head and Neck Cancer\nThey are classiﬁed based on their location. Oral cavity (mouth) Pharynx (throat) Larynx (voice box) Paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity 5 Salivary glands (not shown in the image)"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-04-08.jpg", "heading": "Locations of Head and Neck Cancer"}, "01-04-09": {"chunks": ["What is not head and neck cancer?\nSome cancers that start in the head and neck and are not labeled as head and neck cancers. These include:\n• Sarcomas\n• Thyroid cancer\n• Melanoma\n• Non-melanoma skin cancers Treatments for these cancers may be similar to those for head and neck cancer, but may also be different."], "photo": "/sms-images/01-04-09.jpg", "heading": "What is not head and neck cancer?"}, "01-04-10": {"chunks": ["Types of Head and Neck\nCancer"], "photo": null, "heading": "Types of Head and Neck"}, "01-04-11": {"chunks": ["What is the most common type of head and neck cancer?\nThe most common type of head and neck cancer is mucosal squamous cell carcinoma. This cancer begins in squamous cells, which are tiny ﬂat cells that cover and protect your mouth, throat, and voicebox.", "It is important to know that squamous cells also cover many other parts of you body, like your skin and lungs. If cancer starts from these cells, this is not considered head and neck cancer."], "photo": null, "heading": "What is the most common type of head and neck cancer?"}, "01-04-12": {"chunks": ["What are the other types of head and neck cancer?\nSalivary gland carcinomas are the other major type of head and neck cancer. These can start in any of the 3 major salivary glands, which make saliva and release it into the mouth.", "Salivary gland cancers can also start from minor salivary glands, which line your mouth, throat, and voicebox."], "photo": null, "heading": "What are the other types of head and neck cancer?"}, "01-04-13": {"chunks": ["What are the other types of head and neck cancer?\nThere are 3 major salivary glands that release saliva into the mouth. Parotid glands Submandibular glands Sublingual glands"], "photo": "/sms-images/01-04-13.jpg", "heading": "What are the other types of head and neck cancer?"}, "01-04-14": {"chunks": ["References:\nTo learn more about the biopsy and staging, please visit the following webpages, which are hosted by the National Cancer Institute and the American Cancer Society. https://www.cancer.gov/types/head-and-neck/head-neck-fact-sheet https://www.cancer.org/cancer/understanding-cancer/what-is-cancer.html"], "photo": null, "heading": "References:"}, "02-01-02": {"chunks": ["Curative Head and Neck Cancer Treatments: Overview\nMedication Followed by Surgery or Radiation Surgery Radiation"], "photo": null, "heading": "Curative Head and Neck Cancer Treatments: Overview"}, "02-01-03": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Treatments Aimed at Cure\nThe main goal of curative treatment is to eliminate the cancer and prevent it from coming back. Surgery and radiation are the two types of treatment that are known to cure head and neck cancers. Depending on the stage, location, and overall health of the patient, different combinations of treatments may be used."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-03.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Treatments Aimed at Cure"}, "02-01-04": {"chunks": ["Surgery\nSurgery is one of the main treatment approaches for head and neck cancers. It can treat cancers involving the mouth, throat, voice box, nose or sinonasal cavities, and saliva glands. It is rarely used to treat cancers of the throat behind the nose (nasopharynx).", "Surgery is often the preferred treatment option for cancers involving the mouth, or oral cavity, and the saliva glands."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-04.jpg", "heading": "Surgery"}, "02-01-05": {"chunks": ["Surgery\nDepending on the risk of recurrence, patients may need radiation and/or chemotherapy after surgery. The risk of recurrence depends on the features and behavior of the tumor. Oftentimes, these are only fully understood after the tumor is removed.", "We discuss this topic in depth in Module 9, which is titled “Postoperative Radiation and Chemotherapy.”"], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-05.jpg", "heading": "Surgery"}, "02-01-06": {"chunks": ["Radiation\nRadiation is the other major treatment for head and neck cancers.", "Like surgery, it can treat cancers involving the front of mouth, throat, voice box, nose or sinonasal cavities, and saliva glands. It is not commonly used as the ﬁrst step of treatment for cancers involving the front of mouth, or oral cavity, and saliva glands."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-06.jpg", "heading": "Radiation"}, "02-01-07": {"chunks": ["Radiation\nRadiation is often preferred for cancers involving the back of the throat behind the mouth, or oropharynx, the back of the throat behind the nose, or nasopharynx, and voice box, or larynx.", "It is also commonly used for cancers that are very large, difﬁcult to access, or where surgery may cause meaningful loss of function, such as a reduced ability to speak or swallow."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-07.jpg", "heading": "Radiation"}, "02-01-08": {"chunks": ["Chemotherapy\nChemotherapy is often added to increase the effectiveness of radiation. Adding chemotherapy is a complex decision that depends on many factors including:", "- The tumor size and extent of local tumor spread - Lymph node involvement, the size of the lymph nodes, and whether tumor has spilled out of the lymph node into the surrounding tissues - The overall health of the patient and the patient’s ability to handle the side effects of chemotherapy"], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-08.jpg", "heading": "Chemotherapy"}, "02-01-09": {"chunks": ["Chemotherapy\nYour cancer treatment team will discuss your case together and determine whether you would beneﬁt from treatment with chemotherapy. Chemotherapy is usually delivered weekly, or every three weeks, during radiation. It is important to know that chemotherapy alone does not cure head and neck cancer."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-09.jpg", "heading": "Chemotherapy"}, "02-01-10": {"chunks": ["Medication Followed by Surgery or Radiation\nIn some uncommon situations, systemic therapy is given before surgery or radiation. Systemic therapies include immunotherapy, chemotherapy, and targeted biologic therapy. This is called a “neoadjuvant” approach to treatment.", "Systemic therapy is also given after surgery or radiation. This is called “adjuvant” treatment."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-10.jpg", "heading": "Medication Followed by Surgery or Radiation"}, "02-01-11": {"chunks": ["Medication Followed by Surgery or Radiation\nGenerally speaking, the goal of this approach is to shrink the tumor before surgery or radiation. Shrinking the tumor may make it easier to completely remove or treat."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-11.jpg", "heading": "Medication Followed by Surgery or Radiation"}, "02-01-12": {"chunks": ["Medication Followed by Surgery or Radiation\nMost commonly, these medications are given intravenously, or into the blood through needles in the veins, every 1-3 weeks. The length of treatment with medications before surgery or radiation is commonly 6-12 weeks, but in some scenarios, may last many months."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-01-12.jpg", "heading": "Medication Followed by Surgery or Radiation"}, "02-02-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nIntroduction to palliative cancer treatment Forms of palliative cancer therapy Overview of supportive care Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "02-02-03": {"chunks": ["Introduction to palliative cancer\ntreatment"], "photo": null, "heading": "Introduction to palliative cancer"}, "02-02-04": {"chunks": ["Palliative Cancer Treatment: Why?\nSometimes head and neck cancer spreads too far or a patient’s health prevents doctors from offering aggressive cancer treatment. When head and neck cancer cannot be cured with surgery or radiation, doctors may recommend palliative cancer treatment."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-04.jpg", "heading": "Palliative Cancer Treatment: Why?"}, "02-02-05": {"chunks": ["Palliative Cancer Treatment: Why?\nWith palliative cancer treatment, doctors focus on controlling symptoms and improving quality of life. It is important to know that while we don’t expect palliative treatment to be curative, in some circumstances, it may prolong survival or provide long-term disease control."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-05.jpg", "heading": "Palliative Cancer Treatment: Why?"}, "02-02-06": {"chunks": ["Palliative Cancer Treatment: What is It?\nThere are several different types of palliative cancer treatment: - Medication, or palliative systemic therapy - Palliative radiation therapy - Palliative surgery - Best supportive care - Hospice"], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-06.jpg", "heading": "Palliative Cancer Treatment: What is It?"}, "02-02-08": {"chunks": ["Palliative Systemic Therapy\nPalliative systemic therapy is the most common type of palliative cancer treatment. It involves chemotherapy, targeted therapy, or immunotherapy.", "It is used when cancers are very advanced in the head and neck, when the cancer has spread to other organs, or when surgery or radiation are unlikely to be curative. The choice of drugs and intensity depends on the patient’s symptoms, tumor, and overall health."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-08.jpg", "heading": "Palliative Systemic Therapy"}, "02-02-09": {"chunks": ["Palliative Systemic Therapy\nTreatment with palliative systemic therapy traditionally means the cancer is incurable. However, newer treatments such as immunotherapy can sometimes make the cancer disappear completely, for years or more."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-09.jpg", "heading": "Palliative Systemic Therapy"}, "02-02-10": {"chunks": ["Palliative Radiation Therapy\nPalliative radiation aims to temporarily shrink the cancer. It also aims to reduce symptoms from the cancer, such as pain. There are a number of different ways palliative radiation can be delivered.", "It is often a short course of treatment; most commonly completed in 1-2 weeks of daily treatment."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-10.jpg", "heading": "Palliative Radiation Therapy"}, "02-02-11": {"chunks": ["Palliative Surgery\nPalliative surgery is a less common treatment option for head and neck cancers. It shares the same goals of other palliative treatment options: to reduce symptoms from the cancer and improve quality of life."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-11.jpg", "heading": "Palliative Surgery"}, "02-02-13": {"chunks": ["Best Supportive Care\nBest supportive care aims to prevent and address the symptoms and side effects of head and neck cancer and improve quality of life. Unlike palliative systemic therapy, radiation, and surgery, best supportive care does not directly treat the cancer.", "Supportive care can include pain management, mental health support, nutrition, respiratory support with oxygen, swallow and speech support. The palliative care team can help with symptoms."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-13.jpg", "heading": "Best Supportive Care"}, "02-02-14": {"chunks": ["Hospice\nHospice care also aims to treat the symptoms and side effects of head and neck cancer and improve quality of life. Usually, your doctors will only recommend hospice when you are expected to live for a short period. Hospice care can be given at home or in a facility."], "photo": "/sms-images/02-02-14.jpg", "heading": "Hospice"}, "03-01-02": {"chunks": ["Surgical oncologist\nA surgical oncologist is a surgeon who diagnoses and surgically treats your cancer. Surgical oncologists:", "• Take biopsies to help diagnose cancer\n• Discuss potential surgical treatments\n• Explain the beneﬁts and risks of potential surgical treatment\n• Explain to you how to prepare for surgery\n• Discuss with you what to expect after surgery\n• Oversee your recovery from surgery"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-02.jpg", "heading": "Surgical oncologist"}, "03-01-03": {"chunks": ["Medical oncologist\nAn oncologist is a cancer doctor that specializes in diagnosing and treating cancer with medications like chemotherapy, immunotherapy, targeted therapy, or hormone therapy. Medical oncologists:", "• Plan and discuss with you treatment options\n• Help you understand the risks and beneﬁts of each treatment option\n• Oversee your cancer treatment\n• Help manage any side effects from and after your treatment"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-03.jpg", "heading": "Medical oncologist"}, "03-01-04": {"chunks": ["Radiation oncologist\nRadiation oncologists are doctors that treat cancer with radiation therapy. Radiation oncologists:", "• Help decide if you would beneﬁt from radiation therapy and if so which kind\n• Explain to you your radiation therapy options and how it works\n• Design and discuss with you your treatment plan\n• Oversee treatment and help you to manage radiation therapy side effects"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-04.jpg", "heading": "Radiation oncologist"}, "03-01-05": {"chunks": ["Speech and language pathologist (SLP)\nSpeech and language pathologists help with cancer and treatment effects on your voice, swallowing, and communication skills. Speech and language pathologists will meet with you before, during, and after treatment to:", "• Evaluate and treat swallowing difﬁculties\n• Support communication after surgeries or treatment affecting the mouth and throat\n• Teach you safe eating and drinking strategies\n• Create a personalized plan to meet your communication and swallowing goals"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-05.jpg", "heading": "Speech and language pathologist (SLP)"}, "03-01-06": {"chunks": ["Dentist\nDentists are doctors who specialize in treatment of the teeth, gums, and mouth. Dentists:", "• Detect early signs of potential oral cancer\n• Manage any oral complications that come up during and after cancer treatment\n• If necessary, can help correct any anatomical changes or changes in eating and speaking with prosthetic dentistry"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-06.jpg", "heading": "Dentist"}, "03-01-07": {"chunks": ["Oral maxillofacial surgeon\nOral and maxillofacial surgeons are specialists in treating conditions that affect the mouth, jaw, and face. They work with your medical and dental teams to protect your ability to speak, chew, and maintain oral health. Oral and maxillofacial surgeons:", "• Treat complications affecting the mouth, teeth, or jaw\n• Place dental implants or preparing for dentures after surgery\n• Support cosmetic and functional recovery to improve quality of life."], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-07.jpg", "heading": "Oral maxillofacial surgeon"}, "03-01-08": {"chunks": ["Dietician\nA dietician is a healthcare professional that specializes in food and nutrition. Ask your provider to place a referral if you would like to be contacted. Dieticians help:", "• Improve and maintain your nutritional status before, during, and after your cancer treatment\n• Manage changes in your weight or appetite\n• Help you develop an eating plan that meets your needs\n• Answer your questions on diet and nutrition throughout your treatment and recovery"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-08.jpg", "heading": "Dietician"}, "03-01-09": {"chunks": ["Social worker\nA social worker helps you and your family cope with and manage practical and emotional challenges that come with cancer and its treatment. Social workers:", "• Connect you with support groups and community services\n• Help you navigate and ﬁnd resources for transportation, housing and ﬁnancial assistance\n• Assist with paperwork and insurance concerns\n• Advocate for you and your family's’ needs"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-09.jpg", "heading": "Social worker"}, "03-01-10": {"chunks": ["Counselor or Therapist\nA counselor or therapist supports your emotional and mental wellbeing and help you address emotional, psychological, and social challenges. This may be your psycho-oncologist. Counselors or therapists:", "• Provide a safe space to talk\n• Help you manage stress, anxiety, and depression\n• Support you mentally and emotionally through your treatment and beyond."], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-10.jpg", "heading": "Counselor or Therapist"}, "03-01-11": {"chunks": ["Chaplain\nA chaplain supports you and your loved ones’ spiritual and emotional well being--regardless of religious background or beliefs. Chaplains help:", "• Provide emotional support during difﬁcult moments\n• Explore questions about meaning, purpose, or hope\n• Support your spiritual or religious practices through treatment and treatment decisions\n• Connect you with religious or spiritual resources in your community"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-11.jpg", "heading": "Chaplain"}, "03-01-12": {"chunks": ["Occupational therapist\nAn occupational therapist helps you stay as independent, active, and safe as possible throughout your treatment. Occupational therapists:", "• Offer strategies to manage physical changes (such as weakness, limited movement and fatigue)\n• Help to adapt your home environment for comfort and safety\n• Recommend tools or equipment to make everyday tasks easier and safer\n• Support your return to work, school, or hobbies"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-12.jpg", "heading": "Occupational therapist"}, "03-01-13": {"chunks": ["Physical therapist\nA physical therapist helps you regain strength, balance, and mobility during and after cancer treatment. Physical therapists:", "• Reduce pain or stiffness during and after treatment\n• Help with walking or movement challenges\n• Teach exercises to improve strength, mobility, and coordination\n• Teach safe ways to stay active and return to daily routines"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-13.jpg", "heading": "Physical therapist"}, "03-01-14": {"chunks": ["Physical medicine and rehabilitation specialist\nPhysical medicine and rehabilitation specialists are doctors that manage your medical issues throughout the treatment and the rehabilitation process. They work with physical therapists, occupational therapists, and other specialists to optimize recovery.", "Physical medicine and rehabilitation specialists:\n• Develop a rehabilitation plan to improve movement and function\n• Manage and limit pain or discomfort from cancer treatment or surgery\n• Recommend equipment to help mobility challenges"], "photo": "/sms-images/03-01-14.jpg", "heading": "Physical medicine and rehabilitation specialist"}, "04-01-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nStoma/ Lary Tube Care Nutrition Other Considerations Procedure Options Safety Concerns Speech Nutrition Humidiﬁcation Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "04-01-04": {"chunks": ["What does the voice box do?\n• Helps you talk\n• Closes off your lungs when you swallow\n• Helps guide food to your stomach ○ After the voice box is removed, you can still swallow by mouth once your stitches heal (usually in 7–10 days)"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-04.jpg", "heading": "What does the voice box do?"}, "04-01-05": {"chunks": ["Treatment options\n- Surgery - Radiation and/or chemotherapy - Post-op considerations specific to larynx cancer"], "photo": null, "heading": "Treatment options"}, "04-01-06": {"chunks": ["What are my surgical options?\nTransoral Laser Total Laryngectomy Partial Laryngectomy Microsurgery (TLM)"], "photo": null, "heading": "What are my surgical options?"}, "04-01-07": {"chunks": ["What is the difference between a partial and total laryngectomy?\nA partial laryngectomy is surgery to remove part of your voice box (larynx). This may be done to remove cancer while trying to save your ability to speak and swallow.", "A total laryngectomy means the entire voice box (vocal cords) is removed. This is done when cancer involves most or all of the larynx."], "photo": null, "heading": "What is the difference between a partial and total laryngectomy?"}, "04-01-08": {"chunks": ["What changes after a total laryngectomy?\nIn a partial laryngectomy, a temporary stoma may be used to help with breathing while you heal.", "In a total laryngectomy, a permanent stoma is created because the entire voice box is removed, and this becomes your only way to breathe. Moreover, a prosthesis is needed to speak."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-08.jpg", "heading": "What changes after a total laryngectomy?"}, "04-01-09": {"chunks": ["What is transoral laser microsurgery?\n• It is a minimally invasive surgery used to remove tumors from the throat or voice box through the mouth (no open cuts on the neck).\n• A microscope is used for precise vision\n• A laser is used to cut and remove the tumor\n• Usually done under general anesthesia"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-09.jpg", "heading": "What is transoral laser microsurgery?"}, "04-01-10": {"chunks": ["When is TLM used?\nTLM is used most often to treat:\n• Early-stage laryngeal cancer (especially on the vocal cords – glottic cancer)\n• Supraglottic cancer (above the vocal cords)\n• Benign vocal cord growths (like polyps or papillomas)\n• Sometimes used for oropharyngeal cancers (like base of tongue or tonsils)"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-10.jpg", "heading": "When is TLM used?"}, "04-01-12": {"chunks": ["Speaking After Surgery\nThere are a few permanent changes after a total laryngectomy surgery.\n• You will learn to speak a new way.\n• By 3 weeks post-surgery, you may begin using your artiﬁcial prosthesis placed at the time of surgery.\n• This allows you a more natural type of speech."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-12.jpg", "heading": "Speaking After Surgery"}, "04-01-13": {"chunks": ["Prosthesis Considerations and Management\nOccasionally, patients prefer not to have a prosthesis placed or the surgeon does not consider it safe to place the prosthesis at the time of our initial surgery.", "This can always be done at a later date with some risk. If you have this prosthesis, be sure the passage remains open by keeping a catheter or special tube in the opening at all times should the prosthesis become displaced."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-13.jpg", "heading": "Prosthesis Considerations and Management"}, "04-01-15": {"chunks": ["Post-Laryngectomy Hospital Stay\nYou will be in the hospital approximately 7-10 days. A soft plastic tube called a Lary tube will be placed in your stoma. This device helps to keep the surgical site open and permits the use of a HME (heat and moisture exchanger) ﬁlter."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-15.jpg", "heading": "Post-Laryngectomy Hospital Stay"}, "04-01-16": {"chunks": ["Stoma Care\nIt is very important that you learn to care for your stoma as part of your daily hygiene. When released from the hospital you should begin caring for yourself. Your social worker will arrange for the supplies you will need to be delivered to your home Make sure to:", "• Replace HME if it becomes clogged with mucus.\n• Sleep with your head elevated to prevent swelling.\n• Wash your hands each time you touch your stoma"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-16.jpg", "heading": "Stoma Care"}, "04-01-17": {"chunks": ["Daily Stoma Care\nThe following should be performed at least twice a day:\n• Remove soft plastic tube for cleaning so you can see only the stoma\n• Look in the mirror and spray salt water (see recipe later) into your stoma\n• Cough into a tissue held over your stoma\n• Clean your stoma with q-tips, 4x4's and/or a washcloth\n• Use a mixture of 1/2 hydrogen peroxide and 1/2 water to cleanse stitches and/or staples twice a day until they are removed.\n• Place a thin coat of Vaseline or Aquaphor around the stoma and neck to protect and moisturize."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-17.jpg", "heading": "Daily Stoma Care"}, "04-01-19": {"chunks": ["Tips to Maintain Humidity\n• Leave your HME ﬁlter in place at all times except when you are cleaning your stoma or if you need to cough.\n• Get in the shower and breathe water droplets. Some people are more comfortable wearing a baby bib with the plastic side facing out or a shower collar that can be special ordered.", "• Use a bedside humidiﬁer.\n• These are very important steps in preventing mucous from plugging your only breathing passage."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-19.jpg", "heading": "Tips to Maintain Humidity"}, "04-01-20": {"chunks": ["Humidity Spray\nExtra moisture is very important. Spray salt water directly into the stoma at least 4 times a day::\n• (1/4 tsp salt in 1 cup tap water, changed daily)\n• 8am, 12pm, 4pm, 8pm"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-20.jpg", "heading": "Humidity Spray"}, "04-01-22": {"chunks": ["Eating and Drinking After Surgery\nIn order for the surgery site to heal you will not be able to eat or drink by mouth until you are evaluated by your doctor or speech therapist. When it is safe for you to eat by mouth, you may notice a little tightness when you swallow. Don't panic!"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-22.jpg", "heading": "Eating and Drinking After Surgery"}, "04-01-23": {"chunks": ["Post-Surgery Nutrition\n• After surgery, you may experience difﬁculty swallowing due to scarring, swelling, or the effects of radiation.\n• To make eating easier, try grinding meats, using gravy or sauces to moisten foods, and experimenting with different textures.\n• It’s also common for taste to change after surgery. Using more spices and ﬂavorings can help make food more enjoyable."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-23.jpg", "heading": "Post-Surgery Nutrition"}, "04-01-24": {"chunks": ["Post-Surgery Comfort\n• Be aware that certain foods, like acidic items or carbonated drinks, may cause bloating or belching. If this happens, over-the-counter remedies like Tums may provide relief.\n• If swallowing becomes especially difﬁcult, contact your nurse or speech therapist to discuss treatment options."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-24.jpg", "heading": "Post-Surgery Comfort"}, "04-01-26": {"chunks": ["Smelling and Breathing Post Surgery\nYour sense of smell is usually affected after this surgery.\n• Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors are recommended. Your only breathing passage is through your neck.", "• Most laryngectomy patients feel more comfortable wearing an emergency bracelet or having information in their wallet to speak for them in case of emergency.\n• This will also inform emergency personnel that artiﬁcial oxygen would have to be delivered through the neck.\n• If you fall into a large body of water you will drown; therefore, water sports are off limits."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-26.jpg", "heading": "Smelling and Breathing Post Surgery"}, "04-01-28": {"chunks": ["Runny Nose Post-Surgery\nAfter a laryngectomy your body is attempting to adapt to your new way of breathing, this may cause a runny nose. You can use over the counter Claritin to help with the drainage."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-01-28.jpg", "heading": "Runny Nose Post-Surgery"}, "04-02-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nProcedure Options Mouth Cancer Background Testing for Mouth Cancer Other Considerations Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "04-02-04": {"chunks": ["What is the oral cavity?\n• The mouth, or oral cavity, is made up of the lips, cheeks, palate, teeth, and tongue.\n• Oral cavity cancer affects the lips, front 2/3rds of the tongue, the gums, the lining inside the cheeks/lips, the ﬂoor of the mouth, the bony top of the mouth, and the bony ridges that hold the teeth."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-02-04.jpg", "heading": "What is the oral cavity?"}, "04-02-06": {"chunks": ["How does testing work?\n• Prior to treatment planning, testing is needed.\n• Results from imaging and biopsies are relied on to develop individual treatments.\n• A comprehensive medical history, physical exam (speciﬁcally head and neck exam), and scans (CT, PET, Endoscopy, etc.) are utilized."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-02-06.jpg", "heading": "How does testing work?"}, "04-02-07": {"chunks": ["What are my procedure options?\nGlossectomy Maxillectomy Oral Composite Resection Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "What are my procedure options?"}, "04-02-08": {"chunks": ["What is a glossectomy?\n• Surgical removal of part or all of the tongue.", "○ Partial: resection of a portion of the tongue ○ Hemi: removal of one side of the tongue ○ Total: complete tongue removed\n• Surgeons aim to preserve as much of the tongue as possible during the procedure.\n• Afterwards, speech and swallowing therapy is commonly needed during recovery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-02-08.jpg", "heading": "What is a glossectomy?"}, "04-02-09": {"chunks": ["What is a maxillectomy?\n• The maxilla is the upper jawbone. A maxillectomy refers to the removal of part or all of the maxilla.", "○ Due to a tumor in the hard palate, upper gum area, or sinuses.\n• After surgery, some patients might need an obturator (prosthetic device) to aid in restoring speech and chewing.\n• Reconstruction can be done using tissue from other parts of the body."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-02-09.jpg", "heading": "What is a maxillectomy?"}, "04-02-10": {"chunks": ["What is oral composite resection?\n• This is a more extensive surgery involving tumor removal throughout multiple connected structures in the mouth (tongue, ﬂoor of mouth, jawbone, or soft tissue).\n• Used for larger or more complex cancers.\n• Reconstruction and rehabilitation afterwards are common."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-02-10.jpg", "heading": "What is oral composite resection?"}, "04-02-12": {"chunks": ["What to expect after your procedure\n• Recovery from your procedure may include: ○ Swelling, changes in speech/swallowing. ○ Temporary feeding tubes or tracheostomy. ○ Reconstruction or prosthetics may be implemented. ○ Physical, speech, swallowing therapy as part of the healing process."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-02-12.jpg", "heading": "What to expect after your procedure"}, "04-03-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nWhat to Expect After the Procedure Medications and Incision Care Procedure Background General Instructions Eating, Drinking, Activity Concerns Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "04-03-04": {"chunks": ["What is a neck dissection?\n• A neck dissection is a surgical procedure done to remove lymph nodes and surrounding tissue in the neck that may be at risk of containing cancer.\n• It is done to prevent cancer from spreading.\n• It is done under general anesthesia.\n• The surgeon makes an incision along the neck to access and remove lymph nodes."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-04.jpg", "heading": "What is a neck dissection?"}, "04-03-05": {"chunks": ["What to Expect After the\nProcedure"], "photo": null, "heading": "What to Expect After the"}, "04-03-06": {"chunks": ["After the procedure, it is common to have\n• Pain and mild swelling at the incision site. ○ Swelling peaks ~4 days post surgery.\n• Numbness in the surgery area.\n• The skin around the surgery site will be tight and ﬁrm to the touch.", "○ This will soften over 4-6 months.\n• Numbness, tingling, and shooting pains are normal post-surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-06.jpg", "heading": "After the procedure, it is common to have"}, "04-03-08": {"chunks": ["General instructions for recovery\n• Use additional pillows if needed to keep your head raised when you lie down for the ﬁrst few weeks after surgery.\n• This helps prevent increased swelling.\n• Use cold compresses (ice packs, bags of frozen foods) to help reduce swelling.\n• Keep up with ALL follow-up visits"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-08.jpg", "heading": "General instructions for recovery"}, "04-03-10": {"chunks": ["Medications to take post-surgery\n• Take over-the-counter and prescription medications as instructed\n• If you were prescribed an antibiotic, take it as directed. ○ Do not stop taking it even if you feel better.\n• While on pain meds, avoid driving.\n• Some prescriptions can cause constipation. As a result:", "○ Drink enough ﬂuids ○ Take over-the-counter stool softeners ○ Eat foods high in ﬁber ○ Limit foods high in fat and processed sugars"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-10.jpg", "heading": "Medications to take post-surgery"}, "04-03-11": {"chunks": ["Incision care\n• You may shower 48 hours post-surgery. Any soaps can be used. Do not scrub the incision site, just let the water run down your neck.\n• Leave skin glue in place. We will show you how to remove it during your ﬁrst post-op visit.\n• You may have a drain in place which would be removed ~4-5 days after your surgery.", "○ The color of the drainage may change from red, a rose color, or yellow, which is a normal progression."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-11.jpg", "heading": "Incision care"}, "04-03-12": {"chunks": ["Incision care (cont.)\n• You may use a gauze sponge or other dressing to prevent leaking.\n• Check your incision area every day for infection signs such as:", "○ More redness, swelling, pain ○ Fluid or blood ○ Warmth ○ Pus or a foul smell\n• You may shower with a drain in place. Use a headband or something similar to secure the drain to your neck as you shower."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-12.jpg", "heading": "Incision care (cont.)"}, "04-03-14": {"chunks": ["Eating and drinking post-surgery\n• Please follow a regular diet while your drain is in place.\n• If your mouth or jaw is sore, eating soft foods until you feel better is the best option."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-14.jpg", "heading": "Eating and drinking post-surgery"}, "04-03-15": {"chunks": ["Activity post-surgery\n• You might be more easily fatigued after surgery. This is normal. Please rest as needed but also be sure you’re up and moving to improve blood ﬂow and breathing.\n• Do not lift anything heavier than 10 lbs for 3 weeks post surgery.\n• Do not submerge your incision in any swimming pools, rivers, hot tubs, bath tubs, etc. for 4 weeks post-surgery.", "○ You may only shower."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-15.jpg", "heading": "Activity post-surgery"}, "04-03-17": {"chunks": ["Contact our ofﬁce if:\n• You have pain that does not get better with pain medications.\n• You have increasing redness, swelling, or pain around the incision.\n• Your incision feels warm to the touch.\n• You have pus or a bad smell coming from the incision.\n• You develop a fever."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-03-17.jpg", "heading": "Contact our ofﬁce if:"}, "04-04-02": {"chunks": ["What is the oropharynx?\n• The oropharynx is the middle part of your throat.\n• It includes: ○ The back of your tongue ○ Your tonsils ○ The roof of your mouth (soft palate) ○ The walls of your throat\n• This area helps you talk, breathe, eat, and swallow."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-02.jpg", "heading": "What is the oropharynx?"}, "04-04-03": {"chunks": ["What is Transoral Robotic Surgery?\n• TORS stands for Transoral Robotic Surgery.\n• It helps surgeons remove cancer through the mouth.\n• It is a good option when the cancer is in a hard-to-reach area.\n• TORS avoids large cuts and can help you heal faster."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-03.jpg", "heading": "What is Transoral Robotic Surgery?"}, "04-04-04": {"chunks": ["Where is TORS used?\n• Best areas: ○ Tonsils ○ Base of the tongue ○ Roof of the mouth (palate) ○ Back of the throat (pharyngeal wall) ○ Voice box (supraglottis)\n• Some deep areas may still need laser surgery (TLM)"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-04.jpg", "heading": "Where is TORS used?"}, "04-04-05": {"chunks": ["Weighing the Risks and Beneﬁts\nBENEFITS RISKS\n• No large cuts to the face or jaw\n• May reduce or avoid the need for chemo or radiation\n• Often no need for a breathing tube (tracheostomy)\n• Faster return to talking and eating\n• Less pain and bleeding\n• Shorter hospital stay and recovery", "• Bleeding: especially around 10 days after surgery\n• Taste changes (dysgeusia)\n• Trouble swallowing (may need a feeding tube at ﬁrst)\n• Nasal food reﬂux or speech changes (rare)\n• Leak or ﬁstula (more likely if neck surgery is done too)"], "photo": null, "heading": "Weighing the Risks and Beneﬁts"}, "04-04-06": {"chunks": ["Who is a good candidate for TORS?\n• Not everyone is a good ﬁt\n• We look at: ○ Tumor location and size ○ How wide the mouth opens (trismus) ○ Shape of the jaw and tongue ○ Head movement (can you tilt your head back?) ○ Imaging to check blood vessels and tumor location", "**Disclaimer: The risks and benefits listed are general and may not apply to everyone. Your healthcare team will evaluate your individual condition to decide if TORS is right for you."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-06.jpg", "heading": "Who is a good candidate for TORS?"}, "04-04-07": {"chunks": ["Who is NOT a good candidate for TORS?\n• Tumor too close to the spine or big blood vessels\n• Tumor involves too much of the tongue or throat wall\n• Tumor is ﬁxed (stuck) to nearby tissues\n• Carotid artery too close to tumor\n• Not safe for general anesthesia\n• Can’t open mouth enough to reach tumor", "**Disclaimer: The risks and benefits listed are general and may not apply to everyone. Your healthcare team will evaluate your individual condition to decide if TORS is right for you."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-07.jpg", "heading": "Who is NOT a good candidate for TORS?"}, "04-04-08": {"chunks": ["How do I prepare for surgery?\n• You may meet with medical consult and anesthesia teams based on your age and medical history\n• These teams help ensure you are ready for anesthesia and surgery\n• Your care team includes the surgeon, residents, advanced practice providers (APPs), nurses, and anesthesia providers"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-08.jpg", "heading": "How do I prepare for surgery?"}, "04-04-09": {"chunks": ["How do I prepare for surgery? (cont.)\n• You will be told when to stop eating and drinking before surgery (usually nothing after midnight)\n• Your team will tell you which medications to continue or stop\n• You will be notiﬁed of your surgery start time and arrival time"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-09.jpg", "heading": "How do I prepare for surgery? (cont.)"}, "04-04-10": {"chunks": ["What happens in the procedure?\n• The procedure lasts about 3 to 4 hours\n• If neck dissection (removal of lymph nodes) is done, the surgery may take longer\n• You may have a feeding tube placed through your nose to your stomach to ensure you get enough nutrition\n• You will be under general anesthesia, meaning you will be fully asleep"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-10.jpg", "heading": "What happens in the procedure?"}, "04-04-11": {"chunks": ["What happens after the procedure?\n• You will be monitored in the recovery room and then moved to the intensive care unit (ICU) for one night and once stable, you will move to a regular hospital unit\n• Your safety is important, so always ask for help before getting out of bed\n• Your team will monitor you closely for bleeding or other complications"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-11.jpg", "heading": "What happens after the procedure?"}, "04-04-12": {"chunks": ["What is the recovery process like?\n• Recovery time depends on your surgery type, pain control, and ability to eat and drink\n• Most patients stay in the hospital for at least 3 days\n• Throat pain is expected\n• Pain and bleeding risk are highest from days 5 to 10 after surgery\n• Most patients feel better by around 2 weeks"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-12.jpg", "heading": "What is the recovery process like?"}, "04-04-13": {"chunks": ["Diet After Surgery\n• Day 0 (day of surgery): Start with clear liquids like broth, apple juice, or tea\n• Day 1 (day after surgery): Progress to full liquids such as smoothies, milkshakes, and nutritional drinks (Ensure, Boost)\n• Stay on full liquids for 2 days\n• Try soft foods before leaving the hospital and move to a regular diet as tolerated"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-13.jpg", "heading": "Diet After Surgery"}, "04-04-14": {"chunks": ["Self-Care After Discharge\n• If you have a neck incision, clean it with normal saline twice a day and apply Aquaphor\n• Avoid lifting more than 10 pounds for 2 weeks\n• Avoid activities that raise your heart rate\n• Gradually increase activity as tolerated\n• Drink 6 to 8 glasses of water daily\n• Use stool softeners to avoid constipation; stay hydrated and move around to help bowel function"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-14.jpg", "heading": "Self-Care After Discharge"}, "04-04-15": {"chunks": ["Care Team After Discharge\n• Your surgical team will decide when you’re ready for discharge\n• Discharge planning starts before you leave and includes input from your surgical team, nurses, social worker, physical therapist, speech/swallow therapist, and nutritionist\n• You may go home or to a rehab/skilled nursing facility depending on your needs"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-04-15.jpg", "heading": "Care Team After Discharge"}, "04-05-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nSubmandibular Salivary Glands Parotidectomy Gland Resection Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "04-05-04": {"chunks": ["What are salivary glands?\nSalivary glands make saliva, which helps you chew, swallow, and digest food. You have 3 main pairs:\n• Parotid glands (in front of your ears)\n• Submandibular glands (under your jaw)\n• Sublingual glands (under your tongue)", "These glands can sometimes develop tumors, which may be benign (non-cancerous) or malignant (cancerous)."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-05-04.jpg", "heading": "What are salivary glands?"}, "04-05-06": {"chunks": ["What is a parotidectomy?\nSuperﬁcial Parotidectomy Total Parotidectomy A parotidectomy is surgery to remove the parotid gland, the largest salivary gland. There are two types:\n• Superﬁcial parotidectomy\n• Total parotidectomy"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-05-06.jpg", "heading": "What is a parotidectomy?"}, "04-05-07": {"chunks": ["Superﬁcial vs. Total Parotidectomy\nTOTAL SUPERFICIAL\n• The entire parotid gland is removed.\n• This may be needed if:", "• Only the outer (superﬁcial) part of the gland is removed.\n• Used when the tumor is small and not deep.\n• The surgeon carefully avoids the facial nerve, which runs through the gland and controls smiling, blinking, and other facial movements.", "○ The tumor is deep inside the gland ○ The cancer is more aggressive\n• The facial nerve is still preserved when possible, but sometimes needs to be partially removed if involved by the tumor."], "photo": null, "heading": "Superﬁcial vs. Total Parotidectomy"}, "04-05-08": {"chunks": ["What to expect after a parotidectomy?\n• You may feel numbness or weakness in your face—this is usually temporary, but can sometimes last longer.\n• A drain may be placed for 1–2 days to remove extra ﬂuid.\n• You may have a scar near your ear or jaw, which usually heals well over time.\n• Most patients go home within a day or two."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-05-08.jpg", "heading": "What to expect after a parotidectomy?"}, "04-05-09": {"chunks": ["Submandibular Gland\nResection"], "photo": null, "heading": "Submandibular Gland"}, "04-05-10": {"chunks": ["What is a submandibular gland resection?\n• This surgery removes the submandibular gland, located under the jaw.\n• It is done when a tumor or stone is found in the gland.\n• The incision is made in the upper neck, under the jaw."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-05-10.jpg", "heading": "What is a submandibular gland resection?"}, "04-05-11": {"chunks": ["What are the risks of a submandibular gland resection?\n• Temporary or permanent numbness in the tongue or lower lip", "○ The marginal mandibular nerve, which controls the lower lip, runs close by ○ Your surgeon will try to protect it\n• A visible scar in the neck (usually fades over time)\n• Swelling or tightness during healing"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-05-11.jpg", "heading": "What are the risks of a submandibular gland resection?"}, "04-05-12": {"chunks": ["Recovery Tips\n• Keep the area clean and dry\n• Follow your surgeon’s instructions on:", "○ Pain medicine ○ Wound care ○ Drain care (if you have one)\n• You may have a follow-up with the surgeon and possibly a speech therapist if needed.\n• Your care team will let you know if you need radiation or other treatments based on the tumor type."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-05-12.jpg", "heading": "Recovery Tips"}, "04-06-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nBasic concepts, deﬁnitions, and background What to expect before, during, and after the hospital Reconstruction when bone is involved: upper and lower jaw (maxilla and mandible) Reconstruction of soft tissue (no bone is reconstructed) Flap surgery involving the inside of the mouth", "Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "04-06-03": {"chunks": ["Basic Concepts, Deﬁnitions,\nand Background to Flaps"], "photo": null, "heading": "Basic Concepts, Deﬁnitions,"}, "04-06-04": {"chunks": ["What is a ﬂap?\nA ﬂap is a piece of tissue used to ﬁx a wound or cover a missing area after surgery. A ﬂap is different from a graft because it has its own blood supply (a graft does not). Blood ﬂows into the ﬂap through an artery and out of the ﬂap through a vein to keep it alive."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-04.jpg", "heading": "What is a ﬂap?"}, "04-06-05": {"chunks": ["What can a ﬂap be made of?\nSoft tissue (skin, muscle, fat) Bone Both soft tissue and bone"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-05.jpg", "heading": "What can a ﬂap be made of?"}, "04-06-06": {"chunks": ["What is a graft?\nA graft is a piece of tissue used to repair or rebuild an area, but it does not have its own blood supply. A skin graft is very thin (like a piece of paper) and does not need blood vessels. It can use oxygen and nutrients from the tissue it is placed on.", "Because it is so thin, a skin graft is mostly used for surface repairs, not deep wounds."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-06.jpg", "heading": "What is a graft?"}, "04-06-07": {"chunks": ["Bone or Cartilage Grafts\nSometimes bone or cartilage can be used as a graft. Bone or cartilage grafts are small pieces without blood supply. They get oxygen and nutrients from the soft tissue around them."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-07.jpg", "heading": "Bone or Cartilage Grafts"}, "04-06-08": {"chunks": ["Free Flap\nA free ﬂap is taken from a distant body part (like the forearm or lower leg). Blood vessels are cut to move the ﬂap to the needed area. The ﬂap vessels are connected to blood vessels in the neck (artery to artery, vein to vein).", "This process is like a transplant, but since it comes from the patient’s own body, there is no risk of rejection and no anti-rejection medications are needed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-08.jpg", "heading": "Free Flap"}, "04-06-09": {"chunks": ["Pedicled Flap\nA pedicled ﬂap is taken from a nearby area (like the chest or shoulder). The pedicled ﬂap stays attached to its original blood vessels."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-09.jpg", "heading": "Pedicled Flap"}, "04-06-10": {"chunks": ["Key Differences Between Free Flaps and Pedicled Flaps\nA free ﬂap gives more freedom to shape and position the tissue. When bone is needed a free ﬂap is usually necessary (unless the piece of bone is very small)."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-10.jpg", "heading": "Key Differences Between Free Flaps and Pedicled Flaps"}, "04-06-11": {"chunks": ["Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft\nA skin graft may be too thin and not work well for deep wounds. Skin grafts in the mouth can cause scarring and limit tongue movement. Skin grafts cannot be placed directly on bone because bone does not provide enough blood supply."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-11.jpg", "heading": "Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft"}, "04-06-12": {"chunks": ["Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft\nGrafts may not heal well if the area has had or will have radiation therapy. A pedicled ﬂap may be too thick or not reach the area needing repair."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-12.jpg", "heading": "Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft"}, "04-06-13": {"chunks": ["Flap Surgery Involving the\nInside of the Mouth"], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Surgery Involving the"}, "04-06-14": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Flap Surgery Involving the Inside of the Mouth or Throat\nHow does surgery affect eating, drinking, and breathing? The mouth and throat are important for eating, drinking, speaking, and breathing.", "Surgery in these areas can cause swelling, which may make it hard to breathe, sometimes requiring a temporary tracheostomy tube (a breathing tube in the neck). It can also affect the ability to swallow food and liquids, making a feeding tube necessary during healing."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Flap Surgery Involving the Inside of the Mouth or Throat"}, "04-06-15": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer When can I eat by mouth?\nThe flap (usually skin) is stitched into place to repair the surgical wound in the mouth. Stitches dissolve in about a month, but healing takes 2 to 3 weeks or longer. The wound must be fully sealed to prevent saliva from leaking into the wound. Your surgeon will decide when it is safe for you to start eating and drinking again."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-15.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer When can I eat by mouth?"}, "04-06-16": {"chunks": ["Can I swallow my own saliva while I am healing, or should I spit it out?\nYes, you can swallow your saliva. It is not recommended to constantly spit or suction it out, unless you cannot swallow. This could cause dehydration over time."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-06-16.jpg", "heading": "Can I swallow my own saliva while I am healing, or should I spit it out?"}, "04-07-02": {"chunks": ["Lower Jaw (Mandible)\nReconstruction"], "photo": null, "heading": "Lower Jaw (Mandible)"}, "04-07-03": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction\nWhy is a free flap used to repair the lower jaw? Reconstructing the lower jaw usually requires a free flap because these cancers need radiation therapy after surgery to prevent the cancer from returning.", "Radiation would destroy a bone graft because the graft does not have a blood supply, but the free flap does have blood supply."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction"}, "04-07-04": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction\nAlso, a free flap is used when there is a gap in the bone bigger than about 5 cm (2 inches) because larger grafts may not survive.", "The bone-containing flaps that are commonly used for jaw reconstruction are the fibula, scapula, and a small amount from the radius/forearm flaps (AKA osteocutaneous radial forearm free flap)."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-04.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction Continued"}, "04-07-05": {"chunks": ["Which type of ﬂap will be used for the mandible? The ﬁbula ﬂap\nSurgeons usually use the ﬁbula ﬂap to rebuild the jaw. The ﬁbula is a strong bone in the lower leg that can be shaped to ﬁt the jaw. We also take some soft tissue (skin, fat, and fascia) from the leg to help with the reconstruction."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-05.jpg", "heading": "Which type of ﬂap will be used for the mandible? The ﬁbula ﬂap"}, "04-07-06": {"chunks": ["The Fibula Flap\nThe ﬁbula’s blood vessels are long, which helps position the new jaw correctly. Another beneﬁt is that while one surgeon removes the tumor, another can prepare the ﬁbula ﬂap at the same time. This can save 1 to 2 hours in surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-06.jpg", "heading": "The Fibula Flap Continued"}, "04-07-07": {"chunks": ["The Scapula Flap\nAnother option is the scapula ﬂap (from the shoulder blade). However, since it's on the back, it takes more time in surgery because surgeons can’t work on it at the same time as tumor removal.", "The scapula ﬂap is often used if the lower leg isn’t healthy enough for a ﬁbula ﬂap. This is because leg arteries can have more blockage (atherosclerosis), while the scapula’s artery is usually healthier."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-07.jpg", "heading": "The Scapula Flap"}, "04-07-08": {"chunks": ["The Scapula Flap\nUnlike the ﬁbula ﬂap, the scapula ﬂap doesn’t need a special scan (CT angiogram) to check blood ﬂow because the artery for the scapula ﬂap is not vital to any other body part.", "The scapula ﬂap is also a better choice when a lot of soft tissue is needed, as it provides more skin and muscle than the ﬁbula."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-08.jpg", "heading": "The Scapula Flap Continued"}, "04-07-09": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Other Options for Jaw Reconstruction- the Radial Forearm Flap\nAnother option is the radial forearm flap (from the inner forearm). This flap includes a thin piece of bone, so it’s not usually the first choice for the jaw. Instead, it’s often used for reconstructing the upper jaw, cheekbones, or eye sockets."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-09.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Other Options for Jaw Reconstruction- the Radial Forearm Flap"}, "04-07-10": {"chunks": ["How is the ﬂap bone connected to the jaw?\nA titanium plate is used to connect the pieces of bone together. This plate spans the gap across the mandible where the tumor was removed. The plate is screwed into the remaining parts of the mandible as well as the bone of the ﬂap after it is shaped to ﬁt the defect."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-10.jpg", "heading": "How is the ﬂap bone connected to the jaw?"}, "04-07-11": {"chunks": ["The Titanium Plate\nThe size and position of a mandible resection depends on the location of the tumor, but this is an example of a large reconstruction plate.", "Every resection is a little different. In this case, the entire chin is being removed. The bone that needs to be removed is determined by the cancer surgeon, based on the location and size of the tumor. Click here to return to forearm ﬂap slide 81"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-11.jpg", "heading": "The Titanium Plate Continued"}, "04-07-12": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Is the titanium plate safe?\nYes, the titanium plate is safe and stays in the body permanently for most patients. It does not cause allergies or reactions and cannot be rejected by the body."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-12.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Is the titanium plate safe?"}, "04-07-13": {"chunks": ["Is the titanium plate safe?\nThe titanium plate is not magnetic, so it won’t set off metal detectors and is safe for MRIs.", "Occasionally, the plate can become infected over time (rarely) and need to be removed. Another rare complication is when the plate becomes loose over time, requiring removal, or the plate breaks. These situations are rare."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-13.jpg", "heading": "Is the titanium plate safe? Continued"}, "04-07-14": {"chunks": ["Soft Tissue Repair in the Tongue, Cheek, or Under the Tongue\nIf there is a soft tissue defect in the tongue, cheek, or under the tongue (ﬂoor of the mouth) some skin or muscle from the ﬂap is sutured in place to patch the soft tissue defect. The sutures will all dissolve on their own."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-14.jpg", "heading": "Soft Tissue Repair in the Tongue, Cheek, or Under the Tongue"}, "04-07-15": {"chunks": ["Will I look like myself after mandible reconstruction surgery or will I be disﬁgured?\nFor most patients, the changes to appearance after mandible reconstruction are minimal. Signiﬁcant changes are rare and usually only happen if the tumor or wound affects the external skin or soft tissue of the face or neck. This is typically seen in very large tumors."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-15.jpg", "heading": "Will I look like myself after mandible reconstruction surgery or will I be disﬁgured?"}, "04-07-16": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?\nGenerally, this surgery is done through the inside of the mouth and an incision on the neck. It is not usually necessary to place any scars on the face unless the tumor is invading the skin of the face or neck (this is not common)."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-16.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?"}, "04-07-17": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?\nA neck incision is necessary to find blood vessels in the neck to re-establish blood flow to the flap. These neck incisions heal very well and leave a faint scar that is generally not obvious after it heals.", "Usually, lymph nodes need to be removed from the neck as part of the cancer surgery. The same neck incision is used for this as well."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?"}, "04-07-18": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Will my face shape change?\nThe shape of the reconstructed mandible is based on the shape of each individual patient’s jaw. The titanium plate is custom made to each patient’s anatomy.", "It is not possible to make the new jaw the exact same shape as the patient's natural jaw, however, the changes are usually subtle once healing is complete"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-07-18.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Will my face shape change?"}, "04-08-02": {"chunks": ["Swelling of the Face and Neck\nSwelling of the face and neck is normal and expected after surgery. Usually, this swelling is the worst 3 days after the surgery and then gradually improves over the next several weeks to months. Swelling may come and go at times- this is normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-02.jpg", "heading": "Swelling of the Face and Neck"}, "04-08-03": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer When is swelling the worst?\nSometimes patients find that swelling is worse in the morning when they wake up – because they have been lying down all night. If this is the case, sleep with your head elevated on extra pillows, or even in a reclining chair if necessary. Sometimes patients notice swelling increases throughout the day, and it is better in the morning."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-03.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer When is swelling the worst?"}, "04-08-04": {"chunks": ["When should I be worried about swelling?\nIf swelling is gradually worsening over several consecutive days, this may be a cause for concern, especially if there is also increased pain, redness of the neck or face, or drainage of pus from incisions or drain tubes.", "The presence of fevers and/or chills is a warning sign as well. Talk to your surgical team."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-04.jpg", "heading": "When should I be worried about swelling?"}, "04-08-05": {"chunks": ["Is this surgery painful?\nThis type of facial surgery is generally less painful than most patients expect. There is certainly some pain, but it is usually well controlled with pain medicine.", "There may be some pain that comes and goes. Pain may be worse at certain times of day and some days may be worse than others. This is normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-05.jpg", "heading": "Is this surgery painful?"}, "04-08-06": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer When is pain concerning?\nPain is worrisome if it is continually increasing over several days. Especially if there is also fevers/chills, swelling that is gradually increasing over several days, or any new drainage from any incisions."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-06.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer When is pain concerning?"}, "04-08-07": {"chunks": ["Upper Jaw / Midface\nReconstruction"], "photo": null, "heading": "Upper Jaw / Midface"}, "04-08-08": {"chunks": ["Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction\nMaxillary reconstruction is similar to mandible reconstruction in many ways. The bones are smaller than the mandible, so while titanium plates are still used to join the bone segments together, these are smaller than the plates used for mandible reconstruction.", "Plates are still usually custom made for each individual patient."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-08.jpg", "heading": "Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction"}, "04-08-09": {"chunks": ["Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction\nOther than these differences, the details of the mandible reconstruction section above apply to patients who are undergoing maxillary reconstruction."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-09.jpg", "heading": "Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction Continued"}, "04-08-11": {"chunks": ["The Fibula Flap. How is the ﬁbula used for reconstruction?\nThe ﬁbula is the smaller of the two lower leg bones (the larger bone is the tibia). It can be safely removed and used for reconstruction. The central part of the ﬁbula is removed, saving the bottom 3-4 inches by the ankle, and a similar length by the knee."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-11.jpg", "heading": "The Fibula Flap. How is the ﬁbula used for reconstruction?"}, "04-08-12": {"chunks": ["Will this affect blood ﬂow to my leg?\nThe lower leg has 3 arteries that carry blood down to the foot. The artery called the peroneal artery supplies the ﬁbula ﬂap. Because the leg has 3 main arteries, and if the other two arteries are healthy, blood ﬂow to the foot remains normal after surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-12.jpg", "heading": "Will this affect blood ﬂow to my leg?"}, "04-08-13": {"chunks": ["How do we make sure it’s safe?\nWe will check your pulses on your foot and order a scan called a CT Angiogram, which examines the arteries in the legs. We do this to make sure that it is safe to take the peroneal vessels supplying the ﬁbula ﬂap without hurting the circulation to the foot."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-13.jpg", "heading": "How do we make sure it’s safe?"}, "04-08-14": {"chunks": ["What will my leg look like after surgery?\nIf no skin is needed for reconstruction, you will have a in scar, in a straight line, running down the outside of the calf, from below the knee to above the ankle. The example on the right shows a scar after two weeks of healing—this is not the ﬁnal result."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-14.jpg", "heading": "What will my leg look like after surgery?"}, "04-08-15": {"chunks": ["What happens if skin is needed for reconstruction?\nIf skin is necessary for reconstruction, the resulting skin wound usually cannot be closed. This leaves a shallow, open wound, with exposed muscle, after the ﬂap is taken.", "To repair this, we may take a skin graft from the thigh. This skin graft is paper thin and is used to patch the leg wound. It will always look somewhat like a patch, but the color will even out over time, and the scars will fade and become smooth."], "photo": null, "heading": "What happens if skin is needed for reconstruction?"}, "04-08-16": {"chunks": ["What are my other options for a skin graft?\nMore commonly, we are now using a skin substitute called biodegradable tissue matrix (BTM), rather than a skin graft for ﬁbula donor sites. This is an artiﬁcial skin substitute that helps the wound heal.", "There is a thin plastic layer covering the BTM. The surgeon will take this off in the clinic. If you notice it falling off, do not be alarmed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-16.jpg", "heading": "What are my other options for a skin graft?"}, "04-08-17": {"chunks": ["How does BTM work?\nThe BTM forms a layer of healing tissue called granulation tissue over the wound bed. Once this (granulation tissue) has formed, the body forms skin over the BTM naturally."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-17.jpg", "heading": "How does BTM work?"}, "04-08-18": {"chunks": ["How long will this healing process take?\nThis process can take several months, depending on the size of the defect. This is an example of what it may look like when it's healed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-08-18.jpg", "heading": "How long will this healing process take?"}, "04-09-02": {"chunks": ["What to expect after surgery— drain tube\nThere will be a plastic surgical drain tube placed in the wound at the time of surgery. This is usually removed before you leave the hospital. Drain tube"], "photo": null, "heading": "What to expect after surgery— drain tube"}, "04-09-03": {"chunks": ["What to expect after surgery– wound VAC\nThere will also be a wound VAC placed on the donor site (the are the skin is taken from) in the hospital. Patients rarely go home with a wound VAC, so you don’t need to learn to manage it. *We have additional information on the wound VAC elsewhere"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-03.jpg", "heading": "What to expect after surgery– wound VAC"}, "04-09-04": {"chunks": ["When can I walk?\nYou can walk and bear weight immediately after surgery, with assistance. Immediately after surgery, patients have many monitors, drains, and IV lines attached to them-- it is easy to trip and fall.", "You will need to be helped when getting out of bed while you are in the hospital, especially while in the ICU."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-04.jpg", "heading": "When can I walk?"}, "04-09-05": {"chunks": ["Will I have pain in my leg?\nThe pain from the ﬁbula ﬂap is usually less severe than people expect. Usually, it is more bothersome after patients start walking more at home, and it can linger for weeks or even months."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-05.jpg", "heading": "Will I have pain in my leg?"}, "04-09-06": {"chunks": ["How long will I have pain in my leg?\nThis pain resolves with time, but sometimes it can take months or a year. Usually, the pain is mild soreness and lasts about 6 months. It is like having a sprained ankle"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-06.jpg", "heading": "How long will I have pain in my leg?"}, "04-09-07": {"chunks": ["Will I need physical therapy?\nPhysical therapy is sometimes needed, but not always. Generally, healthy, active people regain full mobility more quickly than patients who have a lower baseline level of mobility and activity prior to surgery.", "Usually, active, healthy patients can walk without assistance (meaning without a cane or walker) in a few weeks."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-07.jpg", "heading": "Will I need physical therapy?"}, "04-09-08": {"chunks": ["Will the surgery affect my walking?\nThe ﬁbula bone functions to provide stability to the ankle, in addition to the many ligaments of the ankle and foot that bind the different bones together. When the ﬁbula is removed, extra stress is placed on these ligaments.", "Over time, the ligaments strengthen and compensate for the loss of the ﬁbula. Until this happens, there will be some discomfort and instability."], "photo": null, "heading": "Will the surgery affect my walking?"}, "04-09-09": {"chunks": ["Will I have have mobility issues?\nPatients who have problems with strength or mobility pre-operatively may require weeks or months of using a walker or cane, along with physical therapy. This is not usually expected, but it is a possibility to prepare for.", "It is very unusual to have any permanent mobility problems after a ﬁbula ﬂap, but healing can be slow."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-09.jpg", "heading": "Will I have have mobility issues?"}, "04-09-10": {"chunks": ["Is leg swelling normal?\nSwelling in the ﬁbula ﬂap donor leg is quite common. If you have any swelling in the legs before surgery, it is very likely you will have more severe swelling in the donor leg after surgery. This is usually temporary"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-10.jpg", "heading": "Is leg swelling normal?"}, "04-09-11": {"chunks": ["What can I do to help the swelling go down?\nYou can use an ACE wrap or a compression stocking to help with this."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-11.jpg", "heading": "What can I do to help the swelling go down?"}, "04-09-12": {"chunks": ["How do I use the ACE wrap?\nMake sure to wrap the leg starting at the toes, around the foot, and ankle. The goal is to provide more compression to the foot and ankle than to the calf.", "Wrapping the leg without wrapping the ankle will lead to worsened swelling because ﬂuid will be trapped below the compression, in the ankle. *We have a leg dressing guide that provides more instructions on how to wrap the leg appropriately"], "photo": null, "heading": "How do I use the ACE wrap?"}, "04-09-13": {"chunks": ["Scapula (Shoulder Blade)\nFlap"], "photo": null, "heading": "Scapula (Shoulder Blade)"}, "04-09-14": {"chunks": ["Is the whole scapula used?\nWhen the scapula is needed for reconstruction, it is not removed in its entirety. Only the edge of the bone or the tip of the scapula is taken, leaving most of the scapula intact."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-14.jpg", "heading": "Is the whole scapula used?"}, "04-09-15": {"chunks": ["What will the incision look like?\nThe incision will run along the ﬂank, from the axilla (under-arm) down about 10 inches or so. The donor site wound can usually be closed completely, without a skin graft."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-15.jpg", "heading": "What will the incision look like?"}, "04-09-16": {"chunks": ["Will my shoulder function be affected?\nIt is rare to have permanent major functional problems from a scapula ﬂap. There will certainly be some soreness and stiffness, which may last several months. If necessary, we will arrange for physical therapy."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-09-16.jpg", "heading": "Will my shoulder function be affected?"}, "04-09-17": {"chunks": ["How sore or stiff will I feel after?\nThe degree of soreness and stiffness depends on the amount of tissue that is needed for the reconstruction. Smaller tumors and smaller ﬂaps lead to less issues with recovery. Larger surgeries can have more lasting stiffness and soreness."], "photo": null, "heading": "How sore or stiff will I feel after?"}, "04-10-02": {"chunks": ["Will I have drain tubes?\nTwo rubber drainage tubes are left in the ﬂank at the time of surgery to drain ﬂuid that accumulates in the surgical wound. Usually, one drain is removed after about one week and the second usually stays with the patient after they are discharged home. drain tube"], "photo": null, "heading": "Will I have drain tubes?"}, "04-10-03": {"chunks": ["How do I take care of my drain?\nThe drain needs to be emptied twice daily (or more depending on the amount of drainage) and the output needs to be recorded. The surgeon will want to know the daily output of the drain in milliliters (ML) for each 24-hour period.", "When the drain output is less than 50 ML per 24-hour period, it may be ready to be removed."], "photo": null, "heading": "How do I take care of my drain?"}, "04-10-04": {"chunks": ["Will ﬂuid accumulate after the drain tube is removed?\nOccasionally, ﬂuid continues to accumulate even after the drain is removed. This can lead to swelling of the skin that feels soft, like a water balloon. When you stand up the swelling will drift down to the lowest portion of the surgical site."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-04.jpg", "heading": "Will ﬂuid accumulate after the drain tube is removed?"}, "04-10-05": {"chunks": ["What should I do if ﬂuid accumulates after the drain is removed?\nThis ﬂuid (called a seroma) usually needs to be drained but the procedure is easy and usually done in the ofﬁce.", "The skin overlying the area is usually numb from the surgery and the ﬂuid can be drained through a small needle inserted through the skin. Sometimes this can require several drainage procedures if the ﬂuid keeps reaccumulating."], "photo": null, "heading": "What should I do if ﬂuid accumulates after the drain is removed?"}, "04-10-06": {"chunks": ["How long can I wait to get the ﬂuid drained?\nThis is not urgent – you do not need to come in the same day for this problem. Waiting even a week or two will be okay"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-06.jpg", "heading": "How long can I wait to get the ﬂuid drained?"}, "04-10-07": {"chunks": ["What wound care do I need to do for the scapula ﬂap?\nAside from drain care, all you need to do is apply petroleum jelly (Vaseline or Aquaphor) to the incision several times a day. This is to keep it from scabbing or drying out."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-07.jpg", "heading": "What wound care do I need to do for the scapula ﬂap?"}, "04-10-08": {"chunks": ["Reconstruction of Soft Tissue (No Bone is Reconstructed)\nRegional Reconstruction Skin Graft Forearm Flap Thigh Flap Click on the box to jump to Local Reconstruction each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Reconstruction of Soft Tissue (No Bone is Reconstructed)"}, "04-10-10": {"chunks": ["What is a forearm ﬂap?\nThe forearm ﬂap consists of skin from the inner surface of the forearm, above the wrist."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-10.jpg", "heading": "What is a forearm ﬂap?"}, "04-10-11": {"chunks": ["How does the hand receive blood after surgery?\nThe radial artery is the blood vessel that supplies blood ﬂow to the forearm ﬂap, and it is removed with the ﬂap during surgery. Once this is removed, the ulnar artery is the only vessel supplying blood to the hand."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-11.jpg", "heading": "How does the hand receive blood after surgery?"}, "04-10-12": {"chunks": ["How do we make sure its safe?\nTo ensure the hand will be safe and have sufﬁcient blood ﬂow after the ﬂap is taken, we do a simple, non-invasive test during the clinic examination. It is extremely rare to have an injury to the hand if this test looks good.", "If there is doubt as to the safety of performing a radial forearm ﬂap, we will choose a different type of ﬂap instead."], "photo": null, "heading": "How do we make sure its safe?"}, "04-10-13": {"chunks": ["Is any bone taken from my arm?\nOccasionally, bone is needed from the radius (forearm bone). This is not typical, and unless your surgeon has discussed this with you ahead of time it is extremely unlikely."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-13.jpg", "heading": "Is any bone taken from my arm?"}, "04-10-14": {"chunks": ["What about if bone is taken from my arm?\nIf bone is taken from this location, the part taken doesn't go through the whole bone. A partial thickness shaving is taken from the inner surface (About 40-50%).", "To strengthen the area, a metal plate is placed across the section where part of the bone was removed and held in place with screws."], "photo": null, "heading": "What about if bone is taken from my arm?"}, "04-10-15": {"chunks": ["What is the plate made of?\nThe plate is made of titanium (like the mandible plate described in the lower jaw reconstruction section). Click here to go to the mandible plate slide Titanium does not react with the body or cause allergies.", "It is not magnetic, so it is MRI compatible and does not set off metal detectors."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-15.jpg", "heading": "What is the plate made of?"}, "04-10-16": {"chunks": ["Will I have hand weakness?\nThis procedure does cause some temporary weakness of the hand, and it can take months to regain a strong grip. This is normal and physical therapy can help."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-16.jpg", "heading": "Will I have hand weakness?"}, "04-10-17": {"chunks": ["Wound Healing– Forearm\nUsually, the forearm donor site cannot be closed, and it is necessary to skin graft the forearm. To help the skin graft heal, a special dressing, called a wound VAC, is placed over it for 6 days. Once this is removed, there is a light dressing over the skin graft that you will change daily.", "*We have a separate information packet on wound care for the donor site."], "photo": null, "heading": "Wound Healing– Forearm"}, "04-10-18": {"chunks": ["What happens if part of the graft doesn’t survive?\nOccasionally, some part of the skin graft does not survive. This can lead to muscle or tendon exposure in the area on the forearm where the ﬂap was taken. Generally, these issues heal without further surgery Image to the left is a healed forearm graft"], "photo": null, "heading": "What happens if part of the graft doesn’t survive?"}, "04-10-19": {"chunks": ["How do I care for my forearm donor site?\nWe have a separate information packet on wound care for the donor site."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-10-19.jpg", "heading": "How do I care for my forearm donor site?"}, "04-10-20": {"chunks": ["How do I take care of the wound?\nManagement involves preventing the wound from drying out—Vaseline and a dressing that covers the wound are critical. If, during your care, you experience these wound issues, you will be provided with speciﬁc wound care instructions.", "Again, this type of wound almost always heals on its own and is not a major concern."], "photo": null, "heading": "How do I take care of the wound?"}, "04-11-03": {"chunks": ["Where is the thigh ﬂap taken from?\nThe ﬂap is taken from the outside of the thigh. The thigh ﬂap is based on a blood vessel that is not critical to the function of the leg. There is no test necessary to check this blood vessel (in contrast to the ﬁbula ﬂap and forearm ﬂap)."], "photo": null, "heading": "Where is the thigh ﬂap taken from?"}, "04-11-04": {"chunks": ["Will I need a skin graft?\nSometimes small openings develop along the incision line over the ﬁrst couple weeks. This can generally be treated with dressings and wound care—it rarely requires additional surgery. If this happens, your surgeon will provide speciﬁc instructions on how to care for it."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-04.jpg", "heading": "Will I need a skin graft?"}, "04-11-05": {"chunks": ["How long will a drain be in my leg?\nA drainage tube is left in the thigh at the time of surgery. This tube is about ¼\" thick and is necessary to drain ﬂuid that accumulates in the thigh because of surgery. Usually, the drain stays in place for a couple of weeks and patients go home with this leg drain."], "photo": null, "heading": "How long will a drain be in my leg?"}, "04-11-06": {"chunks": ["Drain Care\nThe nurses in the hospital will teach you how to care for your drain, which is an easy process. ●The drain needs to be emptied twice daily (or more frequently depending on the amount of drainage) and the output needs to be recorded. ●Your surgeon will want to know the daily", "output of the drain in milliliters (mL) for each 24-hour period."], "photo": null, "heading": "Drain Care"}, "04-11-07": {"chunks": ["When can I get my drain removed?\nWhen the drain output is less than 30-50 ML per 24-hour period, it may be ready to be removed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-07.jpg", "heading": "When can I get my drain removed?"}, "04-11-08": {"chunks": ["What if there is still ﬂuid post-drain removal?\nOccasionally, ﬂuid continues to accumulate even after the drain is removed. This can lead to swelling of the leg that feels soft, like a water balloon. When you stand up the swelling will move down to above the knee.", "This ﬂuid (called a seroma) usually needs to be drained, but the procedure is easy and done in the ofﬁce."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-08.jpg", "heading": "What if there is still ﬂuid post-drain removal?"}, "04-11-09": {"chunks": ["How will the ﬂuid (seroma) be drained?\nThe skin overlying the area is usually numb from the surgery and the ﬂuid can be drained through a small needle inserted through the skin. Sometimes this can require several drainage procedures if the ﬂuid keeps reaccumulating.", "This is not urgent – you do not need to come in the same day for this problem. Waiting even a week or two will be ok. The procedure is very minor and not bothersome for patients."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-09.jpg", "heading": "How will the ﬂuid (seroma) be drained?"}, "04-11-10": {"chunks": ["What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?\nAside from drain care, all you need to do is apply Vaseline or Aquaphor to the incision several times a day to keep it from scabbing or drying out."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-10.jpg", "heading": "What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?"}, "04-11-11": {"chunks": ["What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?\nWrap your thigh with an ACE bandage, so that compression is applied to the leg.\n• This will help reduce the amount of ﬂuid draining from the thigh.\n• Avoid excessive pressure causing pain or knee swelling."], "photo": null, "heading": "What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?"}, "04-11-12": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer How long do I need to wrap my thigh?\nContinue using the ACE wrap for 1 month after surgery, even after the drain is removed. ●Wrap as much of the thigh as possible. ●Another option is a full leg compression stocking."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer How long do I need to wrap my thigh?"}, "04-11-14": {"chunks": ["What are skin grafts?\n●A thin sheet of skin shaved from one area and placed onto a wound somewhere else. ●It is used for more surface-level wounds when the underlying tissue is healthy to support healing. ●Take 2-3 weeks to fully heal. ○Monitor both the donor and graft sites for infection"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-14.jpg", "heading": "What are skin grafts?"}, "04-11-16": {"chunks": ["What is local reconstruction?\n●This procedure uses tissue from around the surgical site (nearby skin or cells) to ﬁll a defect. ●Generally a shorter surgery and quicker recovery ○Mild bruising and swelling is common"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-16.jpg", "heading": "What is local reconstruction?"}, "04-11-18": {"chunks": ["What is regional reconstruction?\n• This procedure uses tissue from a nearby region (such as the chest muscle) which is rotated into the defect area.\n• This is ideal for larger defects and is more robust than a local reconstruction.", "○ It may leave a visible scar on the chest or neck. ○ Drains may be in place to prevent buildup of ﬂuid."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-11-18.jpg", "heading": "What is regional reconstruction?"}, "04-12-02": {"chunks": ["What to expect before, during, and after the hospital\nFitness and Preparation for Surgery What to expect in the hospital After the hospital and follow up Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-02.jpg", "heading": "What to expect before, during, and after the hospital"}, "04-12-03": {"chunks": ["Other Questions About Fitness and Preparation for\nSurgery"], "photo": null, "heading": "Other Questions About Fitness and Preparation for"}, "04-12-04": {"chunks": ["Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?\nReconstructive surgery can be lengthy, especially when combined with surgery for tumor removal. This certainly does place stress on the body."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-04.jpg", "heading": "Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?"}, "04-12-05": {"chunks": ["Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?\nYour primary medical doctor (PCP) will be asked to provide a clearance note for you to undergo anesthesia/surgery.", "If you have other medical problems, such as heart, lung, liver or kidney problems, a note may be needed from additional specialists, such as cardiologist, or pulmonologist, if applicable."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-05.jpg", "heading": "Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?"}, "04-12-06": {"chunks": ["Am I too old to have this surgery?\nMost of our patients are in their 50’s-80’s. Age itself does not lead to complications and the overall health and functional status of the patient are more important indicators of ﬁtness and risk."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-06.jpg", "heading": "Am I too old to have this surgery?"}, "04-12-07": {"chunks": ["Am I too old to have this surgery?\nSome patients are in their 80’s and are quite healthy. Others may be younger and have signiﬁcant medical problems that make surgery riskier.", "The decision as to whether anesthesia and surgery are safe for a patient is made by a multidisciplinary team of surgeons, anesthesia personnel, and other specialists."], "photo": null, "heading": "Am I too old to have this surgery?"}, "04-12-08": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer What can I do to prepare for surgery?\nThe most important thing leading up to surgery is to maintain good nutrition to support wound healing and to avoid any activities that worsen wound healing."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-08.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer What can I do to prepare for surgery?"}, "04-12-09": {"chunks": ["Good nutrition requires protein.\nProtein intake should be around 1.25-1.5 g of protein per kilogram of bodyweight per day. Supplementing your diet with Boost, Ensure, or other protein supplements and vitamins is good, especially if you have difﬁculty eating preoperatively.", "Excessive protein intake is not healthy, so it is a good idea to work with a dietician to assist with nutrition."], "photo": null, "heading": "Good nutrition requires protein."}, "04-12-10": {"chunks": ["Supplements\nOne regular daily multivitamin is sufﬁcient and there is no need to take additional supplements unless speciﬁcally instructed to by your doctor. Some herbal supplements can be harmful. Discuss any of these with your surgeon before taking."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-10.jpg", "heading": "Supplements"}, "04-12-11": {"chunks": ["Diabetes\nIf you have diabetes, make sure you are taking all your medications as prescribed by your primary doctor. Avoid excessively fatty and sugary foods."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-11.jpg", "heading": "Diabetes"}, "04-12-12": {"chunks": ["Diabetes\nUncontrolled diabetes will compromise your body’s ability to heal. ●If you're not sure your medicine is right for you, talk to your primary doctor as soon as possible to make sure your diabetes is under control before surgery. ●Also discuss any changes after", "discharge from the hospital with your primary doctor."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-12.jpg", "heading": "Diabetes"}, "04-12-13": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nIt is important to stop tobacco products (smoking, vaping, dipping, cigars, pipes, etc.) at least three weeks before surgery. Smoking impairs wound healing and increases the risk of postoperative complications."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-13.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-12-14": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nHeavy alcohol use can also cause postoperative complications If you have more than one or two drinks per day, stopping all consumption of alcohol “cold turkey” can lead to withdrawal symptoms.\n• It is best to wean yourself down over the course of a couple weeks."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-14.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-12-15": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nIf you are drinking daily before surgery, please let us know. You may require medication to help prevent alcohol withdrawal while you’re in the hospital. This is critical information for appropriate anesthesia and postoperative medications."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-15.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-12-16": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nSometimes it is difﬁcult for patients to be honest with their healthcare team regarding their use of alcohol, tobacco, or other products. It is important to be forthcoming with this information so that we can make sure you have the most appropriate care and best outcome."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-16.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-12-17": {"chunks": ["What to Expect in the\nHospital"], "photo": null, "heading": "What to Expect in the"}, "04-12-18": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery - Arrival\nYou will be asked to come to the hospital early in the morning. The hospital will call you the evening before surgery to tell you where to go and when to arrive. Usually, they want you to come in around 5:30 AM.\n• This is to prepare you for anesthesia.", "You will see the surgeons and anesthesiologists before going back to the operating room."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-18.jpg", "heading": "Day of Surgery - Arrival"}, "04-12-19": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery – Start of Surgery\nOnce in the operating room you will undergo anesthesia. This takes 15-20 minutes. Then once you are asleep, we will prepare you for the surgery by padding and positioning your body, inserting a catheter into the bladder, and inserting several more IV lines.", "Then the surgical site will be cleaned with antiseptics."], "photo": null, "heading": "Day of Surgery – Start of Surgery"}, "04-12-20": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery – During Surgery\nBy the time we start the operation, it will be around 8:30. Depending on the surgery, it may be anywhere from 5 to 12 or more hours.", "A designated family member or friend will receive updates from a nurse in the operating room. The surgeons will talk to family/friends when they have completed their portions of the procedure."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-20.jpg", "heading": "Day of Surgery – During Surgery"}, "04-12-21": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery – After Surgery\nAfter the procedure is completed, you will be transferred to the ICU and will wake up slowly. You may not remember anything from the night immediately following surgery.", "You may have a tracheostomy tube and feeding tube in place (if your surgeon discussed this with you), as well as several surgical drains in the neck and the area where the ﬂap was taken."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-12-21.jpg", "heading": "Day of Surgery – After Surgery"}, "04-13-02": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Hospital Stay\nYou will be in the hospital for about 10 days after free ﬂap surgery. Sometimes this is longer or shorter depending on the type of surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-02.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Hospital Stay"}, "04-13-03": {"chunks": ["Why do I have to stay for ~10 days?\nThe main purpose of the length of stay is to make sure the surgical sites are healing and that the ﬂap is healthy. Wound healing problems often do not arise until about 1 week after surgery. This is the main reason patients remain in the hospital so long."], "photo": null, "heading": "Why do I have to stay for ~10 days?"}, "04-13-04": {"chunks": ["Hospital Stay - ICU\nThe ﬁrst 3 days will be in the ICU. This is not because you will be sick or unstable, but simply for close monitoring. After this you will go to the regular ﬂoor."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-04.jpg", "heading": "Hospital Stay - ICU"}, "04-13-05": {"chunks": ["Hospital Stay – Care\nYou will receive physical therapy in the hospital. This becomes more in-depth after you leave the ICU. You will be helped out of bed to a chair the ﬁrst day after surgery and your activity will be increased from there.", "You will receive teaching regarding any wound care, tracheostomy care, feeding tube, or drain care needs."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-05.jpg", "heading": "Hospital Stay – Care"}, "04-13-06": {"chunks": ["Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?\nMost patients can go home after they leave the hospital. ●But if a patient doesn’t have anyone to help them at home, we might arrange for them to stay at a skilled nursing facility until they can manage on their own."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-06.jpg", "heading": "Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?"}, "04-13-07": {"chunks": ["Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?\nPhysical therapists usually decide if a patient needs to go to a rehab center after leaving the hospital. ●They will check on you several times while you're in the hospital, and if they think you need rehab, we will start making plans for your stay."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-07.jpg", "heading": "Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?"}, "04-13-08": {"chunks": ["Fistula and Wound Infection\nWhen you have surgery involving the mouth or throat, the wound is exposed to saliva. Even if the wounds are sutured perfectly, the body needs to take over and heal the wound or it will eventually open again.", "Wound healing is more difﬁcult in the presence of saliva, but there is no way to keep it off of the wound."], "photo": null, "heading": "Fistula and Wound Infection"}, "04-13-09": {"chunks": ["Complications\nIf part of the incision opens, saliva can track into the incision and down into the neck. This is called a ﬁstula and can cause infection. If saliva is draining into blood vessels, it can cause them to clot or rupture, which can damage or kill a ﬂap.", "Usually, a ﬁstula does not occur until about 7-10 days after surgery.\n• If it does happen, we may simply watch it closely (if it is small) or it may require additional procedures to clean the wound, patch the leak or place new drains."], "photo": null, "heading": "Complications"}, "04-13-10": {"chunks": ["Flap Clotting\nA clot can form in the blood vessel feeding the ﬂap and kill the ﬂap. While this is not the type of clot that can go to your lungs or brain and be life threatening, it can necessitate more surgery. Luckily, this is rare and occurs in about 1 patient out of every 100 cases."], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Clotting"}, "04-13-11": {"chunks": ["Flap Clotting\nSpontaneous ﬂap clotting is most common the ﬁrst couple days after surgery. After this it is rare unless there is a ﬁstula or infection around the ﬂap vessels, so the risk increases again about a week after surgery, when ﬁstulas and infections tend to develop."], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Clotting"}, "04-13-12": {"chunks": ["Flap Clotting Risks\nThe risks may be higher if you have a condition that leads to blood clotting. ●Make sure your surgeon knows if you have ever had a blood clot in your legs or lungs or if blood clots run in your family. ●Clotting disorders include Factor V Leiden, Prothrombin", "mutation, Protein C or S deﬁciency, elevated homocysteine, dysﬁbrinogenemia, and others. These are all rare but very important to know about."], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Clotting Risks"}, "04-13-13": {"chunks": ["Flap Checks\nFlap checks are done to assess the blood ﬂow to the ﬂap and make sure the ﬂap is alive and healthy. A ﬂap check involves looking at the ﬂap skin to make sure it the appropriate color.\n• The skin should look like normal healthy skin.\n• If it is very dark or purple or extremely", "pale, this can be an indication of a problem."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-13.jpg", "heading": "Flap Checks"}, "04-13-14": {"chunks": ["Flap Checks\nA doppler stethoscope is also used to assess blood ﬂow to the ﬂap. This is a non-invasive way to listen to the pulsation of blood through the ﬂap. Sometimes we will need to scratch the ﬂap with a needle to assess the blood ﬂow to the ﬂap.", "When this is done you will not feel pain from the needle stick because the ﬂap has no sensation as it has been detached from all nerves."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-14.jpg", "heading": "Flap Checks"}, "04-13-15": {"chunks": ["After the Hospital and\nFollow Up"], "photo": null, "heading": "After the Hospital and"}, "04-13-16": {"chunks": ["Follow-up\nYou will follow up in your reconstructive surgeon’s ofﬁce 1 week after discharge, then 2-3 weeks after the initial post-op visit, then about 2-3 months later, followed by another 1-3 visits every 6 months. Visits may be more frequent if there are any wound care issues.", "Your cancer surgeon and other doctors will have different schedules."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-13-16.jpg", "heading": "Follow-up"}, "04-13-17": {"chunks": ["Medication Management\nIt is important to check in with your primary doctor and any specialists, such as cardiologist, pulmonologist, endocrinologist, after discharge from the hospital.", "Some of your routine medications may change while you are healing and dosing may be altered during and after the hospital admission."], "photo": null, "heading": "Medication Management"}, "04-13-18": {"chunks": ["Medication Management\nIt is very important to re-establish care with your normal medical doctors after discharge.", "As surgeons, we are not familiar with management of most of these medications and can provide minimal assistance with questions and concerns regarding management of blood pressure, diabetes, neurological conditions, and other medical problems."], "photo": null, "heading": "Medication Management"}, "04-14-002": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nBasic concepts, deﬁnitions, and background What to expect before, during, and after the hospital Reconstruction when bone is involved: upper and lower jaw (maxilla and mandible) Reconstruction of soft tissue (no bone is reconstructed) Flap surgery involving the inside of the mouth", "Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "04-14-003": {"chunks": ["Basic Concepts, Deﬁnitions,\nand Background to Flaps"], "photo": null, "heading": "Basic Concepts, Deﬁnitions,"}, "04-14-004": {"chunks": ["What is a ﬂap?\nA ﬂap is a piece of tissue used to ﬁx a wound or cover a missing area after surgery. A ﬂap is different from a graft because it has its own blood supply (a graft does not). Blood ﬂows into the ﬂap through an artery and out of the ﬂap through a vein to keep it alive."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-004.jpg", "heading": "What is a ﬂap?"}, "04-14-005": {"chunks": ["What can a ﬂap be made of?\nSoft tissue (skin, muscle, fat) Bone Both soft tissue and bone"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-005.jpg", "heading": "What can a ﬂap be made of?"}, "04-14-006": {"chunks": ["What is a graft?\nA graft is a piece of tissue used to repair or rebuild an area, but it does not have its own blood supply. A skin graft is very thin (like a piece of paper) and does not need blood vessels. It can use oxygen and nutrients from the tissue it is placed on.", "Because it is so thin, a skin graft is mostly used for surface repairs, not deep wounds."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-006.jpg", "heading": "What is a graft?"}, "04-14-007": {"chunks": ["Bone or Cartilage Grafts\nSometimes bone or cartilage can be used as a graft. Bone or cartilage grafts are small pieces without blood supply. They get oxygen and nutrients from the soft tissue around them."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-007.jpg", "heading": "Bone or Cartilage Grafts"}, "04-14-008": {"chunks": ["Free Flap\nA free ﬂap is taken from a distant body part (like the forearm or lower leg). Blood vessels are cut to move the ﬂap to the needed area. The ﬂap vessels are connected to blood vessels in the neck (artery to artery, vein to vein).", "This process is like a transplant, but since it comes from the patient’s own body, there is no risk of rejection and no anti-rejection medications are needed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-008.jpg", "heading": "Free Flap"}, "04-14-009": {"chunks": ["Pedicled Flap\nA pedicled ﬂap is taken from a nearby area (like the chest or shoulder). The pedicled ﬂap stays attached to its original blood vessels."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-009.jpg", "heading": "Pedicled Flap"}, "04-14-010": {"chunks": ["Key Differences Between Free Flaps and Pedicled Flaps\nA free ﬂap gives more freedom to shape and position the tissue. When bone is needed a free ﬂap is usually necessary (unless the piece of bone is very small)."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-010.jpg", "heading": "Key Differences Between Free Flaps and Pedicled Flaps"}, "04-14-011": {"chunks": ["Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft\nA skin graft may be too thin and not work well for deep wounds. Skin grafts in the mouth can cause scarring and limit tongue movement. Skin grafts cannot be placed directly on bone because bone does not provide enough blood supply."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-011.jpg", "heading": "Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft"}, "04-14-012": {"chunks": ["Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft\nGrafts may not heal well if the area has had or will have radiation therapy. A pedicled ﬂap may be too thick or not reach the area needing repair."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-012.jpg", "heading": "Situations When the Free Flap is a Better Option than the Pedicled Flap or Graft"}, "04-14-013": {"chunks": ["Flap Surgery Involving the\nInside of the Mouth"], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Surgery Involving the"}, "04-14-014": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Flap Surgery Involving the Inside of the Mouth or Throat\nHow does surgery affect eating, drinking, and breathing? The mouth and throat are important for eating, drinking, speaking, and breathing.", "Surgery in these areas can cause swelling, which may make it hard to breathe, sometimes requiring a temporary tracheostomy tube (a breathing tube in the neck). It can also affect the ability to swallow food and liquids, making a feeding tube necessary during healing."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Flap Surgery Involving the Inside of the Mouth or Throat"}, "04-14-015": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer When can I eat by mouth?\nThe flap (usually skin) is stitched into place to repair the surgical wound in the mouth. Stitches dissolve in about a month, but healing takes 2 to 3 weeks or longer. The wound must be fully sealed to prevent saliva from leaking into the wound. Your surgeon will decide when it is safe for you to start eating and drinking again."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-015.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer When can I eat by mouth?"}, "04-14-016": {"chunks": ["Can I swallow my own saliva while I am healing, or should I spit it out?\nYes, you can swallow your saliva. It is not recommended to constantly spit or suction it out, unless you cannot swallow. This could cause dehydration over time."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-016.jpg", "heading": "Can I swallow my own saliva while I am healing, or should I spit it out?"}, "04-14-017": {"chunks": ["Reconstruction When Bone is Involved\nLower Jaw (Mandible) Upper Jaw/ Midface (Maxilla) Fibula Flap Scapula (Shoulder Blade) Flap Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Reconstruction When Bone is Involved"}, "04-14-018": {"chunks": ["Lower Jaw (Mandible)\nReconstruction"], "photo": null, "heading": "Lower Jaw (Mandible)"}, "04-14-019": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction\nWhy is a free flap used to repair the lower jaw? Reconstructing the lower jaw usually requires a free flap because these cancers need radiation therapy after surgery to prevent the cancer from returning.", "Radiation would destroy a bone graft because the graft does not have a blood supply, but the free flap does have blood supply."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction"}, "04-14-020": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction\nAlso, a free flap is used when there is a gap in the bone bigger than about 5 cm (2 inches) because larger grafts may not survive.", "The bone-containing flaps that are commonly used for jaw reconstruction are the fibula, scapula, and a small amount from the radius/forearm flaps (AKA osteocutaneous radial forearm free flap)."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-020.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Lower Jaw (Mandible) Reconstruction Continued"}, "04-14-021": {"chunks": ["Which type of ﬂap will be used for the mandible? The ﬁbula ﬂap\nSurgeons usually use the ﬁbula ﬂap to rebuild the jaw. The ﬁbula is a strong bone in the lower leg that can be shaped to ﬁt the jaw. We also take some soft tissue (skin, fat, and fascia) from the leg to help with the reconstruction."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-021.jpg", "heading": "Which type of ﬂap will be used for the mandible? The ﬁbula ﬂap"}, "04-14-022": {"chunks": ["The Fibula Flap\nThe ﬁbula’s blood vessels are long, which helps position the new jaw correctly. Another beneﬁt is that while one surgeon removes the tumor, another can prepare the ﬁbula ﬂap at the same time. This can save 1 to 2 hours in surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-022.jpg", "heading": "The Fibula Flap Continued"}, "04-14-023": {"chunks": ["The Scapula Flap\nAnother option is the scapula ﬂap (from the shoulder blade). However, since it's on the back, it takes more time in surgery because surgeons can’t work on it at the same time as tumor removal.", "The scapula ﬂap is often used if the lower leg isn’t healthy enough for a ﬁbula ﬂap. This is because leg arteries can have more blockage (atherosclerosis), while the scapula’s artery is usually healthier."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-023.jpg", "heading": "The Scapula Flap"}, "04-14-024": {"chunks": ["The Scapula Flap\nUnlike the ﬁbula ﬂap, the scapula ﬂap doesn’t need a special scan (CT angiogram) to check blood ﬂow because the artery for the scapula ﬂap is not vital to any other body part.", "The scapula ﬂap is also a better choice when a lot of soft tissue is needed, as it provides more skin and muscle than the ﬁbula."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-024.jpg", "heading": "The Scapula Flap Continued"}, "04-14-025": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Other Options for Jaw Reconstruction- the Radial Forearm Flap\nAnother option is the radial forearm flap (from the inner forearm). This flap includes a thin piece of bone, so it’s not usually the first choice for the jaw. Instead, it’s often used for reconstructing the upper jaw, cheekbones, or eye sockets."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-025.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Other Options for Jaw Reconstruction- the Radial Forearm Flap"}, "04-14-026": {"chunks": ["How is the ﬂap bone connected to the jaw?\nA titanium plate is used to connect the pieces of bone together. This plate spans the gap across the mandible where the tumor was removed. The plate is screwed into the remaining parts of the mandible as well as the bone of the ﬂap after it is shaped to ﬁt the defect."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-026.jpg", "heading": "How is the ﬂap bone connected to the jaw?"}, "04-14-027": {"chunks": ["The Titanium Plate\nThe size and position of a mandible resection depends on the location of the tumor, but this is an example of a large reconstruction plate.", "Every resection is a little different. In this case, the entire chin is being removed. The bone that needs to be removed is determined by the cancer surgeon, based on the location and size of the tumor. Click here to return to forearm ﬂap slide 81"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-027.jpg", "heading": "The Titanium Plate Continued"}, "04-14-028": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Is the titanium plate safe?\nYes, the titanium plate is safe and stays in the body permanently for most patients. It does not cause allergies or reactions and cannot be rejected by the body."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-028.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Is the titanium plate safe?"}, "04-14-029": {"chunks": ["Is the titanium plate safe?\nThe titanium plate is not magnetic, so it won’t set off metal detectors and is safe for MRIs.", "Occasionally, the plate can become infected over time (rarely) and need to be removed. Another rare complication is when the plate becomes loose over time, requiring removal, or the plate breaks. These situations are rare."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-029.jpg", "heading": "Is the titanium plate safe? Continued"}, "04-14-030": {"chunks": ["Soft Tissue Repair in the Tongue, Cheek, or Under the Tongue\nIf there is a soft tissue defect in the tongue, cheek, or under the tongue (ﬂoor of the mouth) some skin or muscle from the ﬂap is sutured in place to patch the soft tissue defect. The sutures will all dissolve on their own."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-030.jpg", "heading": "Soft Tissue Repair in the Tongue, Cheek, or Under the Tongue"}, "04-14-031": {"chunks": ["Will I look like myself after mandible reconstruction surgery or will I be disﬁgured?\nFor most patients, the changes to appearance after mandible reconstruction are minimal. Signiﬁcant changes are rare and usually only happen if the tumor or wound affects the external skin or soft tissue of the face or neck. This is typically seen in very large tumors."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-031.jpg", "heading": "Will I look like myself after mandible reconstruction surgery or will I be disﬁgured?"}, "04-14-032": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?\nGenerally, this surgery is done through the inside of the mouth and an incision on the neck. It is not usually necessary to place any scars on the face unless the tumor is invading the skin of the face or neck (this is not common)."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-032.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?"}, "04-14-033": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?\nA neck incision is necessary to find blood vessels in the neck to re-establish blood flow to the flap. These neck incisions heal very well and leave a faint scar that is generally not obvious after it heals.", "Usually, lymph nodes need to be removed from the neck as part of the cancer surgery. The same neck incision is used for this as well."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Will I have visible scars?"}, "04-14-034": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Will my face shape change?\nThe shape of the reconstructed mandible is based on the shape of each individual patient’s jaw. The titanium plate is custom made to each patient’s anatomy.", "It is not possible to make the new jaw the exact same shape as the patient's natural jaw, however, the changes are usually subtle once healing is complete"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-034.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Will my face shape change?"}, "04-14-035": {"chunks": ["Swelling of the Face and Neck\nSwelling of the face and neck is normal and expected after surgery. Usually, this swelling is the worst 3 days after the surgery and then gradually improves over the next several weeks to months. Swelling may come and go at times- this is normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-035.jpg", "heading": "Swelling of the Face and Neck"}, "04-14-036": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer When is swelling the worst?\nSometimes patients find that swelling is worse in the morning when they wake up – because they have been lying down all night. If this is the case, sleep with your head elevated on extra pillows, or even in a reclining chair if necessary. Sometimes patients notice swelling increases throughout the day, and it is better in the morning."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-036.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer When is swelling the worst?"}, "04-14-037": {"chunks": ["When should I be worried about swelling?\nIf swelling is gradually worsening over several consecutive days, this may be a cause for concern, especially if there is also increased pain, redness of the neck or face, or drainage of pus from incisions or drain tubes.", "The presence of fevers and/or chills is a warning sign as well. Talk to your surgical team."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-037.jpg", "heading": "When should I be worried about swelling?"}, "04-14-038": {"chunks": ["Is this surgery painful?\nThis type of facial surgery is generally less painful than most patients expect. There is certainly some pain, but it is usually well controlled with pain medicine.", "There may be some pain that comes and goes. Pain may be worse at certain times of day and some days may be worse than others. This is normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-038.jpg", "heading": "Is this surgery painful?"}, "04-14-039": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer When is pain concerning?\nPain is worrisome if it is continually increasing over several days. Especially if there is also fevers/chills, swelling that is gradually increasing over several days, or any new drainage from any incisions."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-039.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer When is pain concerning?"}, "04-14-040": {"chunks": ["Upper Jaw / Midface\nReconstruction"], "photo": null, "heading": "Upper Jaw / Midface"}, "04-14-041": {"chunks": ["Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction\nMaxillary reconstruction is similar to mandible reconstruction in many ways. The bones are smaller than the mandible, so while titanium plates are still used to join the bone segments together, these are smaller than the plates used for mandible reconstruction.", "Plates are still usually custom made for each individual patient."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-041.jpg", "heading": "Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction"}, "04-14-042": {"chunks": ["Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction\nOther than these differences, the details of the mandible reconstruction section above apply to patients who are undergoing maxillary reconstruction."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-042.jpg", "heading": "Upper Jaw/Midface (Maxilla) Reconstruction Continued"}, "04-14-044": {"chunks": ["The Fibula Flap. How is the ﬁbula used for reconstruction?\nThe ﬁbula is the smaller of the two lower leg bones (the larger bone is the tibia). It can be safely removed and used for reconstruction. The central part of the ﬁbula is removed, saving the bottom 3-4 inches by the ankle, and a similar length by the knee."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-044.jpg", "heading": "The Fibula Flap. How is the ﬁbula used for reconstruction?"}, "04-14-045": {"chunks": ["Will this affect blood ﬂow to my leg?\nThe lower leg has 3 arteries that carry blood down to the foot. The artery called the peroneal artery supplies the ﬁbula ﬂap. Because the leg has 3 main arteries, and if the other two arteries are healthy, blood ﬂow to the foot remains normal after surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-045.jpg", "heading": "Will this affect blood ﬂow to my leg?"}, "04-14-046": {"chunks": ["How do we make sure it’s safe?\nWe will check your pulses on your foot and order a scan called a CT Angiogram, which examines the arteries in the legs. We do this to make sure that it is safe to take the peroneal vessels supplying the ﬁbula ﬂap without hurting the circulation to the foot."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-046.jpg", "heading": "How do we make sure it’s safe?"}, "04-14-047": {"chunks": ["What will my leg look like after surgery?\nIf no skin is needed for reconstruction, you will have a in scar, in a straight line, running down the outside of the calf, from below the knee to above the ankle. The example on the right shows a scar after two weeks of healing—this is not the ﬁnal result."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-047.jpg", "heading": "What will my leg look like after surgery?"}, "04-14-048": {"chunks": ["What happens if skin is needed for reconstruction?\nIf skin is necessary for reconstruction, the resulting skin wound usually cannot be closed. This leaves a shallow, open wound, with exposed muscle, after the ﬂap is taken.", "To repair this, we may take a skin graft from the thigh. This skin graft is paper thin and is used to patch the leg wound. It will always look somewhat like a patch, but the color will even out over time, and the scars will fade and become smooth."], "photo": null, "heading": "What happens if skin is needed for reconstruction?"}, "04-14-049": {"chunks": ["What are my other options for a skin graft?\nMore commonly, we are now using a skin substitute called biodegradable tissue matrix (BTM), rather than a skin graft for ﬁbula donor sites. This is an artiﬁcial skin substitute that helps the wound heal.", "There is a thin plastic layer covering the BTM. The surgeon will take this off in the clinic. If you notice it falling off, do not be alarmed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-049.jpg", "heading": "What are my other options for a skin graft?"}, "04-14-050": {"chunks": ["How does BTM work?\nThe BTM forms a layer of healing tissue called granulation tissue over the wound bed. Once this (granulation tissue) has formed, the body forms skin over the BTM naturally."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-050.jpg", "heading": "How does BTM work?"}, "04-14-051": {"chunks": ["How long will this healing process take?\nThis process can take several months, depending on the size of the defect. This is an example of what it may look like when it's healed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-051.jpg", "heading": "How long will this healing process take?"}, "04-14-052": {"chunks": ["What to expect after surgery— drain tube\nThere will be a plastic surgical drain tube placed in the wound at the time of surgery. This is usually removed before you leave the hospital. Drain tube"], "photo": null, "heading": "What to expect after surgery— drain tube"}, "04-14-053": {"chunks": ["What to expect after surgery– wound VAC\nThere will also be a wound VAC placed on the donor site (the are the skin is taken from) in the hospital. Patients rarely go home with a wound VAC, so you don’t need to learn to manage it. *We have additional information on the wound VAC elsewhere"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-053.jpg", "heading": "What to expect after surgery– wound VAC"}, "04-14-054": {"chunks": ["When can I walk?\nYou can walk and bear weight immediately after surgery, with assistance. Immediately after surgery, patients have many monitors, drains, and IV lines attached to them-- it is easy to trip and fall.", "You will need to be helped when getting out of bed while you are in the hospital, especially while in the ICU."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-054.jpg", "heading": "When can I walk?"}, "04-14-055": {"chunks": ["Will I have pain in my leg?\nThe pain from the ﬁbula ﬂap is usually less severe than people expect. Usually, it is more bothersome after patients start walking more at home, and it can linger for weeks or even months."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-055.jpg", "heading": "Will I have pain in my leg?"}, "04-14-056": {"chunks": ["How long will I have pain in my leg?\nThis pain resolves with time, but sometimes it can take months or a year. Usually, the pain is mild soreness and lasts about 6 months. It is like having a sprained ankle"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-056.jpg", "heading": "How long will I have pain in my leg?"}, "04-14-057": {"chunks": ["Will I need physical therapy?\nPhysical therapy is sometimes needed, but not always. Generally, healthy, active people regain full mobility more quickly than patients who have a lower baseline level of mobility and activity prior to surgery.", "Usually, active, healthy patients can walk without assistance (meaning without a cane or walker) in a few weeks."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-057.jpg", "heading": "Will I need physical therapy?"}, "04-14-058": {"chunks": ["Will the surgery affect my walking?\nThe ﬁbula bone functions to provide stability to the ankle, in addition to the many ligaments of the ankle and foot that bind the different bones together. When the ﬁbula is removed, extra stress is placed on these ligaments.", "Over time, the ligaments strengthen and compensate for the loss of the ﬁbula. Until this happens, there will be some discomfort and instability."], "photo": null, "heading": "Will the surgery affect my walking?"}, "04-14-059": {"chunks": ["Will I have have mobility issues?\nPatients who have problems with strength or mobility pre-operatively may require weeks or months of using a walker or cane, along with physical therapy. This is not usually expected, but it is a possibility to prepare for.", "It is very unusual to have any permanent mobility problems after a ﬁbula ﬂap, but healing can be slow."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-059.jpg", "heading": "Will I have have mobility issues?"}, "04-14-060": {"chunks": ["Is leg swelling normal?\nSwelling in the ﬁbula ﬂap donor leg is quite common. If you have any swelling in the legs before surgery, it is very likely you will have more severe swelling in the donor leg after surgery. This is usually temporary"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-060.jpg", "heading": "Is leg swelling normal?"}, "04-14-061": {"chunks": ["What can I do to help the swelling go down?\nYou can use an ACE wrap or a compression stocking to help with this."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-061.jpg", "heading": "What can I do to help the swelling go down?"}, "04-14-062": {"chunks": ["How do I use the ACE wrap?\nMake sure to wrap the leg starting at the toes, around the foot, and ankle. The goal is to provide more compression to the foot and ankle than to the calf.", "Wrapping the leg without wrapping the ankle will lead to worsened swelling because ﬂuid will be trapped below the compression, in the ankle. *We have a leg dressing guide that provides more instructions on how to wrap the leg appropriately"], "photo": null, "heading": "How do I use the ACE wrap?"}, "04-14-063": {"chunks": ["Scapula (Shoulder Blade)\nFlap"], "photo": null, "heading": "Scapula (Shoulder Blade)"}, "04-14-064": {"chunks": ["Is the whole scapula used?\nWhen the scapula is needed for reconstruction, it is not removed in its entirety. Only the edge of the bone or the tip of the scapula is taken, leaving most of the scapula intact."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-064.jpg", "heading": "Is the whole scapula used?"}, "04-14-065": {"chunks": ["What will the incision look like?\nThe incision will run along the ﬂank, from the axilla (under-arm) down about 10 inches or so. The donor site wound can usually be closed completely, without a skin graft."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-065.jpg", "heading": "What will the incision look like?"}, "04-14-066": {"chunks": ["Will my shoulder function be affected?\nIt is rare to have permanent major functional problems from a scapula ﬂap. There will certainly be some soreness and stiffness, which may last several months. If necessary, we will arrange for physical therapy."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-066.jpg", "heading": "Will my shoulder function be affected?"}, "04-14-067": {"chunks": ["How sore or stiff will I feel after?\nThe degree of soreness and stiffness depends on the amount of tissue that is needed for the reconstruction. Smaller tumors and smaller ﬂaps lead to less issues with recovery. Larger surgeries can have more lasting stiffness and soreness."], "photo": null, "heading": "How sore or stiff will I feel after?"}, "04-14-068": {"chunks": ["Will I have drain tubes?\nTwo rubber drainage tubes are left in the ﬂank at the time of surgery to drain ﬂuid that accumulates in the surgical wound. Usually, one drain is removed after about one week and the second usually stays with the patient after they are discharged home. drain tube"], "photo": null, "heading": "Will I have drain tubes?"}, "04-14-069": {"chunks": ["How do I take care of my drain?\nThe drain needs to be emptied twice daily (or more depending on the amount of drainage) and the output needs to be recorded. The surgeon will want to know the daily output of the drain in milliliters (ML) for each 24-hour period.", "When the drain output is less than 50 ML per 24-hour period, it may be ready to be removed."], "photo": null, "heading": "How do I take care of my drain?"}, "04-14-070": {"chunks": ["Will ﬂuid accumulate after the drain tube is removed?\nOccasionally, ﬂuid continues to accumulate even after the drain is removed. This can lead to swelling of the skin that feels soft, like a water balloon. When you stand up the swelling will drift down to the lowest portion of the surgical site."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-070.jpg", "heading": "Will ﬂuid accumulate after the drain tube is removed?"}, "04-14-071": {"chunks": ["What should I do if ﬂuid accumulates after the drain is removed?\nThis ﬂuid (called a seroma) usually needs to be drained but the procedure is easy and usually done in the ofﬁce.", "The skin overlying the area is usually numb from the surgery and the ﬂuid can be drained through a small needle inserted through the skin. Sometimes this can require several drainage procedures if the ﬂuid keeps reaccumulating."], "photo": null, "heading": "What should I do if ﬂuid accumulates after the drain is removed?"}, "04-14-072": {"chunks": ["How long can I wait to get the ﬂuid drained?\nThis is not urgent – you do not need to come in the same day for this problem. Waiting even a week or two will be okay"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-072.jpg", "heading": "How long can I wait to get the ﬂuid drained?"}, "04-14-073": {"chunks": ["What wound care do I need to do for the scapula ﬂap?\nAside from drain care, all you need to do is apply petroleum jelly (Vaseline or Aquaphor) to the incision several times a day. This is to keep it from scabbing or drying out."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-073.jpg", "heading": "What wound care do I need to do for the scapula ﬂap?"}, "04-14-074": {"chunks": ["Reconstruction of Soft Tissue (No Bone is Reconstructed)\nRegional Reconstruction Skin Graft Forearm Flap Thigh Flap Click on the box to jump to Local Reconstruction each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Reconstruction of Soft Tissue (No Bone is Reconstructed)"}, "04-14-076": {"chunks": ["What is a forearm ﬂap?\nThe forearm ﬂap consists of skin from the inner surface of the forearm, above the wrist."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-076.jpg", "heading": "What is a forearm ﬂap?"}, "04-14-077": {"chunks": ["How does the hand receive blood after surgery?\nThe radial artery is the blood vessel that supplies blood ﬂow to the forearm ﬂap, and it is removed with the ﬂap during surgery. Once this is removed, the ulnar artery is the only vessel supplying blood to the hand."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-077.jpg", "heading": "How does the hand receive blood after surgery?"}, "04-14-078": {"chunks": ["How do we make sure its safe?\nTo ensure the hand will be safe and have sufﬁcient blood ﬂow after the ﬂap is taken, we do a simple, non-invasive test during the clinic examination. It is extremely rare to have an injury to the hand if this test looks good.", "If there is doubt as to the safety of performing a radial forearm ﬂap, we will choose a different type of ﬂap instead."], "photo": null, "heading": "How do we make sure its safe?"}, "04-14-079": {"chunks": ["Is any bone taken from my arm?\nOccasionally, bone is needed from the radius (forearm bone). This is not typical, and unless your surgeon has discussed this with you ahead of time it is extremely unlikely."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-079.jpg", "heading": "Is any bone taken from my arm?"}, "04-14-080": {"chunks": ["What about if bone is taken from my arm?\nIf bone is taken from this location, the part taken doesn't go through the whole bone. A partial thickness shaving is taken from the inner surface (About 40-50%).", "To strengthen the area, a metal plate is placed across the section where part of the bone was removed and held in place with screws."], "photo": null, "heading": "What about if bone is taken from my arm?"}, "04-14-081": {"chunks": ["What is the plate made of?\nThe plate is made of titanium (like the mandible plate described in the lower jaw reconstruction section). Click here to go to the mandible plate slide Titanium does not react with the body or cause allergies.", "It is not magnetic, so it is MRI compatible and does not set off metal detectors."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-081.jpg", "heading": "What is the plate made of?"}, "04-14-082": {"chunks": ["Will I have hand weakness?\nThis procedure does cause some temporary weakness of the hand, and it can take months to regain a strong grip. This is normal and physical therapy can help."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-082.jpg", "heading": "Will I have hand weakness?"}, "04-14-083": {"chunks": ["Wound Healing– Forearm\nUsually, the forearm donor site cannot be closed, and it is necessary to skin graft the forearm. To help the skin graft heal, a special dressing, called a wound VAC, is placed over it for 6 days. Once this is removed, there is a light dressing over the skin graft that you will change daily.", "*We have a separate information packet on wound care for the donor site."], "photo": null, "heading": "Wound Healing– Forearm"}, "04-14-084": {"chunks": ["What happens if part of the graft doesn’t survive?\nOccasionally, some part of the skin graft does not survive. This can lead to muscle or tendon exposure in the area on the forearm where the ﬂap was taken. Generally, these issues heal without further surgery Image to the left is a healed forearm graft"], "photo": null, "heading": "What happens if part of the graft doesn’t survive?"}, "04-14-085": {"chunks": ["How do I care for my forearm donor site?\nWe have a separate information packet on wound care for the donor site."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-085.jpg", "heading": "How do I care for my forearm donor site?"}, "04-14-086": {"chunks": ["How do I take care of the wound?\nManagement involves preventing the wound from drying out—Vaseline and a dressing that covers the wound are critical. If, during your care, you experience these wound issues, you will be provided with speciﬁc wound care instructions.", "Again, this type of wound almost always heals on its own and is not a major concern."], "photo": null, "heading": "How do I take care of the wound?"}, "04-14-088": {"chunks": ["Where is the thigh ﬂap taken from?\nThe ﬂap is taken from the outside of the thigh. The thigh ﬂap is based on a blood vessel that is not critical to the function of the leg. There is no test necessary to check this blood vessel (in contrast to the ﬁbula ﬂap and forearm ﬂap)."], "photo": null, "heading": "Where is the thigh ﬂap taken from?"}, "04-14-089": {"chunks": ["Will I need a skin graft?\nSometimes small openings develop along the incision line over the ﬁrst couple weeks. This can generally be treated with dressings and wound care—it rarely requires additional surgery. If this happens, your surgeon will provide speciﬁc instructions on how to care for it."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-089.jpg", "heading": "Will I need a skin graft?"}, "04-14-090": {"chunks": ["How long will a drain be in my leg?\nA drainage tube is left in the thigh at the time of surgery. This tube is about ¼\" thick and is necessary to drain ﬂuid that accumulates in the thigh because of surgery. Usually, the drain stays in place for a couple of weeks and patients go home with this leg drain."], "photo": null, "heading": "How long will a drain be in my leg?"}, "04-14-091": {"chunks": ["Drain Care\nThe nurses in the hospital will teach you how to care for your drain, which is an easy process. ●The drain needs to be emptied twice daily (or more frequently depending on the amount of drainage) and the output needs to be recorded. ●Your surgeon will want to know the daily", "output of the drain in milliliters (mL) for each 24-hour period."], "photo": null, "heading": "Drain Care"}, "04-14-092": {"chunks": ["When can I get my drain removed?\nWhen the drain output is less than 30-50 ML per 24-hour period, it may be ready to be removed."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-092.jpg", "heading": "When can I get my drain removed?"}, "04-14-093": {"chunks": ["What if there is still ﬂuid post-drain removal?\nOccasionally, ﬂuid continues to accumulate even after the drain is removed. This can lead to swelling of the leg that feels soft, like a water balloon. When you stand up the swelling will move down to above the knee.", "This ﬂuid (called a seroma) usually needs to be drained, but the procedure is easy and done in the ofﬁce."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-093.jpg", "heading": "What if there is still ﬂuid post-drain removal?"}, "04-14-094": {"chunks": ["How will the ﬂuid (seroma) be drained?\nThe skin overlying the area is usually numb from the surgery and the ﬂuid can be drained through a small needle inserted through the skin. Sometimes this can require several drainage procedures if the ﬂuid keeps reaccumulating.", "This is not urgent – you do not need to come in the same day for this problem. Waiting even a week or two will be ok. The procedure is very minor and not bothersome for patients."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-094.jpg", "heading": "How will the ﬂuid (seroma) be drained?"}, "04-14-095": {"chunks": ["What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?\nAside from drain care, all you need to do is apply Vaseline or Aquaphor to the incision several times a day to keep it from scabbing or drying out."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-095.jpg", "heading": "What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?"}, "04-14-096": {"chunks": ["What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?\nWrap your thigh with an ACE bandage, so that compression is applied to the leg.\n• This will help reduce the amount of ﬂuid draining from the thigh.\n• Avoid excessive pressure causing pain or knee swelling."], "photo": null, "heading": "What wound care do I need to do for the thigh ﬂap?"}, "04-14-097": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer How long do I need to wrap my thigh?\nContinue using the ACE wrap for 1 month after surgery, even after the drain is removed. ●Wrap as much of the thigh as possible. ●Another option is a full leg compression stocking."], "photo": null, "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer How long do I need to wrap my thigh?"}, "04-14-099": {"chunks": ["What are skin grafts?\n●A thin sheet of skin shaved from one area and placed onto a wound somewhere else. ●It is used for more surface-level wounds when the underlying tissue is healthy to support healing. ●Take 2-3 weeks to fully heal. ○Monitor both the donor and graft sites for infection"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-099.jpg", "heading": "What are skin grafts?"}, "04-14-101": {"chunks": ["What is local reconstruction?\n●This procedure uses tissue from around the surgical site (nearby skin or cells) to ﬁll a defect. ●Generally a shorter surgery and quicker recovery ○Mild bruising and swelling is common"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-101.jpg", "heading": "What is local reconstruction?"}, "04-14-103": {"chunks": ["What is regional reconstruction?\n• This procedure uses tissue from a nearby region (such as the chest muscle) which is rotated into the defect area.\n• This is ideal for larger defects and is more robust than a local reconstruction.", "○ It may leave a visible scar on the chest or neck. ○ Drains may be in place to prevent buildup of ﬂuid."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-103.jpg", "heading": "What is regional reconstruction?"}, "04-14-104": {"chunks": ["What to expect before, during, and after the hospital\nFitness and Preparation for Surgery What to expect in the hospital After the hospital and follow up Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-104.jpg", "heading": "What to expect before, during, and after the hospital"}, "04-14-105": {"chunks": ["Other Questions About Fitness and Preparation for\nSurgery"], "photo": null, "heading": "Other Questions About Fitness and Preparation for"}, "04-14-106": {"chunks": ["Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?\nReconstructive surgery can be lengthy, especially when combined with surgery for tumor removal. This certainly does place stress on the body."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-106.jpg", "heading": "Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?"}, "04-14-107": {"chunks": ["Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?\nYour primary medical doctor (PCP) will be asked to provide a clearance note for you to undergo anesthesia/surgery.", "If you have other medical problems, such as heart, lung, liver or kidney problems, a note may be needed from additional specialists, such as cardiologist, or pulmonologist, if applicable."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-107.jpg", "heading": "Am I healthy enough to have this surgery?"}, "04-14-108": {"chunks": ["Am I too old to have this surgery?\nMost of our patients are in their 50’s-80’s. Age itself does not lead to complications and the overall health and functional status of the patient are more important indicators of ﬁtness and risk."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-108.jpg", "heading": "Am I too old to have this surgery?"}, "04-14-109": {"chunks": ["Am I too old to have this surgery?\nSome patients are in their 80’s and are quite healthy. Others may be younger and have signiﬁcant medical problems that make surgery riskier.", "The decision as to whether anesthesia and surgery are safe for a patient is made by a multidisciplinary team of surgeons, anesthesia personnel, and other specialists."], "photo": null, "heading": "Am I too old to have this surgery?"}, "04-14-110": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer What can I do to prepare for surgery?\nThe most important thing leading up to surgery is to maintain good nutrition to support wound healing and to avoid any activities that worsen wound healing."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-110.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer What can I do to prepare for surgery?"}, "04-14-111": {"chunks": ["Good nutrition requires protein.\nProtein intake should be around 1.25-1.5 g of protein per kilogram of bodyweight per day. Supplementing your diet with Boost, Ensure, or other protein supplements and vitamins is good, especially if you have difﬁculty eating preoperatively.", "Excessive protein intake is not healthy, so it is a good idea to work with a dietician to assist with nutrition."], "photo": null, "heading": "Good nutrition requires protein."}, "04-14-112": {"chunks": ["Supplements\nOne regular daily multivitamin is sufﬁcient and there is no need to take additional supplements unless speciﬁcally instructed to by your doctor. Some herbal supplements can be harmful. Discuss any of these with your surgeon before taking."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-112.jpg", "heading": "Supplements"}, "04-14-113": {"chunks": ["Diabetes\nIf you have diabetes, make sure you are taking all your medications as prescribed by your primary doctor. Avoid excessively fatty and sugary foods."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-113.jpg", "heading": "Diabetes"}, "04-14-114": {"chunks": ["Diabetes\nUncontrolled diabetes will compromise your body’s ability to heal. ●If you're not sure your medicine is right for you, talk to your primary doctor as soon as possible to make sure your diabetes is under control before surgery. ●Also discuss any changes after", "discharge from the hospital with your primary doctor."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-114.jpg", "heading": "Diabetes"}, "04-14-115": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nIt is important to stop tobacco products (smoking, vaping, dipping, cigars, pipes, etc.) at least three weeks before surgery. Smoking impairs wound healing and increases the risk of postoperative complications."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-115.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-14-116": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nHeavy alcohol use can also cause postoperative complications If you have more than one or two drinks per day, stopping all consumption of alcohol “cold turkey” can lead to withdrawal symptoms.\n• It is best to wean yourself down over the course of a couple weeks."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-116.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-14-117": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nIf you are drinking daily before surgery, please let us know. You may require medication to help prevent alcohol withdrawal while you’re in the hospital. This is critical information for appropriate anesthesia and postoperative medications."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-117.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-14-118": {"chunks": ["Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery\nSometimes it is difﬁcult for patients to be honest with their healthcare team regarding their use of alcohol, tobacco, or other products. It is important to be forthcoming with this information so that we can make sure you have the most appropriate care and best outcome."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-118.jpg", "heading": "Alcohol and tobacco use prior to surgery"}, "04-14-119": {"chunks": ["What to Expect in the\nHospital"], "photo": null, "heading": "What to Expect in the"}, "04-14-120": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery - Arrival\nYou will be asked to come to the hospital early in the morning. The hospital will call you the evening before surgery to tell you where to go and when to arrive. Usually, they want you to come in around 5:30 AM.\n• This is to prepare you for anesthesia.", "You will see the surgeons and anesthesiologists before going back to the operating room."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-120.jpg", "heading": "Day of Surgery - Arrival"}, "04-14-121": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery – Start of Surgery\nOnce in the operating room you will undergo anesthesia. This takes 15-20 minutes. Then once you are asleep, we will prepare you for the surgery by padding and positioning your body, inserting a catheter into the bladder, and inserting several more IV lines.", "Then the surgical site will be cleaned with antiseptics."], "photo": null, "heading": "Day of Surgery – Start of Surgery"}, "04-14-122": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery – During Surgery\nBy the time we start the operation, it will be around 8:30. Depending on the surgery, it may be anywhere from 5 to 12 or more hours.", "A designated family member or friend will receive updates from a nurse in the operating room. The surgeons will talk to family/friends when they have completed their portions of the procedure."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-122.jpg", "heading": "Day of Surgery – During Surgery"}, "04-14-123": {"chunks": ["Day of Surgery – After Surgery\nAfter the procedure is completed, you will be transferred to the ICU and will wake up slowly. You may not remember anything from the night immediately following surgery.", "You may have a tracheostomy tube and feeding tube in place (if your surgeon discussed this with you), as well as several surgical drains in the neck and the area where the ﬂap was taken."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-123.jpg", "heading": "Day of Surgery – After Surgery"}, "04-14-124": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Hospital Stay\nYou will be in the hospital for about 10 days after free ﬂap surgery. Sometimes this is longer or shorter depending on the type of surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-124.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Hospital Stay"}, "04-14-125": {"chunks": ["Why do I have to stay for ~10 days?\nThe main purpose of the length of stay is to make sure the surgical sites are healing and that the ﬂap is healthy. Wound healing problems often do not arise until about 1 week after surgery. This is the main reason patients remain in the hospital so long."], "photo": null, "heading": "Why do I have to stay for ~10 days?"}, "04-14-126": {"chunks": ["Hospital Stay - ICU\nThe ﬁrst 3 days will be in the ICU. This is not because you will be sick or unstable, but simply for close monitoring. After this you will go to the regular ﬂoor."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-126.jpg", "heading": "Hospital Stay - ICU"}, "04-14-127": {"chunks": ["Hospital Stay – Care\nYou will receive physical therapy in the hospital. This becomes more in-depth after you leave the ICU. You will be helped out of bed to a chair the ﬁrst day after surgery and your activity will be increased from there.", "You will receive teaching regarding any wound care, tracheostomy care, feeding tube, or drain care needs."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-127.jpg", "heading": "Hospital Stay – Care"}, "04-14-128": {"chunks": ["Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?\nMost patients can go home after they leave the hospital. ●But if a patient doesn’t have anyone to help them at home, we might arrange for them to stay at a skilled nursing facility until they can manage on their own."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-128.jpg", "heading": "Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?"}, "04-14-129": {"chunks": ["Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?\nPhysical therapists usually decide if a patient needs to go to a rehab center after leaving the hospital. ●They will check on you several times while you're in the hospital, and if they think you need rehab, we will start making plans for your stay."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-129.jpg", "heading": "Rehab/Nursing Facility/ \"Can I go home after the hospital?"}, "04-14-130": {"chunks": ["Fistula and Wound Infection\nWhen you have surgery involving the mouth or throat, the wound is exposed to saliva. Even if the wounds are sutured perfectly, the body needs to take over and heal the wound or it will eventually open again.", "Wound healing is more difﬁcult in the presence of saliva, but there is no way to keep it off of the wound."], "photo": null, "heading": "Fistula and Wound Infection"}, "04-14-131": {"chunks": ["Complications\nIf part of the incision opens, saliva can track into the incision and down into the neck. This is called a ﬁstula and can cause infection. If saliva is draining into blood vessels, it can cause them to clot or rupture, which can damage or kill a ﬂap.", "Usually, a ﬁstula does not occur until about 7-10 days after surgery.\n• If it does happen, we may simply watch it closely (if it is small) or it may require additional procedures to clean the wound, patch the leak or place new drains."], "photo": null, "heading": "Complications"}, "04-14-132": {"chunks": ["Flap Clotting\nA clot can form in the blood vessel feeding the ﬂap and kill the ﬂap. While this is not the type of clot that can go to your lungs or brain and be life threatening, it can necessitate more surgery. Luckily, this is rare and occurs in about 1 patient out of every 100 cases."], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Clotting"}, "04-14-133": {"chunks": ["Flap Clotting\nSpontaneous ﬂap clotting is most common the ﬁrst couple days after surgery. After this it is rare unless there is a ﬁstula or infection around the ﬂap vessels, so the risk increases again about a week after surgery, when ﬁstulas and infections tend to develop."], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Clotting"}, "04-14-134": {"chunks": ["Flap Clotting Risks\nThe risks may be higher if you have a condition that leads to blood clotting. ●Make sure your surgeon knows if you have ever had a blood clot in your legs or lungs or if blood clots run in your family. ●Clotting disorders include Factor V Leiden, Prothrombin", "mutation, Protein C or S deﬁciency, elevated homocysteine, dysﬁbrinogenemia, and others. These are all rare but very important to know about."], "photo": null, "heading": "Flap Clotting Risks"}, "04-14-135": {"chunks": ["Flap Checks\nFlap checks are done to assess the blood ﬂow to the ﬂap and make sure the ﬂap is alive and healthy. A ﬂap check involves looking at the ﬂap skin to make sure it the appropriate color.\n• The skin should look like normal healthy skin.\n• If it is very dark or purple or extremely", "pale, this can be an indication of a problem."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-135.jpg", "heading": "Flap Checks"}, "04-14-136": {"chunks": ["Flap Checks\nA doppler stethoscope is also used to assess blood ﬂow to the ﬂap. This is a non-invasive way to listen to the pulsation of blood through the ﬂap. Sometimes we will need to scratch the ﬂap with a needle to assess the blood ﬂow to the ﬂap.", "When this is done you will not feel pain from the needle stick because the ﬂap has no sensation as it has been detached from all nerves."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-136.jpg", "heading": "Flap Checks"}, "04-14-137": {"chunks": ["After the Hospital and\nFollow Up"], "photo": null, "heading": "After the Hospital and"}, "04-14-138": {"chunks": ["Follow-up\nYou will follow up in your reconstructive surgeon’s ofﬁce 1 week after discharge, then 2-3 weeks after the initial post-op visit, then about 2-3 months later, followed by another 1-3 visits every 6 months. Visits may be more frequent if there are any wound care issues.", "Your cancer surgeon and other doctors will have different schedules."], "photo": "/sms-images/04-14-138.jpg", "heading": "Follow-up"}, "04-14-139": {"chunks": ["Medication Management\nIt is important to check in with your primary doctor and any specialists, such as cardiologist, pulmonologist, endocrinologist, after discharge from the hospital.", "Some of your routine medications may change while you are healing and dosing may be altered during and after the hospital admission."], "photo": null, "heading": "Medication Management"}, "04-14-140": {"chunks": ["Medication Management\nIt is very important to re-establish care with your normal medical doctors after discharge.", "As surgeons, we are not familiar with management of most of these medications and can provide minimal assistance with questions and concerns regarding management of blood pressure, diabetes, neurological conditions, and other medical problems."], "photo": null, "heading": "Medication Management"}, "05-01-03": {"chunks": ["What does smoking and alcohol have to do with head and neck cancer?\n• Smoking and heavy alcohol use have a exponential effect on developing head and neck cancer. This means that the effect is more than adding the impact of these two causes together."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-03.jpg", "heading": "What does smoking and alcohol have to do with head and neck cancer?"}, "05-01-04": {"chunks": ["Quitting Smoking at Least 3 Weeks Before Head and Neck Cancer Treatment:\nWhy and How to Do it"], "photo": null, "heading": "Quitting Smoking at Least 3 Weeks Before Head and Neck Cancer Treatment:"}, "05-01-05": {"chunks": ["So what should I do?\nWe recommend you quit smoking immediately after your cancer diagnosis and as many weeks as possible before the start of cancer treatment.", "Since quitting is hard, we recommend you get as much help as possible from your clinical team, which includes your surgeon, other oncologists, primary care doctor, nurse, social worker, and team members within the tobacco treatment program."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-05.jpg", "heading": "So what should I do?"}, "05-01-06": {"chunks": ["Why should I quit smoking immediately after a cancer diagnosis?\nSmoking before surgery increases the risk of wound healing problems and death around the time of surgery. This is because quitting smoking improves your body’s ability to heal and respond to treatment."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-06.jpg", "heading": "Why should I quit smoking immediately after a cancer diagnosis?"}, "05-01-07": {"chunks": ["Why should I quit smoking immediately after a cancer diagnosis?\nPatients who receive intensive smoking cessation treatment at least four weeks before surgery have a lower risk of surgical complications."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-07.jpg", "heading": "Why should I quit smoking immediately after a cancer diagnosis?"}, "05-01-08": {"chunks": ["Why should I quit smoking immediately after a cancer diagnosis?\nQuitting at the time of diagnosis may reduce the risk of your cancer returning after treatment. Quitting also reduces your risk that a second cancer will develop. Quitting smoking reduces your risk that your cancer will be fatal."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-08.jpg", "heading": "Why should I quit smoking immediately after a cancer diagnosis?"}, "05-01-09": {"chunks": ["So what should I do? How do I quit smoking?\nSupport to Stop Smoking is Available:\n• Regularly talking to an expert to help you quit AND taking medication is the most effective form of treatment. Doing both are more effective than doing one or the other.", "• A person in expertise in helping patients quit smoking can help you identify triggers, develop coping strategies and provide emotional support.\n• FDA-approved medications help address cravings as well as withdrawal and mood symptoms."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-09.jpg", "heading": "So what should I do? How do I quit smoking?"}, "05-01-10": {"chunks": ["If you’re interested in quitting smoking:\n• Ask your doctor for a referral to the UTSW nicotine cessation program.\n• Ask your doctor for a prescription for medications to help you quit."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-10.jpg", "heading": "If you’re interested in quitting smoking:"}, "05-01-11": {"chunks": ["What to Expect When Quitting Smoking\nSmoking affects several key chemicals called neurotransmitters in the brain:\n• Creating alertness\n• Increasing energy\n• Elevating mood Quitting can lead to feelings of anxiety and low mood/depression as the brain adjusts to a change in its chemistry.", "Medications can help re-balance these neurotransmitters without the harmful side-effects of smoking."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-11.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect When Quitting Smoking"}, "05-01-12": {"chunks": ["What to Expect When Quitting Smoking\nQuitting is hard but your clinical team is here to support you. Even so called “failures” are successes – there is a good chance you can quit long-term if you are able to stay off cigarettes for one week."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-12.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect When Quitting Smoking"}, "05-01-14": {"chunks": ["Reducing Your Alcohol Intake\nIf you drink heavily:\n• Notify your surgeon\n• Reduce your alcohol intake, ideally to sparingly, if at all\n• This means 1-2 drinks per week or less We do not know if there is a “safe” amount of alcohol intake for head and neck cancer survivors."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-14.jpg", "heading": "Reducing Your Alcohol Intake"}, "05-01-15": {"chunks": ["Detoxing From Alcohol\n• Sometimes a surgeon may recommend detoxing from alcohol before starting treatment.\n• Detoxing from alcohol can be life threatening and may require admission to the hospital.\n• In the hospital, they can help your withdrawal symptoms with medications, such as benzodiazepines."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-15.jpg", "heading": "Detoxing From Alcohol"}, "05-01-16": {"chunks": ["What to Expect When Quitting Alcohol\n• Alcohol acts as a depressant in the body, affecting heart rate, blood pressure and breathing.", "• Alcohol also interferes with the chemicals that affect our mood. Drinking can make you feel relaxed initially but later leads to feeling irritable and restless. Another drink repeats the cycle."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-16.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect When Quitting Alcohol"}, "05-01-17": {"chunks": ["Why should I decrease my alcohol use?\nContinued heavy alcohol use may lead to:\n• Lowered ability of your immune system to ﬁght cancer\n• Delayed surgery\n• Longer and more frequent hospitalizations\n• Longer recoveries from treatment"], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-17.jpg", "heading": "Why should I decrease my alcohol use?"}, "05-01-18": {"chunks": ["What are the beneﬁts to quitting alcohol?\nQuitting alcohol helps your body ﬁght cancer and reduces your risk of developing new cancers. Quitting alcohol also reduces the severity of treatment side effects, such as mucositis."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-18.jpg", "heading": "What are the beneﬁts to quitting alcohol?"}, "05-01-19": {"chunks": ["What to Expect When Quitting Alcohol\nSupport to abstain from alcohol is available:\n• We can refer you to a specialized psychiatrist to help quit alcohol by managing cravings and mood symptoms\n• Support from the clinic social worker to identify triggers, alternative coping skills and problem-solve barriers to quit"], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-19.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect When Quitting Alcohol"}, "05-01-22": {"chunks": ["Why Do I Need Proper Nutrition?\nProper nutrition plays an important role before and after your surgery. Proper nutrition:\n• Supports wound healing\n• Strengthens your immune system\n• Helps the body manage the stress of surgery", "Malnutrition can increase the risk of complications, prolonged hospital stays, and slower recovery"], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-22.jpg", "heading": "Why Do I Need Proper Nutrition?"}, "05-01-23": {"chunks": ["How do I maintain my nutrition status?\nA dietician is a healthcare professional that specializes in food and nutrition. Ask your provider to place a referral if you would like to be contacted or need help with any of the following listed below. Their goals are to help you make sure you are:", "• Consuming adequate protein\n• Maintaining your weight\n• Staying hydrated They can also help with:\n• Coping with potential nutrition-related side effects from treatment.\n• Tube feeding assistance. For more information, refer to the NG tube module."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-23.jpg", "heading": "How do I maintain my nutrition status?"}, "05-01-24": {"chunks": ["How do I maintain my nutrition status?\nProtein is essential for faster healing so it’s important to increase your protein intake both before and after surgery. Calories are equally important for the healing process as they help you maintain your weight.", "• Select foods that are both high in protein and calories to support recovery.", "• Drink 2-3 protein shakes a day for at least two weeks prior to surgery is recommended. Shakes that are high in protein and calories are best. For more information on speciﬁc brands, refer to the Dietician Orientation video at the start of this module."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-24.jpg", "heading": "How do I maintain my nutrition status?"}, "05-01-25": {"chunks": ["What are good sources of protein?\nDepending on tumor size and location, soft protein sources may be easier for you to eat before surgery. After surgery you will likely rely on soft foods, soups, and shakes for your protein needs.", "Eggs are a good source of protein. You can incorporate dishes like egg salad, quiche, and custard into your diet."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-25.jpg", "heading": "What are good sources of protein?"}, "05-01-26": {"chunks": ["What are good sources of protein?\nMeat is also a good source of protein: include meats such as chicken, pork, beef, and lamb to meet your protein needs.\n• If it is difﬁcult to chew or swallow meat, opt for shredded or ground meat with extra gravy, or choose casseroles and chicken salad for easier consumption.", "Fish is another great soft high protein food."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-26.jpg", "heading": "What are good sources of protein?"}, "05-01-27": {"chunks": ["What are good sources of protein?\nDairy products like greek yogurt, cottage cheese, and milk are also high in protein.\n• Choosing whole milk or chocolate milk can add extra calories, helping you maintain your weight.\n• For added calories and protein, you can prepare oatmeal with milk instead of water", "• Creamy or chunky soup like clam chowder, potato, cream of chicken, broccoli, or soups with beans are also a good option."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-27.jpg", "heading": "What are good sources of protein?"}, "05-01-28": {"chunks": ["What are good sources of protein?\nFor vegetarian protein sources, try:\n• Beans, lentils, or hummus (chickpeas)\n• Tofu\n• Soy milk or soy yogurt\n• Nut butters\n• Veggie burgers"], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-28.jpg", "heading": "What are good sources of protein?"}, "05-01-30": {"chunks": ["Why is it important to stay active before my surgery?\nStaying active before surgery can help your body prepare and recover more quickly. Generally, healthy, active people regain full mobility quicker than patients who have a lower baseline level of mobility and activity prior to surgery.", "Usually, active, healthy patients can walk without assistance (meaning without a cane or walker) in a few weeks."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-01-30.jpg", "heading": "Why is it important to stay active before my surgery?"}, "05-02-02": {"chunks": ["Preparing for Your Hospitalization After\nFree Flap Surgery and Tracheostomy"], "photo": null, "heading": "Preparing for Your Hospitalization After"}, "05-02-03": {"chunks": ["Bring a Caretaker\nIf possible, plan to have a family member or a loved one stay with you in the hospital for at least the ﬁrst few days after your surgery. It is helpful to have someone with you that knows you well and who can anticipate your needs to support you while you recover.", "You will have your own room with a private bathroom. There will also be a recliner or pull out bed so your support person can stay comfortably with you."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-03.jpg", "heading": "Bring a Caretaker"}, "05-02-04": {"chunks": ["What else should I bring?\nTo help you feel more at ease during your stay, we encourage you to bring a few comfort items and things to keep yourself entertained, such as:", "• Cozy socks or slippers\n• A favorite blanket\n• A sound machine or headphones\n• A tablet\n• Puzzles or games\n• Books or magazines\n• Pictures of loved ones"], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-04.jpg", "heading": "What else should I bring?"}, "05-02-05": {"chunks": ["What else should I bring?\nMost likely you will be unable to talk right after your surgery so it is a good idea to have a way to communicate your needs. While a caregiver can help, you can also use the following to help communicate:", "• A whiteboard and a marker (provided by the hospital)\n• Phone-based apps like a talk to text app or a notes app"], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-05.jpg", "heading": "What else should I bring?"}, "05-02-07": {"chunks": ["What To Expect When You Wake Up\nThe ﬁrst few days after surgery require the most adjustment as you grow accustomed to your trach and the changes with your surgery, the hospital setting and the close monitoring of your progress.", "Remember, you will adjust to these changes and your hospital team is there to support you."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-07.jpg", "heading": "What To Expect When You Wake Up"}, "05-02-08": {"chunks": ["What To Expect When You Wake Up\nWhen you wake up, you will be coming out of a major surgery and still feeling the effects of anesthesia. This is completely normal. You will be given pain medication to keep you comfortable but it may also make you feel groggy, sleepy, or a little confused at ﬁrst.", "You will likely rely on your caregivers or written or text-based forms of communication, at ﬁrst."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-08.jpg", "heading": "What To Expect When You Wake Up"}, "05-02-09": {"chunks": ["What To Expect When You Wake Up\nWhen you wake up from surgery, you will likely have several thing attached to your body, such as a tracheostomy, drains, tubes, or monitors.\n• These are all in place to help you heal and will be removed as you recover.", "It is helpful to remember that the ﬁrst few days after surgery can be the hardest to adjust to but your team is there to support you through this difﬁcult experience."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-09.jpg", "heading": "What To Expect When You Wake Up"}, "05-02-10": {"chunks": ["Will I be able to eat and drink?\nBecause your surgery involves the head and neck, you won't be able to eat or drink by mouth when you wake up. Your care team will ensure you receive nutrition in other ways until your surgeon determines that it is safe for you to resume eating and drinking again."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-10.jpg", "heading": "Will I be able to eat and drink?"}, "05-02-11": {"chunks": ["How independent will I be after surgery?\nAt the beginning, you will need help from the hospital staff to do most things--this is completely normal and expected. You may also need to rely on your caretaker during this time to communicate your needs.", "• Having someone who knows you well can be comforting and can help ensure your care team understands your wishes."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-11.jpg", "heading": "How independent will I be after surgery?"}, "05-02-12": {"chunks": ["How independent will I be after surgery?\nYou will be moving around and encouraged to get out of bed the ﬁrst few days with assistance. During your stay, you will gradually regain strength and be able to move around more on your own, but this won’t happen right away.", "Your care team will be there to support you every step of the way. This team includes physical and occupational therapy."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-12.jpg", "heading": "How independent will I be after surgery?"}, "05-02-14": {"chunks": ["What to Expect in the ICU\nWhen you ﬁrst wake up from the surgery, you will be placed in the Surgical Intensive Care Unit (SICU). In the SICU you will have your own room with glass doors that can be closed for privacy and to maintain a quiet environment.", "There is a lot of activity in the SICU and a lot of people coming to check on various aspects of your healing.\n• After your reconstruction, your team will be monitoring your new free ﬂap frequently to make sure your new tissue is healthy and healing."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-14.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect in the ICU"}, "05-02-15": {"chunks": ["What to Expect in the ICU\nThe ﬁrst few nights in the ICU may not provide quality restful sleep. It is important to ensure you have items that will make you as comfortable as you can be."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-15.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect in the ICU"}, "05-02-16": {"chunks": ["Comfort Care in the SICU\nIn the SICU, your pain medication will likely be given through a device called a Patient-Controlled Analgesia (PCA) pump also known as a “pain button”.\n• This device allows you to self-administer medication for pain relief by pressing a button.", "If you are tall or you ﬁnd that your feet touch the headboard, the hospital can provide a bed extender.\n• You can also ask you care team ahead of time if you think you might need one."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-16.jpg", "heading": "Comfort Care in the SICU"}, "05-02-17": {"chunks": ["Can I have visitors in the ICU?\nYes, you can have visitors in the ICU. Check with the front desk to ﬁnd out the visitor policy."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-17.jpg", "heading": "Can I have visitors in the ICU?"}, "05-02-19": {"chunks": ["What to Expect on the Recovery Floor\nAfter a few days, you will be transferred from the ICU to a recovery ﬂoor. Here you will have a private room (with a full bath) with doors that can be closed for privacy and to maintain a quiet environment. There is less activity here as well.", "• While your care team will still be monitoring your healing, it will be less frequent than in the ICU."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-19.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect on the Recovery Floor"}, "05-02-20": {"chunks": ["What to Expect on the Recovery Floor\nOn the ﬂoor, your pain medication will likely be given PRN which means as needed.\n• This means your pain medication will not be given at set times, but instead when you ask for it. This gives you more control over when you receive pain relief.", "• It is important to pay attention to how you are feeling and let your care team when you are feeling uncomfortable. Don’t wait until the pain is severe--your team is here to help manage your pain and keep you as comfortable as possible."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-20.jpg", "heading": "What to Expect on the Recovery Floor"}, "05-02-22": {"chunks": ["Planning for Discharge\nAs you get closer to leaving the hospital, a care coordinator will help ensure everything is in place for a smooth and safe discharge. They work closely with your doctors, nurses, and caregiver to:", "• Coordinate arrangements following your discharge including medical equipment, prescriptions, nutritional supplements.\n• Answer any questions you have about your discharge plan."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-22.jpg", "heading": "Planning for Discharge"}, "05-02-23": {"chunks": ["Where will I discharge to?\nWhen it's time to leave the hospital, your care team and care coordinator will talk with you and your loved ones to help choose the best option for your recovery. Common discharge options include:\n• Home with home health\n• To a nursing facility\n• To a rehabilitation facility", "More information about each of these discharge options will be provided on the next slides."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-23.jpg", "heading": "Where will I discharge to?"}, "05-02-24": {"chunks": ["Where will I discharge to?\nHome with home health:\n• You go home but have a home health provider visit you a few times a week to help with things like wound care and medications."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-24.jpg", "heading": "Where will I discharge to?"}, "05-02-25": {"chunks": ["Where will I discharge to?\nTo a rehabilitation facility:\n• You go to a rehab center for more intensive therapy, like physical or occupational therapy, to help you recover strength and function before returning home. To a skilled nursing facility:", "• You temporarily stay at a facility where you can get more hands-on medical care and help with daily activities until you are stronger."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-25.jpg", "heading": "Where will I discharge to?"}, "05-02-26": {"chunks": ["Planning for Discharge\nBefore you leave the hospital, your care team will help you schedule follow-up appointments with your treatment team before you discharge.\n• You will be coming back in about a week for a follow up appointment with your surgical oncologist and your reconstructive surgeon."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-26.jpg", "heading": "Planning for Discharge"}, "05-02-27": {"chunks": ["Planning for Discharge\nShould you go home with any medical equipment your hospital team will provide education on the items you will discharge with.\n• This may include things like tracheostomy training, wound vac training, wound care training, feeding tube training."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-27.jpg", "heading": "Planning for Discharge"}, "05-02-28": {"chunks": ["Support at Home\nIf possible, after you leave the hospital, having help from a caregiver or loved one will be very important. Caregivers are encouraged to reach out to the care coordinator if any questions arise.", "They can assist with daily tasks, help manage medications, and provide emotional support as you continue to recover at home.", "If you have any questions or concerns at any point, don’t hesitate to talk with your care team—they’re here to help and support you throughout your recovery. Additionally, caregivers can contact the care coordinator if they have any questions."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-02-28.jpg", "heading": "Support at Home"}, "05-03-2": {"chunks": ["What is a Modiﬁed Barium Swallow Study (MBS)?\nA modiﬁed barium swallow study is a video X-ray that shows how food and drink move through your mouth and throat when you swallow. It helps check how well your throat muscles are working and how food travels into the top part of your esophagus (food pipe). It speciﬁcally tests the safety and efﬁciency of your swallow."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-03-2.jpg", "heading": "What is a Modiﬁed Barium Swallow Study (MBS)?"}, "05-03-3": {"chunks": ["What is the Process of getting an MBS?\nDuring testing, you will be seated in an open x-ray machine. Video x-rays are recorded as you swallow liquid and bites of food. The food and drink you consume is tinted with barium, which allows it to be seen under an x-ray."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-03-3.jpg", "heading": "What is the Process of getting an MBS?"}, "05-03-4": {"chunks": ["What Happens if you have Difﬁculty Swallowing?\nThe MBS study helps your care team ﬁgure out which foods and drinks are safe for you to eat. It also lets the speech pathologist see if you might beneﬁt from swallowing therapy.", "Having this test done before head and neck cancer treatment can help your team plan for your nutrition and decide if a feeding tube may be needed after treatment."], "photo": null, "heading": "What Happens if you have Difﬁculty Swallowing?"}, "05-03-5": {"chunks": ["How Should You Prepare for the Test?\n• You can eat and drink normally before the test; there are no restrictions.\n• Bring any food or drink you ﬁnd hard to swallow so we can observe you ingesting them.\n• Avoid wearing jewelry or clothing with metal.\n• If you use special drinking tools (like a straw or bottle), please bring them."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-03-5.jpg", "heading": "How Should You Prepare for the Test?"}, "05-03-6": {"chunks": ["How Should You Prepare for the Test?\n• Please arrive 30 minutes early to allow time for check-in.", "○ Check in at the Outpatient Building: 1801 Inwood Rd, Dallas, TX 75390, 1st ﬂoor.\n• On MyChart, your MBS appointment will appear as an X-ray visit followed by a speech pathology visit. Both are done during the same visit at the outpatient building.\n• For any questions, please contact us via MyChart or at 214-645-8898."], "photo": "/sms-images/05-03-6.jpg", "heading": "How Should You Prepare for the Test?"}, "06-01-02": {"chunks": ["What is a Tracheostomy Tube?\nA tracheostomy tube is a medical device inserted into a small opening in your neck called a stoma, which leads directly into your windpipe (trachea).", "This tube helps you breathe when you're unable to breathe normally through your nose or mouth. It allows air to ﬂow directly into your lungs and may be used temporarily or long-term depending on your condition."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-01-02.jpg", "heading": "What is a Tracheostomy Tube?"}, "06-01-03": {"chunks": ["Parts of Your Tracheostomy Tube\nOuter Cannula Neck Plate Neck Plate Slot Inner Cannula Hub"], "photo": "/sms-images/06-01-03.jpg", "heading": "Parts of Your Tracheostomy Tube"}, "06-01-04": {"chunks": ["Parts of Your Tracheostomy Tube\nInner cannula Obturator"], "photo": "/sms-images/06-01-04.jpg", "heading": "Parts of Your Tracheostomy Tube Continued"}, "06-01-05": {"chunks": ["Parts of Your Tracheostomy Tube\nThe inner cannula is a hollow tube placed in the tracheostomy opening that remains there during routine use. It's primary purpose is to keep the airway open and allows it to be cleaned at regular intervals without removing the tracheostomy tube.", "The obturator is a solid insert used during tube insertion or exchange. It serves as a guide when inserting the tube and is then removed."], "photo": null, "heading": "Parts of Your Tracheostomy Tube Continued"}, "06-01-06": {"chunks": ["Before Discharge From the Hospital\nPatients or caregivers should demonstrate tracheostomy care (such as suctioning, cleaning, and tube changes) under supervision to conﬁrm understanding and safe technique."], "photo": null, "heading": "Before Discharge From the Hospital"}, "06-01-07": {"chunks": ["Cleaning Your Tracheostomy\nTube"], "photo": null, "heading": "Cleaning Your Tracheostomy"}, "06-01-08": {"chunks": ["Supplies Needed to Clean Your Tracheostomy Tube\nClean washcloth Distilled water or sterile water Cotton tipped swabs Cleaning brush Tap water 2 clean bowls or cups"], "photo": "/media/trach-sup.jpg", "heading": "Supplies Needed to Clean Your Tracheostomy Tube"}, "06-01-09": {"chunks": ["Step 1: Wash your hands with soap and water before you start.\n\nStep 2: Pour distilled water or sterile water into two clean bowls or cups."], "photo": null, "heading": "Cleaning your trach tube"}, "06-01-10": {"chunks": ["Step 3: Unlock the inner cannula of your tracheostomy and take it out, as seen below."], "photo": "/media/trach-s3.jpg", "heading": "Cleaning your trach tube"}, "06-01-11": {"chunks": ["If you have a reusable inner cannula, follow steps 4 and 5:\n\nStep 4: Put the inner cannula into the distilled or sterile water. Use a cotton tip swab or cleaning brush to clean the inner cannula as shown below."], "photo": "/media/trach-s4.jpg", "heading": "Cleaning your trach tube"}, "06-01-12": {"chunks": ["If you have a reusable inner cannula, follow steps 4 and 5:\n\nStep 5: Rinse the inner cannula with fresh tap water. Shake off excess water."], "photo": "/media/trach-s5.jpg", "heading": "Cleaning your trach tube"}, "06-01-13": {"chunks": ["Step 6: Dip cotton tipped swabs in fresh distilled or sterile water and clean around your tracheostomy. Wipe from within the tracheostomy stoma outwards towards the skin, as shown below."], "photo": "/media/trach-s6.jpg", "heading": "Cleaning your trach tube"}, "06-01-15": {"chunks": ["Step 8: Wet a clean washcloth with clean warm water. Wipe the skin around your tracheostomy stoma.\n\nStep 9: Dry your skin with a clean towel.\n\nStep 10: Wash your hands with soap and water."], "photo": "/media/trach-s8.jpg", "heading": "Cleaning your trach tube"}, "06-02-02": {"chunks": ["Suctioning\nYour tracheostomy should be suctioned if you feel mucus in your chest and are unable to cough it out. If you are able to cough up the mucus, you should not need to suction the tracheostomy."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-02.jpg", "heading": "Suctioning"}, "06-02-03": {"chunks": ["Supplies Needed to Suction Your Tracheostomy Tube\nSuction machine with tubing Sterile suction kit (with red rubber catheter) Clean cup with tap water Normal saline bullets"], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-03.jpg", "heading": "Supplies Needed to Suction Your Tracheostomy Tube"}, "06-02-04": {"chunks": ["Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps\nStep 1: Wash your hands. Step 2: Turn on the suction machine. Step 3: Sit or stand in front of a mirror. Step 4: Open the suction kit. Step 5: Put on the gloves that are found inside the kit."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-04.jpg", "heading": "Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps"}, "06-02-05": {"chunks": ["Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps\nStep 6: Remove the suction catheter from the kit as shown in the bottom left image, and connect it to the suction line coming from the machine as shown in the bottom right image. Keep the tip of the suction catheter as clean as possible."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-05.jpg", "heading": "Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps Continued"}, "06-02-06": {"chunks": ["Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps\nStep 7: Gently insert the catheter into your tracheostomy tube stoma about 8 cm (roughly the length of your index ﬁnger) or until you cough. Step 8: Do not apply suction when you are pushing the catheter into your tracheostomy tube stoma."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-06.jpg", "heading": "Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps Continued"}, "06-02-07": {"chunks": ["Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps\nStep 9: While slowly pulling the catheter out, pulse the suction by covering and uncovering the thumbhole of the catheter as shown below."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-07.jpg", "heading": "Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps Continued"}, "06-02-08": {"chunks": ["Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps\nStep 10: Do not suction more than 10 seconds.", "Step 11: You can repeat the suction again if needed, but ﬁrst take a few deep breaths. If suctioning doesn’t help, squeeze a saline bullet into your tracheostomy tube and repeat suction. The saline may cause you to cough, but this is normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-08.jpg", "heading": "Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps Continued"}, "06-02-09": {"chunks": ["Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps\nStep 12: Rinse the catheter by suctioning the water from the cup until the catheter and suction machine are free of mucous. Remove the catheter and throw away the catheter and gloves. Never reuse a catheter. Step 13: Wash your hands with soap and water."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-09.jpg", "heading": "Suctioning Your Tracheostomy Tube Steps Continued"}, "06-02-10": {"chunks": ["Concerning Secretions\nContact your provider if you notice any of the following:\n• Yellow-green thick secretions\n• Pus or foul-smelling discharge\n• Frank blood or signiﬁcant blood-tinged discharge\n• Increased volume of secretions that may also be associated with fever, cough, or trouble breathing"], "photo": null, "heading": "Concerning Secretions"}, "06-02-11": {"chunks": ["Changing the Velcro Neck\nBand"], "photo": null, "heading": "Changing the Velcro Neck"}, "06-02-12": {"chunks": ["Supplies Needed to Change the Velcro Neck Band\nVelcro Neck Band Scissors It is important to keep a ﬁnger on the neck plate at all times to hold the tracheostomy tube in place while changing ties. The velcro band can be changed as needed and does not need to be done at the same time as suctioning the tube."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-12.jpg", "heading": "Supplies Needed to Change the Velcro Neck Band"}, "06-02-13": {"chunks": ["Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps\nStep 1: Wash your hands. Step 2: Remove the tracheostomy tube neck band from the package. Step 3: Separate the long and short end of the neck band."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-13.jpg", "heading": "Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps"}, "06-02-14": {"chunks": ["Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps\nStep 4: Hold the tracheostomy tube secure and remove one side of the old ties. Thread the thin velcro tab (of the longer side of the new band) through the back of the neck plate slit and fold it back to stick it to the band (as shown below)."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-14.jpg", "heading": "Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps"}, "06-02-15": {"chunks": ["Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps\nStep 5: Bring the long end of the cotton band behind your neck. Remove the second side of the old Velcro band. Thread the thin Velcro tab of the shorter side of the new band through the back of the other neck plate slot and fold it back to stick it to the band, as shown below."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-15.jpg", "heading": "Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps Continued"}, "06-02-16": {"chunks": ["Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps\nStep 6: Adjust the neck band to the proper length. You should be able to ﬁt two ﬁngers under the band, between the band and your neck, as shown in the bottom right image. Secure it in place by sticking the wide Velcro tab to the free end of the opposite band. If you have too much extra band, you can trim the long end of the band with scissors.", "Step 7. Discard the old neck band in the trash."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-02-16.jpg", "heading": "Changing the Velcro Neck Band Steps Continued"}, "06-03-02": {"chunks": ["How to Put My Tracheostomy\nTube Back in If It Comes Out"], "photo": null, "heading": "How to Put My Tracheostomy"}, "06-03-03": {"chunks": ["Supplies Needed\nReplacement tracheostomy tube Obturator Inner cannula Velcro neck band or tracheostomy ties Mirror Scissors"], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-03.jpg", "heading": "Supplies Needed"}, "06-03-04": {"chunks": ["How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps\nStep 1: If your tracheostomy tube accidentally comes out, do not panic. The stoma in your neck will not close. Take a few slow deep breaths. Step 2: Locate the spare tracheostomy tube sent home in your tracheostomy care kit.", "Step 3: Open the box and remove the tracheostomy tube and obturator."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-04.jpg", "heading": "How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps"}, "06-03-05": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps\nStep 4: Place the obturator inside the tracheostomy tube, as shown below."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-05.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps"}, "06-03-06": {"chunks": ["How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps\nStep 5: Stand in front of the mirror, holding the tracheostomy tube and obturator with your dominant hand, as shown in the top picture to the right."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-06.jpg", "heading": "How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps Continued"}, "06-03-07": {"chunks": ["How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps\nStep 6: As you breathe in, insert the tracheostomy tube (with obturator in place) into your stoma by pushing in and following the curve of the tube."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-07.jpg", "heading": "How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps Continued"}, "06-03-08": {"chunks": ["How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps\nStep 7: Hold the tube in place with your ﬁngers resting on the neck plate, while quickly removing the obturator. Insert the cannula and secure in place.", "Step 8: Secure the tracheostomy tube in place as previously described in the “Changing The Velcro Neck Band” section of this module."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-08.jpg", "heading": "How to Put Your Tracheostomy Tube Back in Steps Continued"}, "06-03-10": {"chunks": ["Protecting Your Tracheostomy Tube\nDo not let water get into your tracheostomy tube. This means no swimming or water sports.", "When you take a shower, move the shower head, so the water hits you below the tracheostomy tube. When you rinse your head or neck, cup your hands over your tracheostomy tube. You can also wring out a washcloth and put it over your tracheostomy tube."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-10.jpg", "heading": "Protecting Your Tracheostomy Tube"}, "06-03-11": {"chunks": ["Protecting Your Tracheostomy Tube\nProtect the opening of the tracheostomy tube. You can do this with a piece of moist gauze, knitted tracheostomy cover, or folded handkerchief. This is important so things like dust, hair, lint, or insects cannot get into your tracheostomy tube."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-11.jpg", "heading": "Protecting Your Tracheostomy Tube Continued"}, "06-03-12": {"chunks": ["Improving Comfort for Your Tracheostomy Tube\nTake in plenty of liquids either by mouth or feeding tube once the clinical team has deemed it safe. This helps keep your mucus thinner and easier to cough out."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-12.jpg", "heading": "Improving Comfort for Your Tracheostomy Tube"}, "06-03-13": {"chunks": ["Humidifying the Airway\nHumidiﬁcation helps keep your secretions thin, which reduces the risk of developing mucus plugs. Wear your tracheostomy mask (also known as tracheostomy collar) while sleeping or when sitting at home for long periods of time.", "Use a humidiﬁer with distilled water to maintain proper airway moisture."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-13.jpg", "heading": "Humidifying the Airway"}, "06-03-14": {"chunks": ["Humidifying the Airway\nBoth tracheostomy masks and HME caps provide humidiﬁcation of the airway. A tracheostomy mask or tracheostomy collar provides external humidiﬁed air or oxygen through mist.", "An HME (heat and moisture exchanger) cap is a small device attached to the tracheostomy tube that helps retain heat and moisture during breathing. Contact provider if you have any additional questions."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-14.jpg", "heading": "Humidifying the Airway Continued"}, "06-03-15": {"chunks": ["Speaking Valves\nTracheostomy speaking valves are one-way valves, such as the Passy Muir Valve, which opens when you breathe in and closes when you breathe out. This redirects airﬂow to enable speech."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-15.jpg", "heading": "Speaking Valves"}, "06-03-16": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Dental Care\nKeep your mouth clean. Brush your teeth and follow up with dentist regularly for preventative care."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-16.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Dental Care"}, "06-03-17": {"chunks": ["Tracheostomy Tube Insertion Difﬁculty Troubleshooting\n1) Stay calm and check for signs of trouble breathing. 2) Try to reinsert with the same size tube. May use water-based lubricant, but do not force the tube back in. 3) If unsuccessful with the same-size tube, try a tube that is a", "half-size smaller. 4) If a smaller tube fails, insert a endotracheal tube. 5) If a tube is not able to be inserted, call 911 and use a bag-valve mask to ventilate."], "photo": null, "heading": "Tracheostomy Tube Insertion Difﬁculty Troubleshooting"}, "06-03-18": {"chunks": ["Emergency Situations\nIf you have severe problems with breathing and suctioning your tracheostomy tube does not help, call 911 right away. Go to the nearest emergency room."], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-18.jpg", "heading": "Emergency Situations"}, "06-03-19": {"chunks": ["When to Be Concerned\nCome to the hospital right away if:\n• Your tracheostomy tube comes out, and you are unable to put on back in yourself\n• You bleed from your tracheostomy stoma or notice blood in the tube\n• You have trouble breathing"], "photo": "/sms-images/06-03-19.jpg", "heading": "When to Be Concerned"}, "07-01-2": {"chunks": ["What is a gastrostomy (G tube) or nasogastric tube (NG tube)?\nA gastrostomy tube (G tube) and a nasogastric tube (NG tube) are types of feeding tubes that help provide nutrition, ﬂuids, and medications when a person is unable to eat or drink enough by mouth.", "They are essential tools for supporting health and healing in people with certain medical conditions affecting the mouth, throat, esophagus, or digestive system."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-01-2.jpg", "heading": "What is a gastrostomy (G tube) or nasogastric tube (NG tube)?"}, "07-01-3": {"chunks": ["What is a gastrostomy tube (G tube)?\nA G tube is a soft, ﬂexible tube that is placed through the skin of the abdomen directly into the stomach. It provides a way to deliver nutrition, ﬂuids, and medications directly into the stomach when oral feeding is not possible or safe.", "G tubes are typically used for long-term feeding needs (weeks to years). Source: Cancer Research UK / Wikimedia Commons"], "photo": null, "heading": "What is a gastrostomy tube (G tube)?"}, "07-01-4": {"chunks": ["What is a nasogastric tube (NG tube)?\nAn NG tube is a thin, ﬂexible tube that is inserted through the nose, down the throat, and into the stomach. NG tubes are generally used for short-term feeding (days to weeks). They may also be used to remove stomach contents or administer medications.", "Source: Cancer Research UK / Wikimedia Commons"], "photo": null, "heading": "What is a nasogastric tube (NG tube)?"}, "07-01-5": {"chunks": ["Parts of the G tube or NG tube\n1. Feeding tube - soft tube inserted into the stomach (G tube) or through the nose (NG tube) 2. External Flange/Bumper- Round disk on the outside of the body that holds the tube in place 3. Internal Retention Device- Internal balloon or bumper that prevents the tube from slipping out"], "photo": "/sms-images/07-01-5.jpg", "heading": "Parts of the G tube or NG tube"}, "07-01-6": {"chunks": ["Parts of the G tube or NG tube\n4. Extension set- connects to the feeding tube and allows for feeding or ﬂushing 5. Feeding bag/ syringe- holds liquid nutrition or medication to be given through the tube 6. Cap or clamp- closes off the tube when not in use, preventing leakage or air entry", "7. Stoma - the small opening on your body’s surface where a feeding tube (like a G tube or NG tube) enters and connects to your stomach or digestive system."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-01-6.jpg", "heading": "Parts of the G tube or NG tube Continued"}, "07-02-03": {"chunks": ["Day 1–7 Post-Insertion\nClean around the tube and stoma (hole where the tube goes through the belly into the stomach) with mild soap and water using gauze or Q-tips. Dry thoroughly and place a new piece of gauze with a Y cut into the center to allow for the gauze to sit around the tube and under the external ﬂange.", "Do not use lotions, ointments, or creams on the skin. Change the gauze at least every 24 hours."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-03.jpg", "heading": "Day 1–7 Post-Insertion"}, "07-02-04": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer After Day 7 Post-Insertion\nRemove the gauze and do not replace it. Continue daily cleaning with mild soap and water around the stoma and tube."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-04.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer After Day 7 Post-Insertion"}, "07-02-05": {"chunks": ["After Day 7 Post-Insertion\nAfter cleaning the tube and stoma site, rotate or spin the tube in your hand several times, this is to make sure it is free and can freely spin. If it does not rotate or spin, please call the clinic.", "Rotate the tube 360° once daily to prevent sticking. Rotate the ﬂange/bumper feet at least once a day so that the feet do not rest on the same area of skin as this can cause damage."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-05.jpg", "heading": "After Day 7 Post-Insertion Continued"}, "07-02-06": {"chunks": ["After Day 7 Post-Insertion\nIf you still have the stitch on the tube, please call the clinic to schedule an appointment to have this removed."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-06.jpg", "heading": "After Day 7 Post-Insertion Continued"}, "07-02-07": {"chunks": ["Nutritional Supplement Information\nBefore you leave the hospital, you and your family will be taught how to use your feeding tube. It’s important to feel comfortable using it at home. The tube is used to give you liquid nutrition, but you can also use it for other liquids like water or electrolyte drinks.", "It is important to administer the nutritional supplement slowly. Do not to rush the feeding as it can cause an upset stomach."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-07.jpg", "heading": "Nutritional Supplement Information"}, "07-02-09": {"chunks": ["Checking Your G Tube Position\nYour G tube has a number printed on it near the plastic disk that rests against your skin (called the external bumper or ﬂange). This number shows how far the tube is inserted. When you ﬁrst got your tube, the doctor or nurse made a note of this number—it’s your correct tube position.", "Check this number every day. The number should stay the same unless your healthcare provider tells you otherwise."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-09.jpg", "heading": "Checking Your G Tube Position"}, "07-02-10": {"chunks": ["Checking Your G Tube Position\nIf the number is smaller, the tube may have pulled out. Please call the clinic immediately. If the number is larger, this may cause the tube to be too loose. To correct this, gently pull the tube up and slide the external ﬂange down to the correct marking."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-10.jpg", "heading": "Checking Your G Tube Position Continued"}, "07-02-11": {"chunks": ["G Tube Daily Care\nThe external ﬂange/bumper on the G tube should rest snugly on top of the skin, but there should be enough room for daily cleaning underneath. After the G tube is inserted, there may be a small amount of red or yellowish ﬂuid around the new site. This is normal."], "photo": null, "heading": "G Tube Daily Care"}, "07-02-12": {"chunks": ["G-Tube Daily Care\nMild leakage around the tube is normal. The leakage might be the color of the tube feeds, stomach/bilious ﬂuid or pinkish.", "Place a new piece of gauze with a Y cut into the center to allow for the gauze to sit around the tube and under the external ﬂange. Change this as the gauze gets saturated. DO NOT allow saturated gauze to sit on your skin for any period of time."], "photo": null, "heading": "G-Tube Daily Care Continued"}, "07-02-13": {"chunks": ["G-Tube Daily Care\nKeep the tube at a 90° angle from the skin for the ﬁrst 2 weeks. This will help prevent leaking and other complications. Tuck at least 1 inch of gauze around the tube and on top of the bumper, and tape the gauze in place. Do not tape the tube itself down to the skin."], "photo": null, "heading": "G-Tube Daily Care Continued"}, "07-02-14": {"chunks": ["Chemical Burns\nIf the skin turns red or is hot, then please call the clinic and upload a picture of the site on MyChart. This is usually a chemical burn from the drainage around the tube, if this is conﬁrmed, then follow the following steps:", "- Keep the site as dry as possible, even if this means replacing the gauze frequently. - Place a thin layer of diaper rash cream on the skin around the tube site to protect it from drainage."], "photo": null, "heading": "Chemical Burns"}, "07-02-15": {"chunks": ["Showering\nYou may shower 48 hours after tube insertion. You may NOT submerge your tube site (in a bathtub/swimming pool/lake/etc.) until at least 2 weeks after insertion. It is important to cleanse the site with mild soapy water and thoroughly dry the site after it gets wet."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-15.jpg", "heading": "Showering"}, "07-02-16": {"chunks": ["G-Tube Maintenance\nFlush your tube with 30 mL of drinking water: - Before and after feedings - Before and after medications Regular ﬂushing will help to prevent the tube from clogging. If any feeding or medication is left in the tube, this puts your tube at a high risk of clogging.", "Some discoloration of the G-tube over time is normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-16.jpg", "heading": "G-Tube Maintenance"}, "07-02-17": {"chunks": ["Replacing Your G-Tube\nReplace your G-tube approximately every 6 months, or sooner if damaged. Please call the clinic for an appointment to replace your tube. If the tube cracks or the connections come loose sooner, please call the clinic for a replacement sooner."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-17.jpg", "heading": "Replacing Your G-Tube"}, "07-02-18": {"chunks": ["Oral Care\nIt is important to maintain good oral care even if you may not be eating/drinking anything by mouth. This can help prevent complications like infection.", "Please gently brush your teeth, gums, tongue, and palate at least twice a day if you have been told it is safe to do so. You should also rinse with mouthwash if it is deemed safe (Biotene can help if you are experiencing dry mouth)."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-18.jpg", "heading": "Oral Care"}, "07-02-19": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Oral Care\nIf you are at a high risk of aspiration, you may need to use oral swabs and a suction catheter."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-19.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Oral Care"}, "07-02-21": {"chunks": ["If Your Tube is Clogged\nIf you are unable to ﬂush your tube or meet signiﬁcant resistance when trying to ﬂush, your tube is likely clogged. Don’t panic! Here are a few things you can try to unclog your tube:", "- Make sure your tube is not clamped - If using a valve at the end of your tube, make sure it is in the correct position"], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-21.jpg", "heading": "If Your Tube is Clogged"}, "07-02-22": {"chunks": ["If Your Tube is Clogged\n- Carefully massage the tube between your thumb and index ﬁnger to try to break up any clogs. Be careful not to tug or pull hard on your tube to keep it in place. - Hook up an empty syringe and slowly and gently try to draw back any material that is stuck."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-22.jpg", "heading": "If Your Tube is Clogged Continued"}, "07-02-23": {"chunks": ["If Your Tube is Clogged\nTry using warm water to unclog the tube. Use water that is hot but not too hot—it should be warm enough to drink comfortably. Gently push the warm water into the tube and let it sit for 10 to 15 minutes. Then, try ﬂushing the tube again. If you still encounter resistance, repeat the process:", "- Add more warm water - Gently massage the tube - Wait another 10 to 15 minutes Keep trying until the tube ﬂushes easily."], "photo": null, "heading": "If Your Tube is Clogged Continued"}, "07-02-24": {"chunks": ["If Your Tube is Clogged\nIf your tube remains clogged after attempting the 6 steps described previously, you should present to the emergency room to either let medical professionals attempt to unclog it or have the tube exchanged."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-24.jpg", "heading": "If Your Tube is Clogged Continued"}, "07-02-25": {"chunks": ["If Your Tube is Clogged\nDo NOT try to ﬂush your tube with a large amount of force. This can cause the tubing to bubble and even burst or leak. If you notice that your tube is leaking (not from your skin but from the actual tubing itself) you will need to have your tube replaced.", "Do NOT attempt to insert anything into your tube to mechanically push the clog out. This is likely to damage the tube and is not safe."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-25.jpg", "heading": "If Your Tube is Clogged Continued"}, "07-02-26": {"chunks": ["Emergency Situations\nIf your tube comes out within 4 weeks of placement, go to the ER immediately. The hole can close within hours. Call the UTSW MDSC Surgery Clinic at 214-645-2900 and/or the on-call surgeon for instructions. This is an emergency. Do not wait until morning."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-02-26.jpg", "heading": "Emergency Situations"}, "07-03-02": {"chunks": ["The Nasogastric (NG) Tube\n• The NG tube is used to deliver nutrients from the nose to the stomach.\n• If the tube is only needed for shorter periods of time, the NG tube is usually preferred over the G tube. Source: Cancer Research UK / Wikimedia Commons"], "photo": null, "heading": "The Nasogastric (NG) Tube"}, "07-03-03": {"chunks": ["How is the NG tube secured?\n• Often a nasal bridle system is used to secure the NG tube. The system has small magnets to help keep the tube in the nose, which should not be painful.\n• This decreases risk of accidental removal including during feedings. However, if the bridle is pulled this may result in discomfort."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-03-03.jpg", "heading": "How is the NG tube secured?"}, "07-03-04": {"chunks": ["Nutritional Supplement Information\n• Before you leave the hospital, you and your family will be taught how to use your feeding tube. It’s important to feel comfortable using it at home.\n• The tube is used to give you liquid nutrition, but you can also use it for other liquids like water or electrolyte drinks.", "• It is important to administer the nutritional supplement slowly. Do not to rush the feeding as it can cause an upset stomach."], "photo": null, "heading": "Nutritional Supplement Information"}, "07-03-05": {"chunks": ["How Tube Feeds Are Delivered\n• Bolus feeds: Formula is given manually using a syringe several times per day.\n• Pump feeds: Formula is delivered slowly using a feeding pump (e.g., Kangaroo pump), often overnight.\n• Your care team will choose the method based on your nutritional needs and comfort."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-03-05.jpg", "heading": "How Tube Feeds Are Delivered"}, "07-03-06": {"chunks": ["NG Tube Clogs\n• To help keep the tube working properly, try to avoid putting crushed-up medications or thick liquids through the tube, as these can cause clogs.", "• If the tube does get clogged, a small amount of carbonated soda (like ginger ale or cola) may help clear it. If that doesn’t work, you may need to visit your doctor’s ofﬁce or the emergency room."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-03-06.jpg", "heading": "NG Tube Clogs"}, "07-03-07": {"chunks": ["Comfort Tips for Nasogastric (NG) Tube Use\n• Wearing a nasal feeding tube can feel uncomfortable at ﬁrst, and it’s common to become very aware of its presence.\n• The tube may occasionally get tugged or caught on clothing or other objects. While it is unlikely to come out, accidental pulling can cause discomfort."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-03-07.jpg", "heading": "Comfort Tips for Nasogastric (NG) Tube Use"}, "07-03-08": {"chunks": ["Comfort Tips for Nasogastric (NG) Tube Use\n• To help manage this discomfort, some patients ﬁnd it helpful to secure the tube by gently pinning it to the collar of their shirt or by looping it up and around the ear to keep it out of the way.", "• Finding a method that works for you can make daily activities more comfortable and help you feel more at ease while wearing the tube."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-03-08.jpg", "heading": "Comfort Tips for Nasogastric (NG) Tube Use"}, "07-03-09": {"chunks": ["Removing the NG Tube\n• When it’s time to remove the feeding tube, the process is quick and can be done during a scheduled clinic appointment.\n• Removing the NG tube does not require a return to the hospital."], "photo": "/sms-images/07-03-09.jpg", "heading": "Removing the NG Tube"}, "07-03-10": {"chunks": ["Warning signs (to contact your provider)\n• Bright green output\n• Bloody drainage\n• Increased nausea/vomiting\n• Sudden increase in suction output"], "photo": null, "heading": "Warning signs (to contact your provider)"}, "08-01-02": {"chunks": ["Wound Care for Incisions\nIncision: A surgical cut made by the doctor to access the treatment area, which is usually closed with stitches or staples. Dressings should be changed regularly to prevent infection of the wound site.", "If the dressings are more complex, a home health nurse may be able to come to your home to change the dressings. Talk to your doctor about this option."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-02.jpg", "heading": "Wound Care for Incisions"}, "08-01-03": {"chunks": ["How do I avoid irritating the incision area?\n• Wear loose-ﬁtting clothing over the donor site.\n• Avoid rubbing, soaking, or scrubbing the area\n• Avoid bathtubs or pools\n• Don’t expose it to direct sunlight because the skin is extra sensitive"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-03.jpg", "heading": "How do I avoid irritating the incision area?"}, "08-01-04": {"chunks": ["How to prevent infection?\n• Wash your hands before touching the area.\n• Don’t apply creams or ointments unless prescribed.\n• Watch for signs of infection: ○ Increased redness or swelling ○ Yellow or green drainage ○ Foul smell ○ Fever or chills"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-04.jpg", "heading": "How to prevent infection?"}, "08-01-05": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer When is pain concerning?\nPain is worrisome if it is continually increasing over several days. New or worsening pain at the incision or donor site is also worrisome."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-05.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer When is pain concerning?"}, "08-01-06": {"chunks": ["When should I be worried about swelling?\nIf swelling is gradually worsening over several consecutive days, this may be a cause for concern, especially if there is also redness of the neck or face."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-06.jpg", "heading": "When should I be worried about swelling?"}, "08-01-07": {"chunks": ["When should I be worried about drainage?\nNew or foul smelling drainage is worrisome especially if you also have fever over 100.4°F (38°C). Cloudy drainage is is a warning sign as well. Talk to your surgical team."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-07.jpg", "heading": "When should I be worried about drainage?"}, "08-01-08": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer Saltwater Mouthwash\nSaltwater mouth rinses are commonly recommended after many HNC surgeries to promote oral hygiene, reduce infection risk, and support healing."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-08.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer Saltwater Mouthwash"}, "08-01-09": {"chunks": ["When to use a saltwater mouthwash?\n• Oral Cavity Surgeries: ○ Glossectomy (partial or total tongue removal) ○ Mandibulectomy ○ Maxillectomy ○ Oral composite resection ○ Mouth cancer surgery\n• Pharyngectomy\n• Laryngectomy (total or partial)", "These surgeries involve direct incisions in the mouth. Saltwater rinses help keep wounds clean, reduce bacteria, and prevent infection without irritating the surgical site."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-09.jpg", "heading": "When to use a saltwater mouthwash?"}, "08-01-10": {"chunks": ["How to use the mouthwash?\nSupplies needed:\n• 16 ounce bottle of water\n• 1 teaspoon salt\n• 1 teaspoon baking soda 1. Keep a bottle by the bathroom and kitchen sinks 2. Rinse, gargle, and spit after meals, before bed, and anytime your mouth feels dry or sore"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-01-10.jpg", "heading": "How to use the mouthwash?"}, "08-02-02": {"chunks": ["What is a donor site?\nYou have a skin graft donor site where a thin layer of skin was removed (a split-thickness skin graft) to help repair another area. Most commonly, the skin graft is taken from the thigh. The donor site may look like a rug burn or road rash—this is completely normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-02.jpg", "heading": "What is a donor site?"}, "08-02-03": {"chunks": ["Understanding the Donor Site\nOnly the outermost layer of skin is removed. The full thickness of the skin (dermis) is not affected. Because the wound is very superﬁcial, it generally heals well with minimal risk of infection. If an infection does occur, it is usually very mild."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-03.jpg", "heading": "Understanding the Donor Site"}, "08-02-04": {"chunks": ["Initial Dressing – Mepilex Ag\nYour donor site has been covered with a dressing called Mepilex Ag, which contains silver to help prevent infection and promote healing. This dressing may look bloody or crusty over time. This is expected and normal."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-04.jpg", "heading": "Initial Dressing – Mepilex Ag"}, "08-02-05": {"chunks": ["Caring for the Mepilex Ag Dressing\nKeep the Mepilex Ag dressing in place until it falls off on its own. Do not remove it unless instructed to do so by your doctor. If the edges begin to peel up, you may trim them carefully with clean scissors"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-05.jpg", "heading": "Caring for the Mepilex Ag Dressing"}, "08-02-06": {"chunks": ["What to do if the Mepilex comes off early?\nIf the dressing comes off early or is removed, some bleeding may occur. Apply gentle pressure to stop any bleeding. This is not cause for concern."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-06.jpg", "heading": "What to do if the Mepilex comes off early?"}, "08-02-07": {"chunks": ["What to do after the Mepilex comes off?\nOnce the Mepilex Ag dressing has naturally fallen off, begin daily dressing care as follows:", "1. There is no speciﬁc need to clean the donor area. It is okay to shower and to let water run over the site. Do not scrub or soak the area. Pat it dry with a clean towel. Do not submerge it in a tub or pool. 2. Apply a thin layer of Vaseline or Aquaphor to the wound."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-07.jpg", "heading": "What to do after the Mepilex comes off?"}, "08-02-08": {"chunks": ["What to do after the Mepilex comes off (continued)?\n3. Cover the area with a non-adherent dressing, such as Vaseline gauze, Adaptid, Xeroform, or Telfa.", "4. Place a layer of regular gauze over the non-adherent dressing and tape it down. Do not place regular gauze directly on the wound, as it may stick and cause bleeding when removed. 5. Change the dressing once per day, or more often if it becomes wet or dirty or develops an odor.", "6. If the dressing starts to stick to the wound, add more Vaseline or Aquaphor to keep the area moist."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-08.jpg", "heading": "What to do after the Mepilex comes off (continued)?"}, "08-02-09": {"chunks": ["What to expect during healing?\nSome bleeding is normal, especially during dressing changes, because the healing tissue is very delicate. If bleeding occurs:\n• Apply ﬁrm pressure with the dressing material for several minutes.\n• You may also use gauze ﬂuffs and secure them with gentle pressure using tape."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-09.jpg", "heading": "What to expect during healing?"}, "08-02-10": {"chunks": ["How do I know the wound is healed?\nThe wound is considered healed when a new layer of skin has formed and there is no longer any crusting or scabbing.", "The new skin is usually pink or purple in color and may look different or shinier from the surrounding skin at ﬁrst. This is normal, and the color will even out over time. Most donor sites heal within 2-3 weeks"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-10.jpg", "heading": "How do I know the wound is healed?"}, "08-02-11": {"chunks": ["How do I care for the new skin after healing is complete?\nOnce healed, the new skin is more fragile and can bleed if scratched or rubbed. While a dressing is no longer needed, you may keep it covered with an ACE bandage or gauze if it is sensitive.", "You may apply a non-adherent dressing with gentle pressure if any minor bleeding occurs."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-11.jpg", "heading": "How do I care for the new skin after healing is complete?"}, "08-02-12": {"chunks": ["Long-term Skin Care\nAfter new skin has formed, you may begin applying vitamin E lotion or other over-the-counter scar cream to help with the appearance of the scar. The new skin should be protected from direct sunlight to reduce the chance of discoloration. This is especially true during the ﬁrst year.", "You may use sunscreen (SPF 30 or higher) on the area once the skin has completely healed. Do not submerge the area in bathtubs, swimming pools, lakes, or hot tubs until the new skin has fully formed."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-02-12.jpg", "heading": "Long-term Skin Care"}, "08-03-2": {"chunks": ["What is a drain tube?\nA drainage tube is left in the surgical wound at the time of surgery. This tube is about ¼\" thick and is necessary to drain ﬂuid that accumulates because of surgery. Usually, the drain stays in place for a couple of weeks and patients go home with this drain. drain tube"], "photo": null, "heading": "What is a drain tube?"}, "08-03-3": {"chunks": ["How do I take care of my drain?\nThe drain needs to be emptied twice daily (or more depending on the amount of drainage) and the output needs to be recorded. The surgeon will want to know the daily output of the drain in milliliters (ML) for each 24-hour period.", "When the drain output is less than 30-50 ML per 24-hour period, it may be ready to be removed."], "photo": null, "heading": "How do I take care of my drain?"}, "08-03-4": {"chunks": ["Will ﬂuid accumulate after the drain tube is removed?\nOccasionally, ﬂuid continues to accumulate even after the drain is removed. This can lead to swelling of the skin that feels soft, like a water balloon. When you stand up the swelling will drift down to the lowest portion of the surgical site."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-03-4.jpg", "heading": "Will ﬂuid accumulate after the drain tube is removed?"}, "08-03-5": {"chunks": ["What should I do if ﬂuid accumulates after the drain is removed?\nThis ﬂuid (called a seroma) usually needs to be drained but the procedure is easy and usually done in the ofﬁce.", "The skin overlying the area is usually numb from the surgery and the ﬂuid can be drained through a small needle inserted through the skin. Sometimes this can require several drainage procedures if the ﬂuid keeps reaccumulating."], "photo": null, "heading": "What should I do if ﬂuid accumulates after the drain is removed?"}, "08-03-6": {"chunks": ["How long can I wait to get the ﬂuid drained?\nThis is not urgent – you do not need to come in the same day for this problem. Waiting even a week or two will be okay"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-03-6.jpg", "heading": "How long can I wait to get the ﬂuid drained?"}, "08-04-2": {"chunks": ["What is a wound VAC ?\nA Wound VAC (Vacuum-Assisted Closure) is a medical device that supports wound healing through negative pressure therapy. This increases blood ﬂow in the wound and helps with healing."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-04-2.jpg", "heading": "What is a wound VAC ?"}, "08-04-3": {"chunks": ["How does it work?\nIt involves placing a foam dressing within the wound bed, sealing the area with an adhesive drape, and connecting it to a vacuum pump via tubing. The system gently removes ﬂuid and debris, reduces swelling, and helps draw the edges of the wound together."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-04-3.jpg", "heading": "How does it work?"}, "08-04-4": {"chunks": ["Why use a wound VAC?\nWound VACs are commonly used after major head and neck cancer surgeries, especially when there are large or complex surgical wounds, tissue ﬂaps, or areas at high risk for ﬂuid collection.", "They help the area heal by removing ﬂuid, improving blood ﬂow, preventing infection, and protecting tissue ﬂaps."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-04-4.jpg", "heading": "Why use a wound VAC?"}, "08-04-5": {"chunks": ["What to expect?\nThe VAC device will remain in place continuously, typically connected to portable suction. The dressing is sealed tightly to the skin, and you may feel a light pulling or suction sensation. Mild discomfort can occur but should not be severe.", "The dressing is usually changed every 2–3 days by trained providers, either in the hospital or clinic setting."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-04-5.jpg", "heading": "What to expect?"}, "08-04-6": {"chunks": ["Daily management of the Wound VAC\nAvoid disconnecting or turning off the Wound VAC unless instructed. Keep the tubing free of kinks and below the wound site to allow proper drainage."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-04-6.jpg", "heading": "Daily management of the Wound VAC"}, "08-04-7": {"chunks": ["When to Call Your Care Team\nMake sure the seal is intact—if you hear air leaks or see dressing lifting, notify your care team. If the canister becomes full or alarms sound, contact your provider or home health nurse."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-04-7.jpg", "heading": "When to Call Your Care Team"}, "08-04-8": {"chunks": ["Hygiene and activity precautions\n• Do not shower while the VAC is in place unless your provider has approved a special cover or dressing.\n• Avoid submerging the VAC or dressing in water.\n• Be cautious with neck movement and avoid pulling on the tubing.\n• Limit strenuous activities and avoid laying directly on the VAC dressing site to prevent pressure disruption."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-04-8.jpg", "heading": "Hygiene and activity precautions"}, "08-05-2": {"chunks": ["I Can Manage Cancer What is cancer-related Lymphedema\n• Lymphedema refers to swelling caused by the buildup of lymph ﬂuid beneath the skin. The lymphatic system normally functions to collect and transport excess ﬂuid, proteins, and waste products from body tissues back into the bloodstream. It also plays a key role in ﬁltering out bacteria, viruses, and other harmful substances.\n• When lymph nodes or vessels are damaged or obstructed, lymph ﬂuid cannot drain effectively. This results in ﬂuid accumulation and swelling."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-05-2.jpg", "heading": "I Can Manage Cancer What is cancer-related Lymphedema"}, "08-05-3": {"chunks": ["What are the causes?\nCancer related lymphedema occurs when the lymphatic system is blocked or damaged from the effects of cancer cancer treatments. Common causes:\n• Surgery to remove lymph nodes\n• Radiation treatment for cancer\n• Tumor growth from cancer"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-05-3.jpg", "heading": "What are the causes?"}, "08-05-4": {"chunks": ["What increases the risk?\nThe following factors may make you more likely to develop this complication:\n• Older age\n• Obesity\n• Rheumatoid or inﬂammatory arthritis\n• History of lymphedema"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-05-4.jpg", "heading": "What increases the risk?"}, "08-05-5": {"chunks": ["What are the physical exam signs of lymphedema?\n• Sensation of heaviness, tightness, or fullness in the affected area\n• Mild swelling that may come and go, especially at the end of the day\n• Clothing, jewelry, or compression garments feeling tighter than usual\n• Skin changes, such as slight pufﬁness, decreased elasticity, or indentation when pressed\n• Aching, tingling, or discomfort in the limb or region\n• Reduced range of motion in the neck, shoulder, or affected limb"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-05-5.jpg", "heading": "What are the physical exam signs of lymphedema?"}, "08-05-6": {"chunks": ["How to treat lymphedema?\nCompression Garments\n• Sleeves or stockings to maintain pressure\n• Elevated the limb above the level of your heart if possible Compression Bandaging\n• Multi-layer wraps to reduce swelling Pneumatic Compression Pump", "• External pump with inﬂatable sleeve to promote drainage Surgery (for severe cases)\n• Reserved for chronic, refractory lymphedema Complete Decongestive Therapy (CDT)\n• Manual lymphatic drainage (specialized massage)\n• Compression therapy\n• Gentle exercise\n• Skin care"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-05-6.jpg", "heading": "How to treat lymphedema?"}, "08-05-7": {"chunks": ["Instructions at home\nDO DON’T", "• Check your skin and the affected area everyday for signs of increased swelling, irritation or redness\n• Keep the affected area clean and dry\n• Apply lotion to prevent dry skin\n• Protect your skin from cuts and injuries\n• Protect the affected area from extreme cold\n• Avoid medical procedures such as blood draws and injections in the affected limb", "• Walk barefoot\n• Have your blood pressure checked on the affected limb\n• Apply ice to the affected area\n• Use a heating pad over the affected area\n• Wear right clothes, shoes, or jewelry\n• Sit with your legs crossed"], "photo": null, "heading": "Instructions at home"}, "08-05-8": {"chunks": ["Contact a healthcare provider IF….\n• You have a fever\n• You have a cut or injury that does not heal\n• You have redness or pain in the affected area\n• You have new or increased swelling\n• You develop purplish spots or sores on your affected limb"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-05-8.jpg", "heading": "Contact a healthcare provider IF…."}, "08-05-9": {"chunks": ["Seek immediate medical attention IF…..\n• You develop a skin rash\n• You have chills or sweating\n• You have trouble breathing"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-05-9.jpg", "heading": "Seek immediate medical attention IF….."}, "08-06-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nDysphagia Strategies and Spinal Accessory Nerve Exercises Exercises Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "08-06-03": {"chunks": ["Dysphagia Strategies and\nExercises"], "photo": null, "heading": "Dysphagia Strategies and"}, "08-06-04": {"chunks": ["What is Dysphagia?\n• Dysphagia, or difﬁculty swallowing, is a common postoperative symptom.\n• Postoperative dysphagia can result from swelling, nerve irritation, muscle weakness, or structural changes that disrupt the normal swallowing mechanism."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-04.jpg", "heading": "What is Dysphagia?"}, "08-06-05": {"chunks": ["Speech Therapy\n• Speech therapy plays an integral role in your ability to swallow and is essential to optimize your health while undergoing treatment\n• A wide range of conditions, illnesses, and their treatments can disrupt the process of swallowing which can lead to choking, malnutrition, and other problems\n• UTSW Speech Therapy department will provide you more strategies to successfully navigate your nutritional status before and after surgery."], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-05.jpg", "heading": "Speech Therapy"}, "08-06-06": {"chunks": ["Exercise #1: Jaw Resistance\n1. Slightly open your mouth and press chin against the palm 2. Let your elbow be on your knee 3. Feel the engagement in the center neck and below the chin 4. Sustain the position for 10 seconds 5. Keep breathing (5 repetitions, 3-5 times a day)"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-06.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #1: Jaw Resistance"}, "08-06-07": {"chunks": ["Exercise #2: Tongue Hold (Masako)\n1. Hold your tongue between your teeth 2. Swallow while keeping your tongue between your teeth This exercise strengthens the muscles in the back of your throat. (5 repetitions, 3-5 times a day)"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-07.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #2: Tongue Hold (Masako)"}, "08-06-08": {"chunks": ["Exercise #3: Effortful Swallow\n1. Swallow with extra force 2. Feel your tongue against the roof of your mouth as you swallow (5 repetitions, 3-5 times a day)"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-08.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #3: Effortful Swallow"}, "08-06-09": {"chunks": ["Exercise #4: Tongue Pull-back\n1. Say the “k-k-k-k-k” harshly 2. Feel the engagement of the back of the tongue (5 repetitions, 3-5 times a day)"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-09.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #4: Tongue Pull-back"}, "08-06-10": {"chunks": ["Exercise #5: Pharyngeal Constriction\n1. Say a high pitched “ee” sound for 5 seconds 2. Watch yourself in the mirror and make sure your neck muscles are engaged (5 repetitions, 3-5 times a day)"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-10.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #5: Pharyngeal Constriction"}, "08-06-11": {"chunks": ["Exercise #6: Mendelsohn Maneuver\n1. Feel the top of your larynx (Adam’s apple) moving upward during a swallow 2. At the highest point of your swallow (when you hear the clicking sound), try to hold the larynx up. 3. Hold your breath for 5 seconds.", "This exercise helps lift your larynx and opens the top of your food pipe (esophagus). This can help clear out any leftover food stuck in your throat. (5 repetitions, 3-5 times a day)"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-11.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #6: Mendelsohn Maneuver"}, "08-06-12": {"chunks": ["When are these exercises useful?\nHelpful for:\n• Laryngectomy (partial or total)\n• Oropharynx surgery\n• Radiation to throat or neck Beneﬁts:\n• Keeps swallowing muscles strong\n• Speeds up recovery after treatment\n• Lowers risk of feeding tube or aspiration\n• Helps you keep eating by mouth longer"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-12.jpg", "heading": "When are these exercises useful?"}, "08-06-13": {"chunks": ["Spinal Accessory Nerve\nExercises"], "photo": null, "heading": "Spinal Accessory Nerve"}, "08-06-14": {"chunks": ["What is the spinal accessory nerve?\nThe spinal accessory nerve is located in the back of your upper shoulder. When the nerve is injured, it can cause pain and can greatly reduce the range of motion in that shoulder"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-14.jpg", "heading": "What is the spinal accessory nerve?"}, "08-06-15": {"chunks": ["Exercise #1: Shoulder Shrugs\n1. Shoulder shrugs can strengthen the injured area. 2. Stand with your legs hip-distance apart. 3. Shrug your shoulders up and lower them down as far as you comfortably can 4. Do a total of 10 repetitions 5. As you progress hold weights in your hands as you do this exercise"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-15.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #1: Shoulder Shrugs"}, "08-06-16": {"chunks": ["Exercise #2: Supine Shoulder Rotation\n1. Use a bed or mat for another strengthening exercise 2. Lie on your back and bend your elbows 3. Your upper arm should be at your side horizontal to the ground and your lower arms in the air perpendicular to the ground 4. Slowly bring your hands down to the mat or bed and back up in the air 5. Do a total of 10 repetitions"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-16.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #2: Supine Shoulder Rotation"}, "08-06-17": {"chunks": ["Exercise #3: Shoulder Elevation with Wand\n1. The American Physical Therapy Association suggests this exercise that involves using a cane, broomstick or dowel 2. Lie on your back holding the cane, broomstick or dowel with your hands resting on your upper legs 3. Your hands should be about 18 inches apart, and your palms should be facing up 4. Slowly lift your arms as far over your head as is comfortable, leading with the uninjured arm 5. Hold for 5 seconds and return to your original position do a total of ﬁve repetitions"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-17.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #3: Shoulder Elevation with Wand"}, "08-06-18": {"chunks": ["Exercise #4: Wall Slide\n1. The APTA also recommends a standing exercise to rehabilitate a spinal accessory nerve injury 2. Stand close to a wall, facing sideways 3. Slide your injured arm up the wall using your ﬁngers to walk up the wall, palms facing down 4. Keep sliding your hand until you feel a stretch and hold the position for 5 seconds 5. Slowly drop your arm and do a total of ﬁve repetitions 6. Do not make the mistake of turning your body towards the wall"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-18.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #4: Wall Slide"}, "08-06-19": {"chunks": ["Exercise #5: Wall Press\n1. Stand facing a wall for this strengthening exercise 2. You should be two to three feet away from the wall 3. Put your hands on the wall at shoulder level and press against it 4. As you do this, squeeze your shoulder blades together and then separate them 5. Return to your starting position 6. Do a total of 10 repetitions"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-19.jpg", "heading": "Exercise #5: Wall Press"}, "08-06-20": {"chunks": ["When are these exercises useful?\nHelpful for: Neck dissection surgery (especially levels II–V) Beneﬁts:\n• Protects shoulder and neck strength\n• Reduces risk of shoulder pain and stiffness\n• Improves posture and range of motion\n• Easier return to normal activity after surgery"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-06-20.jpg", "heading": "When are these exercises useful?"}, "08-07-2": {"chunks": ["Diet priorities after surgery\n• High-calorie, high-protein foods to support healing and strength\n• Small, frequent meals if eating by mouth is difﬁcult\n• Feeding tube nutrition is often needed temporarily and helps keep you well-nourished\n• Sip ﬂuids often to prevent dehydration and help with swallowing"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-07-2.jpg", "heading": "Diet priorities after surgery"}, "08-07-3": {"chunks": ["Eggs and Egg Dishes\n• High protein gelatin\n• Hard boiled eggs\n• Custard\n• Scrambled eggs\n• Deviled eggs\n• Omelet\n• French toast\n• Quiche\n• Poached eggs\n• Egg salad\n• Soufﬂé"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-07-3.jpg", "heading": "Eggs and Egg Dishes"}, "08-07-4": {"chunks": ["Meat Salads and Soft Meats\n• Chicken salad\n• Seafood salad\n• Ham salad\n• Tuna salad\n• Roast beef salad\n• Turkey salad\n• Beef stew\n• Meatloaf\n• Ground hamburger with gravy/cream sauce\n• Minced casserole\n• Soufﬂé\n• Flaked ﬁsh with dill sauce\n• Chicken pot pie"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-07-4.jpg", "heading": "Meat Salads and Soft Meats"}, "08-07-5": {"chunks": ["Soft Cheese Foods\n• Cheese cake\n• Macaroni and cheese\n• Cheese cubes\n• Cottage cheese\n• Cheese topping on baked or mashed potato\n• Twice baked potato\n• Fondue\n• Cheese sauce on vegetables"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-07-5.jpg", "heading": "Soft Cheese Foods"}, "08-07-6": {"chunks": ["Dairy Foods and Milk\n• Double strength milk\n• Eggnog\n• Ice cream\n• Mousse\n• Pudding pops\n• Powdered milk mixed into casseroles\n• Soy milk\n• Malts\n• Frozen yogurt\n• Smoothies\n• Hot cocoa made with milk\n• Milkshakes\n• Pudding\n• Yogurt"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-07-6.jpg", "heading": "Dairy Foods and Milk"}, "08-07-7": {"chunks": ["Soups\n• Bean soup\n• Cream of celery\n• Cream of tomato\n• Split pea soup\n• Cream of asparagus\n• Cream of chicken\n• New england clam chowder\n• Cream of broccoli\n• Cream of mushroom\n• Potato soup"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-07-7.jpg", "heading": "Soups"}, "08-07-8": {"chunks": ["Grains, Nuts, and Legumes\n• Baked beans\n• Hummus\n• Peanut butter\n• Almond butter\n• Pork and beans\n• Tofu, soft or silken\n• Oatmeal\n• Cream of wheat"], "photo": "/sms-images/08-07-8.jpg", "heading": "Grains, Nuts, and Legumes"}, "09-01-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nGeneral Reasons for Postoperative Postoperative Radiation and Chemotherapy Timing of Postoperative Radiation Radiation Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "09-01-04": {"chunks": ["Why do I need radiation after surgery?\nYou may need radiation after surgery based on certain risk features found in your tumor. Radiation is used to reduce the risk of cancer returning after surgery.", "When cancer comes back after treatment, it's called a recurrence. Radiation helps lower the chances of this happening."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-04.jpg", "heading": "Why do I need radiation after surgery?"}, "09-01-05": {"chunks": ["Common Reasons for Postoperative Radiation\n• Advanced stage primary tumor: If your tumor is classiﬁed as T3 or T4 (larger or more invasive tumors), radiation is typically recommended.\n• Perineural invasion: If cancer is found spreading along small nerves, this increases the risk of recurrence.\n• Lymphovascular invasion: Cancer cells found in small blood vessels or lymphatic vessels suggest a higher likelihood of spread."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-05.jpg", "heading": "Common Reasons for Postoperative Radiation"}, "09-01-06": {"chunks": ["Common Reasons for Postoperative Radiation (cont.)\n• Close margins: If the tumor is close to the edge of the surgical specimen, there may be microscopic cancer left behind.\n• Lymph node involvement:", "○ Multiple lymph nodes with cancer (pathologic N2 or N3 disease) ○ Large lymph nodes involved ○ Involvement of low or posterior neck lymph nodes (levels 4 or 5)"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-06.jpg", "heading": "Common Reasons for Postoperative Radiation (cont.)"}, "09-01-07": {"chunks": ["Postoperative Radiation and\nChemotherapy"], "photo": null, "heading": "Postoperative Radiation and"}, "09-01-08": {"chunks": ["What is chemotherapy?\nChemotherapy is medicine that travels through your whole body to kill cancer cells. It’s usually given by IV or as a pill, and it works by targeting fast-growing cells like cancer."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-08.jpg", "heading": "What is chemotherapy?"}, "09-01-09": {"chunks": ["Why do I need both radiation and chemotherapy after surgery?\nIn some cases, chemotherapy is added to radiation therapy to improve treatment outcomes. This decision is based on certain high-risk features found during surgery.", "This combination of treatments may increase side effects but can improve the chances of cure in high-risk patients."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-09.jpg", "heading": "Why do I need both radiation and chemotherapy after surgery?"}, "09-01-10": {"chunks": ["Common Reasons for Postoperative Radiation with Chemotherapy\n• Positive surgical margins: If cancer cells are seen right at the edge of the removed tissue, this indicates that not all cancer may have been removed.\n• Extracapsular spread (ECS): If cancer has spread outside a lymph node into surrounding tissue, chemotherapy is typically recommended in addition to radiation."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-10.jpg", "heading": "Common Reasons for Postoperative Radiation with Chemotherapy"}, "09-01-11": {"chunks": ["Timing of Postoperative\nRadiation"], "photo": null, "heading": "Timing of Postoperative"}, "09-01-12": {"chunks": ["When do I start receiving postoperative radiation therapy?\nRadiation is most effective when started soon after surgery, once you have healed but before too much time passes."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-12.jpg", "heading": "When do I start receiving postoperative radiation therapy?"}, "09-01-13": {"chunks": ["What is a typical timeline?\n• Start radiation within 6 weeks of surgery, ideally.\n• This allows enough time for surgical healing while ensuring radiation is still effective.\n• Delays beyond 8 weeks may reduce treatment success, so it's important to keep appointments and complete pre-radiation evaluations on time."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-01-13.jpg", "heading": "What is a typical timeline?"}, "09-02-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nVisiting the Dentist Maintaining Dental Health Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "09-02-04": {"chunks": ["Why do I need to see the dentist?\nRadiation to the head and neck can damage your salivary glands and cause xerostomia (dry mouth) which increases the risk of:\n• Cavities and dental infections\n• Osteoradionecrosis (jawbone damage)\n• Other long-term dental complications"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-04.jpg", "heading": "Why do I need to see the dentist?"}, "09-02-05": {"chunks": ["What do I need to do?\n• Schedule a pre-radiation dental evaluation at least a few weeks before starting radiation.\n• Your dentist will check for and treat any dental issues beforehand.\n• You may need extractions, ﬁllings, or cleanings to reduce the risk of problems during or after treatment."], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-05.jpg", "heading": "What do I need to do?"}, "09-02-06": {"chunks": ["Goals of Pre-Radiation Oral/Dental Evaluation:\n• Patient education (both oral and written) about dental risks and preventive care\n• Complete head and neck dental exam, including x-rays\n• Identify and treat sources of infection before radiation"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-06.jpg", "heading": "Goals of Pre-Radiation Oral/Dental Evaluation:"}, "09-02-08": {"chunks": ["Dry Mouth Management\n• Increase hydration\n• Avoid caffeine and alcohol\n• Use salivary stimulants (e.g., xylitol gum/lozenges, sorbitol, pilocarpine, cevimeline)\n• Use alcohol-free mouthwash (e.g., 0.1% chlorine dioxide rinse)\n• Use salivary substitutes (e.g., gels with lysozyme, lactoferrin, calcium phosphate)"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-08.jpg", "heading": "Dry Mouth Management"}, "09-02-09": {"chunks": ["Dental Caries Prevention\n• Brush and ﬂoss daily\n• Use ﬂuoride treatments: ○ 1.1% NaF gel or dentifrice daily ○ Fluoride varnish 3x/year ○ Calcium phosphate rinses/gels\n• Frequent dental checkups"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-09.jpg", "heading": "Dental Caries Prevention"}, "09-02-10": {"chunks": ["Fungal Infection Prevention\n• Antifungal lozenges or rinses\n• Systemic antifungals if needed (consult infectious disease specialist)"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-10.jpg", "heading": "Fungal Infection Prevention"}, "09-02-11": {"chunks": ["Extractions and Jaw Tightness\n• Determine if tooth extractions are needed before RT (at least 2 weeks prior)\n• Coordinate any future extractions with your oncology team\n• Monitor and manage trismus (jaw tightness)"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-11.jpg", "heading": "Extractions and Jaw Tightness"}, "09-02-12": {"chunks": ["Post-Treatment Dental Management\n• Continue managing dry mouth and trismus\n• Maintain strict oral hygiene and caries prevention\n• Prevent/manage osteoradionecrosis\n• Avoid implants or extractions without radiation oncologist consultation\n• Dental visits every 6 months or more frequently if xerostomia or new dental issues arise"], "photo": "/sms-images/09-02-12.jpg", "heading": "Post-Treatment Dental Management"}, "10-01-02": {"chunks": ["Sections in this Module\nHealth Promotion and Wellness Surveillance Physical and Psychosocial Effects of Treatment Screening for Secondary Cancers Click on the box to jump to each section"], "photo": null, "heading": "Sections in this Module"}, "10-01-03": {"chunks": ["Health Promotion and\nWellness"], "photo": null, "heading": "Health Promotion and"}, "10-01-04": {"chunks": ["Recommendations for survivors post-treatment\n• Maintain a healthy weight and eat healthy, focusing on eating vegetables, fruits, and whole grains\n• Adopt a physically active lifestyle, including exercise and strength training as tolerated\n• We strongly recommend quitting tobacco and limiting alcohol intake\n• Visit the dentist at least once a year\n• Use at least SPF 30 sunscreen daily\n• Notify your doctor of any family history of cancer"], "photo": "/sms-images/10-01-04.jpg", "heading": "Recommendations for survivors post-treatment"}, "10-01-05": {"chunks": ["What action steps can be taken towards wellness?\n• If food intake is an issue or if there are feeding tube issues, notify your doctor\n• Always consult your doctor before starting an exercise program\n• Tobacco cessation counseling is available if you need assistance\n• If you notice any oral cavity changes, notify your doctor"], "photo": "/sms-images/10-01-05.jpg", "heading": "What action steps can be taken towards wellness?"}, "10-01-07": {"chunks": ["Patients should continue to be mindful of\n• Follow-up visits:", "○ Year 1: every 1-3 months ○ Year 2: every 2-6 months ○ Years 3-5: every 4-8 months ○ Years 6+: every 12 months\n• Imaging: a CT scan, MRI, or PET may be ordered 3 months post-treatment\n• Thyroid function: Evaluate function every 6-12 months for 6 years post-treatment\n• Vaccinations: Stay up to date with your inﬂuenza, shingles, and pneumococcal vaccines"], "photo": "/sms-images/10-01-07.jpg", "heading": "Patients should continue to be mindful of"}, "10-01-08": {"chunks": ["Screening for Secondary\nCancers"], "photo": null, "heading": "Screening for Secondary"}, "10-01-09": {"chunks": ["How are second primary cancers detected?\n• Head and neck cancer survivors are at increased risk for new head and neck, lung, and esophageal cancers\n• The risk of developing these can vary depending on treatment variations, age, and genetics\n• Your doctor may decide to screen for lung cancer with CT scans\n• All other cancers can be screened for as they normally are"], "photo": "/sms-images/10-01-09.jpg", "heading": "How are second primary cancers detected?"}, "10-01-10": {"chunks": ["Physical and Psychosocial\nEffects of Treatment"], "photo": null, "heading": "Physical and Psychosocial"}, "10-01-11": {"chunks": ["Types of patients and the effects they can experience\nAny head and neck Survivors who undergo Survivors who undergo Survivors who undergo cancer survivor surgery radiation therapy chemotherapy", "• Sleep apnea\n• Body and self-image concerns\n• Difficulty swallowing and speaking\n• Lymphedema\n• Intimacy issues\n• Depression and anxiety\n• Weakness\n• Decreased neck, arm, shoulder mobility or discomfort\n• Pain\n• Dry mouth\n• Cervical dystonia\n• Altered or loss of taste\n• Radiation dermatitis\n• Dental issues", "• Neuropathy (numbness/tingling in feet, legs, hands)\n• Hearing loss\n• Kidney issues"], "photo": null, "heading": "Types of patients and the effects they can experience"}, "06-01-14": {"chunks": ["Step 7: Put the inner cannula back in and lock it as shown below."], "photo": "/media/trach-s7.jpg", "heading": "Cleaning your trach tube"}}
